euf:10591 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The modified form of dentin derived from animal teeth. The most common example is from the tusks of mature elephants; similar material is obtained from any tusked or large-toothed mammal such as a walrus or narwhal (AAT). For imitation ivory use artifical ivory ."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:europeana-fashion ;
    skos:prefLabel "Elfenbein"@de,
        "ελεφαντόδοντο"@el,
        "ivory (material)"@en,
        "marfil"@es,
        "ivoire"@fr,
        "שנהב"@he,
        "avorio"@it,
        "ivoor"@nl,
        "slonovača"@sr,
        "elfenben"@sv .

euf:10592 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Knochen"@de,
        "bones (material)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The rigid, calcareous material that is white in color and forms the skeleton of vertebrates; primarily composed of calcium hydroxyapatite with smaller amounts of calcium carbonate, calcium fluoride, magnesium phosphate, and ossein, a high molecular weight protein. Bones have a concentric structure with central lymphatic canals surrounded by a spongy lamellar region protected by a dense outer cortex. Bone has been carved and used since ancient times for many purposes, including fish-hooks, spear heads, needles, handles, and art objects. Bones were also burnt to produce bone black and boiled to produce bone glue. Bone can be distinguished from ivory by being generally whiter, more porous, and less dense (AAT)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:europeana-fashion ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bein"@de,
        "κόκαλο"@el,
        "bone (material)"@en,
        "hueso"@es,
        "os"@fr,
        "עצם"@he,
        "osso"@it,
        "been (materiaal)"@nl,
        "osso"@pt,
        "kost"@sr,
        "ben"@sv .

euf:10593 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "parchments (animal material)"@en ;
    skos:definition "Translucent or opaque material made from calf, sheep, or goat skin which has been limed, dehaired, scraped, and dried under tension to produce a thin, strong material for writing, bookbinding, or other uses. For finer quality calf parchment, use vellum. The terms parchment and vellum sometimes have been and still are both confused and used interchangeably (AAT)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:europeana-fashion ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pergament"@de,
        "περγαμηνή"@el,
        "parchment (animal material)"@en,
        "pergamino"@es,
        "parchemin"@fr,
        "קלף"@he,
        "pergamena"@it,
        "perkament"@nl,
        "pergaminho"@pt,
