    skos:prefLabel "Swedenborgian"@en .

aat:300448122 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Originally, make-up or costume intended to imitate the appearance of a black person, particularly as part of a performance. By extension, any practice of stereotyping dark-skinned people in an exaggerated fashion. Though not exclusive to the United States, blackface was associated with minstrel shows in the US from the 1830s until the mid-20th century; and is widely regarded now as an offensive example of racism. Blackface was adopted by some African American performers in minstrel shows from ca.1840 as a means to earn a living in venues that were otherwise denied them in mainstream show business."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "blackface"@en .

aat:300448123 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand held fans commonly used in churches."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "church fans"@en .

aat:300448124 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Houses owned by a church, especially a building next to a church in which social events and meetings connected with the church are held."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "church houses"@en .

aat:300448125 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings serving as meeting places for certain groups or factions associated with a church."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "church lodges"@en .

aat:300448588 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Side altars in Catholic Churches or chapels that venerate the Virgin Mary, often patronized by the wealthy of a particular community."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lady altars"@en .

aat:300448589 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Keys or ciphers referencing one language to another."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "clavises"@en .

aat:300448590 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In universities and colleges, large buildings set apart for the instruction or residence of students."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "university halls"@en .

aat:300448591 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ancient and medieval culture and style of people in what is today Armenia and eastern Georgia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Iberian"@en .

aat:300448592 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Churches associated with a priory, which is a religious house ranking immediately below abbeys and presided over by a prior or prioress."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "priory churches"@en .

aat:300448593 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Building complexes focused on a church."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ecclesiastical complexes"@en .

aat:300448594 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Separate buildings or large rooms in a church building where various activities are facilitated, such as meetings, recreation, meals, workshops, sports, and community events. The term refers to the people giving \"fellowship,\" which is sharing and communing with their fellows."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fellowship halls"@en .

aat:300448595 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A chitosan derivative with an amphoteric character that has the potential to improve paper strength and form films with a good barrier to gases and antibacterial activity. In conservation, it is used as a paper consolidant."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "carboxymethyl chitosan"@en .

aat:300448596 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Motion in a medium in which, when particles are displaced, a force proportional to the displacement acts on the particles to restore them to their original position. If a material has the property of elasticity and the particles in a certain region are set in vibratory motion, an elastic wave will be propagated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "elastic waves"@en .

aat:300448597 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the culture and style of the indigenous Shapra people of Peru."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shapra"@en .

aat:300448598 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An efflorescent salt found on excavated limestones that have been stored or displayed in the presence of acetic acid vapor. Thecotrichite is composed of calcium acetate nitrate chloride hydrate. It is only formed under very specific conditions in which a calcareous stone that contains chloride and nitrate ions is put in an environment with acetic acid vapors and moisture. Mitigation involves any or all of the following: 1) desalination, 2) decreasing relative humidity, or 3) elimination of acetic acid vapors."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "thecotrichite"@en .

aat:300448599 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Clay that has been stabilized with cement in order to improve subgrade properties at a lower cost than either removing and replacing material or increasing the base thickness to reduce subgrade stress."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cement-stabilized clay"@en .

aat:300448600 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lacquer obtained from the sap of the Melanorrhoea (Gluta) usitata tree, which belongs to the Anacardiaceae family and grows in Laos, Myanmar, Thailand and Cambodia. The sap is harvested from the tree by tapping and has a straw colour, which quickly turns glossy black. Its main function is to waterproof and heatproof the object and has excellent adhesive properties, as well as good stability in hot, acid and alkaline conditions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Burmese lacquer"@en .

aat:300448601 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A deep, reddish-orange synthetic pigment. Patented in 1930 by Lederle in Germany, molybdate orange is a solid solution of lead chromate, lead molybdate, and lead sulfate. The toxic pigment has uniform, small, rounded particles and a high refractive index. Molybdate orange has good covering power but moderate lightfastness. It is used industrially in printing inks, paints, and plastic."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "molybdate orange"@en .

aat:300448602 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Structure, especially in the context of semiconductors, that is an arrangement of molecular dimensions, or nanometers."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "nanostructure"@en .

aat:300448603 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The anhydrous form of calcium chloride is very hygroscopic. It is used as a desiccant. Calcium chloride also exists as dihydrate and hexahydrate crystals. These crystalline forms are used in antifreeze solutions, fire extinguishers, fireproofing solutions, wood preservatives, as a gelling agent for starch size and to melt snow and ice from roads. Calcium chloride was used to speed up the rate of cure in mortar, shotcrete, and dry-mix concrete until 1973 when it was replaced by a non-chloride accelerator because it corroded steel reinforcements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "calcium chloride"@en .

aat:300448604 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A white, crystalline substance, glyceryl stearate, (C18H35 O2)3C3H5, found in the solid portion of most animal and vegetable fats and used in soaps, adhesives, textile sizes, etc. It is a glyceril ester of stearic acid."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stearin"@en .

aat:300448605 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An autobiographical genre of academic writing that draws on and analyzes or interprets the lived experience of the author and connects researcher insights to self-identity, cultural rules and resources, communication practices, traditions, premises, symbols, rules, shared meanings, emotions, values, and larger social, cultural, and political issues."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "autoethnography"@en .

aat:300448606 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A process by which an object is removed or detached from its original context and introduced into the collection of a museum, leading to an alteration in the status of the object. This process includes acquisition, documentation, preservation, and often exhibition."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "musealization"@en .

aat:300448607 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A pale blue naturally occurring mineral composed of strontium sulfate. Celestite is found in sedimentary stone usually in conjunction with halite and dolomitic limestone. Specimens have been found in England (Bristol), Italy (Sicily), Mexico (Matehuala) and the U.S. (Ohio, California). Celestite is mined as a source of strontium. It is also used in the manufacture of ceramics, glass, and fireworks."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "celestite"@en .

aat:300448608 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A lead chloride arsenate mineral found in the oxidized zones of lead deposits. Its color is variable, ranging from pale yellow to yellowish-brown to orange-yellow to orange-red, brownish, greenish, white and colorless."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mimetite"@en .

aat:300448609 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Accumulation of the remains of farm buildings, sod walls, manure and other wastes that are a distinct feature of Norse North-Atlantic archeology. They are found primarily in Norway north of the Arctic Circle, Iceland, Greenland, the Faroes, and possibly in the Northern Isles of Scotland. Some of these mounds are now deserted, while others are on inhabited properties."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "farm mounds"@en .

aat:300448610 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Thin laminates made of continuous fabrics of high-strength steel cords embedded within a cementitious matrix. It is used to strengthen masonry and concrete structures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "steel reinforced grout"@en .

aat:300448611 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An electroanalytical scanning probe technique capable of imaging substrate topography and local reactivity with high resolution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "scanning electrochemical microscopy"@en .

aat:300448612 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A deliquescent solid Sr(OH)2 that forms a crystalline octahydrate, that dissolves in water to form a decidedly alkaline solution, and that is used chiefly in making soaps and greases and in refining beet sugar and recovering sugar from the molasses by the formation of insoluble sucrates."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "strontium hydroxide"@en .

aat:300448613 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A style of the second half of the 19th century and early 20th which was inspired by Assyrian works of art, most specifically sculpture, of the 9th to 7th centuries BC. The Assyrian Revival manifested itself both in the decorative arts, in the form of the ‘Assyrian style’ jewelry produced in England, and in the fine arts in the paintings of a number of later 19th-century French Salon painters. Assyrian motifs were also incorporated in a fairly wide range of buildings in the early 20th century, particularly in the United States."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Assyrian Revival"@en .

aat:300448614 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Testing techniques that penetrate or alter the sample at a microscopic level."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "microinvasive testing"@en .

aat:300448615 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A radar system that uses the motion of the vehicle (such as a spacecraft) carrying it to simulate a system having a much larger antenna area and that is used to obtain high-resolution images of a surface (as of a planet). It requires that the radar be moving in a straight line, either on an airplane or orbiting in space."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "synthetic aperture radar"@en .

aat:300448616 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A technique for depositing a film onto a surface in monolayers by utilizing a self-limiting chemical reaction. It is a vapor phase technique capable of producing thin films of a variety of materials."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "atomic layer deposition"@en .

aat:300448617 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A technique for materials analysis that uses an ion beam to excite atoms in a target, and visible light emitted from the target material is analyzed with a fiber-optic spectrometer. Since visible light results from outer-shell transitions, IBIL can give information about the nature of chemical bonds in materials."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ion beam induced luminescence"@en .

aat:300448618 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A synthetic material prepared to simulate the appearance of wood."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "artificial wood"@en .

aat:300448619 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus containing 14-19 species of palms, depending upon the classification system; occupying diverse habitats, native to northern Africa, the Middle East, and South Asia, but naturalized in other regions worldwide."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Phoenix"@en .

aat:300448620 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of palm cultivated for its edible sweet fruit called dates."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Phoenix dactylifera"@en .

aat:300448623 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Edible fruits of the date palm; oblong drupes with a single hard seed and sweet pulp. An important food crop in North Africa and western Asia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dates"@en .

aat:300448624 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Fruits of the fig tree. Figs are syconia, inflorescences formed by an enlarged, fleshy, hollow receptacle with multiple ovaries on the inside surface. Figs are borne singly or in pairs above the scars of fallen leaves or in axils of leaves of the present season."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "figs"@en .

aat:300448625 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A vividly colored and strongly patterned travertine quarried in Algeria and widely diffused in the Roman Empire. Alabastro a pecorella means little sheep alabaster, but it is the richly coloured bushy growths of iron and manganese oxides that are more distinctive, sometimes giving an almost three dimensional effect."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "alabastro a pecorella"@en .

aat:300448626 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An X-ray spectroscopy technique, usually synchrotron-based, that provides information on the chemical state and short-range atomic order of the top few microns of a surface. It concerns the conversion of the X-ray energy absorbed by the system to optical photons often involving multi-step cascade processes. XEOL is often used together with XANES/NEXAFS to provide site specificity which in turn can help reveal the electronic structure and optical properties of the system of interest."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "x-ray excited optical luminescence"@en .

aat:300448627 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A (dolomitic) calcarenite with calcite cement extracted from the quarries of Val Ceresio in the province of Varese, near the town of Viggiù."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pietra di Viggiù"@en .

aat:300448628 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A ‘sensing system’ comprised of three parts: a sampling system, an array of chemical gas sensors producing an array of signals when confronted with a gas, vapor, or odor, and an appropriate pattern-classification system."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "electronic nose technology"@en .

aat:300448629 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Kura-Araxes cultural tradition existed in the highlands of the South Caucasus from 3500 to 2450 BCE. By 3000 BCE, groups bearing this identity had migrated southwest across a wide area from the Taurus Mountains down into the southern Levant, southeast along the Zagros Mountains, and north across the Caucasus Mountains."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kura-Araxes"@en .

aat:300448630 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The process of subliming and depositing ammonium chloride or other white powder onto natural history specimens and is useful for enhancing specimen relief for photography."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "smoking"@en .

aat:300448631 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lamps for lighting that use LEDs, organic light-emitting diodes (OLEDs), or light-emitting polymers consisting of semiconductors that convert electricity into light. Solid-state lighting is used in a variety of lighting applications because it is energy-efficient, environmentally friendly, and has longer life. They are also considered durable, intrinsically safe, and ideal for smaller, flexible light fixtures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "solid-state lamps"@en .

aat:300448632 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A spectroscopic analysis based on a luminescence phenomenon that provides the differential emission intensity of right and left circularly polarized light, thereby providing information on the excited state properties of the chiral molecular systems."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "circularly polarized luminescence"@en .

aat:300448633 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Larger organizing principles and classifications used by archaeologists in researching and understanding the past."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "archaeological theory"@en .

aat:300448845 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A spectroscopic technique that allows for the non-invasive chemical characterization of diffusely scattering materials, ranging from opaque plastics to biological tissues. SORS has been explored for a range of applications, including disease diagnosis, the detection of explosives through unopened containers and the in-depth, non-destructive analysis of pharmaceutical products and objects of art."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "spatially offset Raman spectroscopy"@en .

aat:300448846 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An alternative to spatially offset Raman spectroscopy (SORS) technique that combines conventional SORS with microscopy concepts and represents a new imaging modality in Raman microscopy. Micro-SORS facilitates analytical capability for investigating non-destructively the chemical composition of subsurface, micrometer-scale-thick diffusely scattering layers, such as stratified paint samples, enabling their nondestructive chemical characterization."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "micro-spatially offset Raman spectroscopy"@en .

aat:300448847 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style of artistic production in German colonies featuring a combination of French and native characteristics, particularly during the German Reich era between the late 19th century and the end of the First World War."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "German Colonial"@en .

aat:300448848 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of pottery found in many parts of India which is black on the inside and around the rim and red on the exterior surface. Generally dated to the Chalcolithic through to the Iron Age."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "black-and-red ware"@en .

aat:300448849 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Term used to describe a tradition of figural sculptures that were consumed in Latin America, but produced in Asia after European and Asian models and typically made of ivory. It had been made in Manila since the Spanish first established a base in the Philippines."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hispano-Philippine"@en .

aat:300448850 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the styles and cultures that developed in the Iberian Peninsula under the influence of Roman rule and culture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ibero-Roman"@en .

aat:300448851 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who compose written material, often as an occupation or profession, whether or not they are responsible for the underlying intellectual content."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "writers"@en .

aat:300448852 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings or complexes, often found at an international expositions or fairs, devoted to a theme but not representing by itself the identity of a single nation or geographic area. Each single building or complex structure has homogeneous characteristics that identify it as a discrete item."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "theme buildings"@en .

aat:300448853 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Free-standing structures for individually sponsored exhibits at trade fairs, international expositions, or other venues such as national parks."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "exhibition kiosks"@en .

aat:300448854 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Portraits, usually photographs in any format, that depict members of a profession, either singly or in groups. Those depicted are most often wearing clothing associated with that profession, or display tools or other paraphernalia associated with it."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "occupational portraits"@en .

aat:300448855 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Individual agents who are members of a group engaged in an activity, but whose identity remains unknown, historically."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "undifferentiated people from group"@en .

aat:300448856 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Representatives or proxies who act for others who intend to sell something, most often real estate, but may be extended to those performing this role in any transaction of goods or services."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "seller's agents"@en .

aat:300448857 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Representatives or proxies who act for others who intend to purchase something for sale. The term is most often applied to this role in real estate transactions, but may be extended to this role in buying any object, property, or service."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "buyer's agents"@en .

aat:300448858 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The conclusion of the transfer of ownership via sale from one entity to another, where all actions to resolve the sale have been achieved."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sales completion"@en .

aat:300448859 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Jianzhuzao, literally translated as \"reduced-column construction\" or \"column-elimination construction,\" is a term used to describe the arrangement of interior columns in traditional Chinese architecture. This term was not used in classical literature. Rather, it was first coined by architectural historians who conducted field investigations and wrote Chinese architectural history from the 1930s to 1960s. Generally, jianzhuzao refers to buildings with fewer interior columns than those with a full interior column grid aligned with the eaves pillars (Liang and Liu 1933; Guo 2009, 786). Some scholars, in accordance with the interpretation that there were two timber-frame structural systems (diange 殿閣/diantang 殿堂 and tingtang 廳堂) defined in Yingzao fashi, argued that only buildings with fewer interior columns than these two structural systems could have \"column elimination\" (Fu 2002). However, Chen Mingda, who used the two timber-frame structural systems as a standard to analyze extant buildings, opposed the use of the \"jianzhu\" neologism (Chen 1981, 4,178-181)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jiǎnzhùzào"@en .

aat:300448861 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Da'e, literally \"great forehead\" (Kroll 2015, 73, 99) is usually used to refer to over-sized column-top tie beams often found in the timber architecture of Shanxi and Shaanxi constructed during the Yuan period (thirteenth-fourteenth centuries). As an architectural term, da’e is only recorded in the Yingzao fashi, but it is not clearly defined. Based on the context in which the term is used, it is possible that da’e is an alternate name for lan’e 闌額. This is because it is often used together with yan’e 簷額 and you’e 由額 where the term lan’e would normally appear. Still, nothing in the Yingzao fashi indicates that this so-called da’e is a larger size version of lan’e. In a seminal article, the twentieth-century architectural historian Zhang Yuhuan used this term to describe huge longitudinal beams found in Yuan timber architecture in Shanxi and Shaanxi. Since that time many modern scholars consider it to be the signature element of the so-called “da’e style” buildings of the Yuan dynasty. Da’e can be placed either under the eaves or inside the building, but must be parallel to the roof ridge as part of the zongjia 縱架. Some scholars believe that the Yuan dynasty da’e are equivalent to the yan’e in the Yingzao fashi, while others disagree."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dà'é"@en .

aat:300448863 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Following Wang Puzi's research, xieshan zhuanjiao has the same meaning as xieshan, commonly translated as hip-gable roof. Indeed, xieshan may be short for xieshan zhuanjiao because they are used interchangeably (Wang 1995, 6-10)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "xiēshān zhuǎnjiǎo"@en .

aat:300448865 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning, literally, \"without shaliangtou.\" See shaliangtou 厦兩頭"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bù shàliǎngtóu"@en .

aat:300448866 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Literally meaning “massed [into a] point” (Kroll 2015, 68; 194) cuanjian is a form of traditional Chinese roof that meets at the top to form a spire. The term cuanjian first appeared in the literature of the Qing dynasty, and is characterized by a steep roof which can have several vertical ridges (in a polygonal plan) or no ridges (in a circular plan) but which comes together at the top in a point and is then covered with a tapered, jewel-shaped finial (baoding 寶頂)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cuánjiān"@en .

aat:300448867 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pingliang refers to cross beams that span two rafters and are located on the highest layer of the roof frame. This term is used in the Yingzao fashi (1103), where it is also called pingfu 平栿. In extant architecture, shuzhu 蜀柱 and chashou 叉手 are joined into the top of the pingliang (Liang 1983, 142; Chen 2010, 114). In official Qing dynasty architectural terminology, the beam in the location of the pingliang is called sanjialiang 三架梁, meaning a beam that spans three purlins (and two rafters), or taipingliang 太平梁."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "píngliáng"@en .

aat:300448869 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rufu 乳栿, literally \"infant beam\" or \"nursling beam\" (Hanyu dacidian; Kroll 2015, 391), refers to cross beams that span two rafters, usually with one end located above the eaves puzuo 鋪作 and the other end inserted into an interior pillar. Liang Sicheng suggests that a rufu might also have both ends layered at the top of the puzuo (Liang 1983, 142; Chen 2008). Chen Mingda notes that a rufu should be located on the lowest level of the roof frame (Chen 2010, 196)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rǔfú"@en .

aat:300448870 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Shaliangtou 厦兩頭, usually understood as “with a mansion on either end”(Kroll 492, 458), only appears as a technical term of architecture in the literature of the Tang and Song dynasties. Here it refers to a roof form used on a tingtang 廳堂 (mansion hall) building. It is similar to the xieshan roof in the Qing dynasty, which is commonly translated as “hip-gable” or “hip-and-gable” roof, or a roof composed of four slopes and two gables in which corner beams extend at a 45 degree angle to support eaves sheltering the corner of the building. In the Tang dynasty, only officials of the fifth rank or above were allowed to use shaliangtou in their houses. The same form of roof used in diantang 殿堂 buildings is called jiujidian 九脊殿 (nine-ridged hall) (Tianyige Musuem 2006, 344)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "shàliǎngtóu"@en .

aat:300448871 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "As an architectural term, caojia (literally “draft frame,” Kroll 2015 34, 194), primarily appears in technical manuals such as the Yingzao fashi(1103), Yuanye 園冶 (1631), and Yingzao fayuan 營造法原 (1959). It refers to the roof frame between the interior ceiling (the pingqi 平棊 in Yingzao Fashi and the xuan 軒 or juan 卷 in mansion-hall style architecture of Jiangnan area in Ming and Qing dynasties) and the roof, which is left rough (hence the term “draft”) because it is not visible to the viewer (Chen 2010, 259; Yao 1986, 116)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cǎojià"@en .

aat:300448873 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lingxingmen is an elaborate gateway with a form similar to wutoumen 烏頭門. Usually, a lingxingmen is used at the entrance of ritual architectural complexes, such as the Wenmiao (Temple of Literature), sacrificial altars to the spirits of Heaven and Earth, and other religious complexes. The term lingxingmen was used beginning in the Northern Song dynasty (960-1127)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "língxīngmén"@en .

aat:300448875 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In traditional literature, the term ceyang is primarily used in the Yingzao fashi (1103) of the Song dynasty. Literally meaning “side appearance” (Kroll 2005 35, 534), it refers to the view of architectural frameworks or components from the side perpendicular to the roof ridge, which is then expressed in the form of a drawing (Li 2009, 5.19b, 30.6a-7a, 31.3a-6a)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cèyàng"@en .

aat:300448877 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Wutoumen refers to a type of elaborate gateway used in high ranking buildings through the Song dynasty, after which it declines in use. As we can tell from paintings and extant examples, the shape, size, and structure of wutoumen vary considerably. However, all wutoumen employ tall pillars flanking the gate. The name is likely derived from the black ceramic tube-shaped finials on the top of these tall gate posts (Cefu yuangui 冊府元龜, 1013). Detailed records of wutoumen exist in the Song dynasty Yingzao fashi (1103, Li 2009, 6.2a-4a)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wūtóumén"@en .

aat:300448879 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Literally meaning a “bright” or “open” space (Kroll 2015, 309; 196), mingjian usually refers to the central bay of a building."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "míngjiān"@en .

aat:300448881 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Literally meaning “pretend” or “simulated” ang, jia’ang refers to a bracket arm that appears to be a xia’ang visually, but does not function as a xia’ang structurally. Architectural historians have used jia’ang as a term in traditional Chinese architecture since the 1930s (Liang 1933，26). Usually it refers to a huagong in an eaves bracket set (waiyan dougong 外簷斗栱) with a shape of ang mouth (angzui 昂嘴) on the outside. In most cases, there is a carved huatouzi 華頭子 underneath the jia'ang."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jiǎ’áng"@en .

aat:300448885 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning literally pondweed, or water plant, well (Kroll 2015, 584-585), in pre-modern Chinese literature, zaojing 藻井 refers to a flat or recessed decorative ceiling which is based on an orthogonal grid and obscures the roof frame."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "zǎojǐng"@en .

aat:300448887 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "See xinjian 心間."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dāngxīnjiān"@en .

aat:300448888 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Literally meaning “solid” or “rigid” mountain peak, yingshan is a technical term used to describe a traditional Chinese gable roof, in which the eaves of the gable are flush with the surface of the wall, in contrast to one where the eaves extend past the wall plane on the gable end (see xuanshan). The term yingshan first appeared in the literature of the Qing dynasty. In the narrowest sense, yingshan refers to a roof that has front and rear slopes, in which the purlins are sealed within the walls of the gable ends (Liang 2006, 84; Yao 1986, 40). More broadly, yingshan is regularly used to refer to a gable roof in which the top of the gable ends extend to the eaves tiles or, in some cases, can even extend above the level of the roof."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yìngshān"@en .

aat:300448889 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Danchi, meaning literally “cinnabar staircase or courtyard” (Kroll 2015, 74-75; 50-51), generally refers to the surface of the platform extending in front of the main hall of a palace or the surface of the courtyards in a palace, and is one of the main areas where ceremonies were held. It originated from the practice of painting the steps with cinnabar in the Han dynasty, which may have been inherited from the Zhou dynasty (Yang 2001,163, 214). Over time, the locations of danchi changed. During the Han dynasty danchi usually referred to the ground or floor inside a hall. After the Tang and Song dynasties, danchi was usually associated with the platform in front of a hall. In the Northern Song, it referred to the ground of the topmost platform in front of a palace building, and, together with longchi 龍墀 and shachi 沙墀, it constituted the sanchi 三墀 system. In the Liao dynasty and after it referred to the ground below the platform, which is on the same level as the courtyard ground. During the Ming and Qing dynasties, danchi also referred to the ground below the platform of the main hall in Confucian temples, government offices, and other types of buildings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dānchí"@en .

aat:300448891 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Danbi, literally meaning “cinnabar steps” or “cinnabar platform” (Kroll 2015, 74-75; 20), generally refers to the steps of palace buildings, and in some contexts, to the open platform in front of a main palace hall.The term danbi originated from the practice of painting the steps of palace buildings with cinnabar. The earliest evidence of the use of danbi comes from the Lingqijing, which is traditionally dated to the Western Han. During the Ming and Qing dynasty dynasties, danbi became part of mainstream architectural nomenclature and was used in conjunction with danchi 丹墀, where danbi referred to the platform (yuetai 月台) outside the hall, and danchi referred to the ground in front of the danbi. However, danbi is also commonly translated as \"steps.\" In literature before the Ming and Qing dynasties, danbi was not used for buildings other than palace buildings, while in the Ming and Qing dynasties, the scope of reference broadened to include Confucian temples, government offices, and other types of buildings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dānbì"@en .

aat:300448893 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning literally interrupted or halted mountain (peak) (Kroll 2015, 503, 399), in an architectural context xieshan 歇山 is a form of traditional Chinese gabled roof with four ridges (two per side) framing the gable, above a lower hip roof with an additional four ridges above four corner beams supporting the hip (Qing gongbu 1773, 54.1b). Sometimes it is written as xieshan ding 歇山頂 (anonymous 1644-1911, 29a). The term is first seen in Qing dynasty texts and was only used for palace or other large-scale buildings. In modern research, xieshan also describes a roof form without obvious ridges but with four slopes and two gables in vernacular architecture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "xiēshān"@en .

aat:300448894 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "According to the Yingzao fashi, zhaqian refers to cross beams that span one rafter (Liang 1983, 142). Zhaqian usually only serves to connect two members, but is not load-bearing. In extant buildings in southeast China, a similar component--called shuiliang 水梁 in Zhejiang and Jiangsu, shumu 束木 in southern Fujian, and shuishu 水束 in Guangdong--structurally combines zhaqian, chashou 叉手, and tuojiao 托腳."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "zhāqiān"@en .

aat:300448896 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Literally “heart” or “central” “bay” (Kroll 2015, 507; 196; Steinhardt 1991, 27-50). According to the Yingzao fashi, xinjian (also called dangxinjian 當心間) refers to the central bay of a building, divided longitudinally by the two central front pillars (pingzhu 平柱) and covering the entire depth (YZFS 5.7a). Occasionally, it may also refer to the unit of space between these two pillars (YZFS 4.9a-b;5.4b). According to the Yingzao fashi, there should only be one xinjian in any given building."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "xīnjiān"@en .

aat:300448897 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning literally to prop and rotate (Kroll 2015 453, 479), the term tiao‘wo, as most scholars believe, is a noun that refers to a component in the timber frame structure, used in the interior of a building with an open-frame roof structure (cheshang mingzao 徹上明造; i.e. without a ceiling). Tiao'wo appears in the Yingzao fashi (1103) four times (YZFS 2009, 1.8a, 4.6a, 6.9b, 18.7a). There are two types of tiao'wo. (1) As a part of a component, tiao'wo refers to the upward end of a xia'ang in a bujian puzuo. This kind of tiaowo is also called angwei tiaowo 昂尾挑斡 by some scholars. Sometimes it can also refer to the upward end of a shuatou. (2) As a separate component, tiaowo refers to an oblique element without pointed shape, a particular form of xia'ang. Some scholars believe this is the angting tiaowo mentioned in Yingzao fashi (Zhu 2018)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tiǎowò"@en .

aat:300448899 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning literally to prop and rotate (Kroll 2015 453, 479), the term tiao‘wo, as most scholars believe, is a noun that refers to a component in the timber frame structure, used in the interior of a building with an open-frame roof structure (cheshang mingzao 徹上明造; i.e. without a ceiling). Tiao'wo appears in the Yingzao fashi (1103) four times (YZFS 2009, 1.8a, 4.6a, 6.9b, 18.7a). There are two types of tiao'wo. (1) As a part of a component, tiao'wo refers to the upward end of a xia'ang in a bujian puzuo. This kind of tiaowo is also called angwei tiaowo 昂尾挑斡 by some scholars. Sometimes it can also refer to the upward end of a shuatou. (2) As a separate component, tiaowo refers to an oblique element without pointed shape, a particular form of xia'ang. Some scholars believe this is the angting tiaowo mentioned in Yingzao fashi (Zhu 2018)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "shàng'áng"@en .

aat:300448900 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Literally meaning “dew terrace” or “exposed terrace” (Kroll 285; 440), a lutai is a raised platform within a building complex. In the literature of the Han and Tang dynasties, lutai referred specifically to a structure built by Emperor Wen of the Han dynasty. The term later appeared in a large number of Song dynasty texts to indicate a space for the performance of theater, music, and dance in cities, private gardens, and temples, as well as the places at which sacrifices were made to the gods. There are also a few records that refer to the platform of the ramp of the city gate as a lutai (Li 2009,15.3b)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lùtái"@en .

aat:300448902 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "From the pre-Qin to the Ming and Qing dynasties, the form, structure, and function of ting have undergone significant changes. During the Eastern Zhou, ting referred to a three-zhang (roughly 30 ft) high military fort. In the Eastern Han, it functioned as a station house (yizhan 驛站). It could also be a government compound serving both military and registration purposes. In literature relating to the southern part of China in the Six Dynasties period, ting were built in high places and were often recognized as landmarks or gathering spots for literati and aristocrats. During the Tang dynasty, ting were built in fortified palaces (gongcheng 宮城 or \"palace-cities\"), were used as residences, and were enclosed by walls. They did not have to be high buildings or placed in elevated areas. After the Song dynasty, ting were often found in gardens and used for leisurely activities. In the Yingzao fashi, the ting is considered a non-essential structural form and tingxie, referring to this structural form in conjunction with its platform beneath, is an architectural type coined in the Yingzao fashi. Its form and location became even more flexible in Ming private gardens, though it was mainly a simple and open freestanding structure. Regardless of its transformation through time, the ting’s close connection to the appreciation of natural beauty persisted from the Wei, Jin, Northern and Southern Dynasties period through the Qing dynasty. The modern definition that ting is \"a building with a roof but without enclosing walls\" (He, et al. 2015, 210) is closest to the use of ting in Ming private gardens."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tíng"@en .

aat:300448904 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Chinese architectural tradition, pillars used to support the eaves, usually placed around the outer perimeter of a structure."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yánzhù"@en .

aat:300448906 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning, literally, illustrious marker or memorial (Kroll 2015, 167, 23), huabiao originated as wooden or stone pillars whose function was to show directions or display information. Later, huabiao also helped emphasize the central axis or the central portion of an architectural complex.According to earlier texts quoted by the Tang commentator Sima Zhen (司馬貞, 679-732) in his Shiji suoyin 史記索隱, a huabiao (or a pair of huabiao) was a type of signpost or guidepost with a crosspiece joined through the upper part of the pillar. He quoted Cui Bao 崔豹 (ca. 381-450) who suggested that because of similarities in pronunciation in the Chen and Chu regions, huabiao 華表 may have also been written \"huanbiao 桓表\" or \"hebiao 和表\" (Sima 1959, 10.423-424; Cui 1936). Huabiao-like structures can be seen in visual material, especially in Song and Yuan paintings, such as Zhang Zeduan's \"Jinmingchi zhengbiao tu 金明池爭標圖\" and Wang Zhengpeng's \"Longchi jingdu tujuan 龍池競渡圖卷,\" where a characteristic feature was a crosspiece joined through the upper part of the pillar. After the Song dynasty, we also begin to see crouching animal sculptures on the top of huabiao."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "huábiǎo"@en .

aat:300448908 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The culture of Americans of German descent."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "German-American"@en .

aat:300448909 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The culture of Americans of Italian descent."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Italian-American"@en .

aat:300448910 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The culture of Americans of Irish descent."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Irish-American"@en .

aat:300448911 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Structures, landscape elements, sculptures, or monuments designed and executed with unity and harmony as a composite spatial solution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "architectural ensembles"@en .

aat:300448912 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of granite quarried in the vicinity of Dedham, Massachusetts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dedham granite"@en .

aat:300448913 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Museums focusing in memoriam of a person or event at which people died."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "memorial museums"@en .

aat:300448914 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rooms or buildings for reading or repeating texts or other materials aloud, especially publicly. Most often found in schools or universities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "recitation halls"@en .

aat:300448915 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing federated divisions of a university where a particular field of academic activity is located together, often with residences and shared meals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "residential colleges"@en .

aat:300448916 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Structures housing facilities and institutions dedicated to science."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "science structures"@en .

aat:300448917 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings or complexes having facilities or housing institutions dedicated to the practice or study of an area or areas of science."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "science buildings"@en .

aat:300448918 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Printed books produced in during the early development of printing that feature illustrative elements added by hand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "illuminated incunabula"@en .

aat:300448919 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Unnaturalized foreign residents of a country, nation, or state who do not hold official citizenship status within that place. Noncitizens may or may not possess residence documentation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "noncitizens"@en .

aat:300448920 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings in which science is taught, often containing laboratories or other hands-on learning and research facilities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "science schools"@en .

aat:300448921 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Official residences of a president, who is an appointed or elected head of a group, state, or organization."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "presidents' houses"@en .

aat:300448922 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Enclosures or stands, permanent or temporary, and freestanding or not, used for the stationing of a guard, usually at an entrance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "guard booths"@en .

aat:300448923 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing offices and often the printing presses for a printing and publishing enterprise."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "press buildings"@en .

aat:300448924 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings used for the academic study of, instruction of, or performance of music."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "music buildings"@en .

aat:300448925 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Persons known of by the general public who have a role of special social, cultural, or political prominence as monarchs, state leaders, mayors, ministers, artists or musicians."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "public figures"@en .

aat:300448926 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Persons or corporate bodies who participate in the exhibition of objects, documents, or information at fairs, conferences, shows, or other events, public or private."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "exhibitors"@en .

aat:300448927 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographs of recently deceased persons taken as part of the mourning and memorialization process."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "postmortem photographs"@en .

aat:300448928 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Individual buildings, often in the context of a larger complex or group of buildings that house degree-granting institutions called \"universities,\" which may typically include liberal arts undergraduate colleges, graduate schools, and undergraduate or graduate professional schools."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "university buildings"@en .

aat:300448929 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Individual buildings, often in the context of a larger complex or group of buildings that house institutions of higher learning called \"colleges.\" which provide general or liberal arts education leading to bachelor's degrees; may be independent or parts of universities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "college buildings"@en .

aat:300448930 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings that house facilities and resources for training educators."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "teaching centers"@en .

aat:300448931 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings that house facilities and resources, and offer information and programs, for promoting and educating on topics of international cultures and history."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "international centers"@en .

aat:300448932 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing facilities for teaching continuing education, and related to a university of college."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "extension buildings"@en .

aat:300448933 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing schools focusing on programs help prepare students for careers in specific fields, such as the medical, law, pharmacy, business, library, and social work fields."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "professional schools"@en .

aat:300448934 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Laboratories focused on designing, constructing, and testing engines and machinery, or on structures such as roads and bridges."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "engineering laboratories"@en .

aat:300448935 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Laboratories focused on designing, constructing, and testing computers and computer programs. For centers housing computers and service for computing, use \"computer centers.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "computer laboratories"@en .

aat:300448936 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Laboratories focused on analysis and experimentation with chemicals. The building will be optimized for the necessary equipment for preparation of samples and analysis of liquid and solid samples, housing equipment such as glassware, fume hoods, furnace, centrifuge, mill, and oven."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chemistry laboratories"@en .

aat:300448937 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gates serving as a memorial. May be in the tradition of, or even incorporating a memorial arch."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "memorial gates"@en .

aat:300448940 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings arranged around a space or a courtyard that is usually rectangular in plan. Common examples are in universities and palaces."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "quadrangles"@en .

aat:300448941 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pavilions that represent a nation at an international fair or exhibition of art."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "national pavilions"@en .

aat:300448942 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Structures erected that are intended to remain in place indefinitely."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "permanent structures"@en .

aat:300448943 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing an institution called a \"university college,\" which provides tertiary education but does not have full or independent university status, often part of a larger university."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "university colleges"@en .

aat:300448944 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Institutions that provide tertiary education but do not have full or independent university status, often part of a larger university. The designation varies from place to place, but in British and formerly British educational systems, such an institution of higher learning does not have the authority to award its own degrees. Students enrolled at a university college ordinarily receive their degrees from a recognized university. In due course, a university college may be granted university status."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "university colleges"@en .

aat:300448945 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings in which agricultural theory and practice is taught."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "agricultural schools"@en .

aat:300448946 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Branches or departments of knowledge, particularly at a university."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "faculties"@en .

aat:300448947 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings that are ornamental or functional, usually of light construction; may be designed as temporary. Uses include for shelter, refreshment, entertainment, or recreation. Examples may be found in a park, world's fair, seaside, adjacent to a sports field, or in a group of buildings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pavilions"@en .

aat:300448948 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The condition or role when a party, entity, action, or situation does not have a direct causal or other direct relationship with another party, entity, action, or situation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "indirect relationship"@en .

aat:300448949 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The condition or role when a party, entity, action, or situation does not have a direct causal or other direct relationship with another party, entity, action, or situation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "indirect causal relationship"@en .

aat:300448950 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The condition or role when a party, entity, action, or situation has a direct causal relationship with another party, entity, action, or situation. For example, an action may be proven to have caused a situation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "direct causal relationship"@en .

aat:300448951 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who guide or inspire others, or who affect others by ascendancy, sway, control, or authority, especially when not formally or overtly expressed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "influencers"@en .

aat:300448953 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings that have a light or airy construction, such as those using plywood, plasterboard, steel studs, or frames without walls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "light construction"@en .

aat:300448954 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Halls dedicated as a memorial, particularly to those lost in war. Purposes of the halls vary; often found at universities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "memorial halls"@en .

aat:300448955 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for buildings in which fine arts instruction is carried out or in which fine arts are displayed; examples are found at universities or at a worlds fair."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fine arts buildings"@en .

aat:300448956 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing educational institutions that focus entirely or partially on religious instruction and theology."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "religious schools"@en .

aat:300448957 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings in which the study of religions, doctrines, practices, histories, and relationships of the world's religions is facilitated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "schools of comparative religion"@en .

aat:300448958 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Library buildings having collections focused on the study and application of medicine and healing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "medical libraries"@en .

aat:300448959 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Library buildings having collections focused on the study and application of law."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "law libraries"@en .

aat:300448960 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing educational institutions that focus on training, advising, and research on governmental systems and practical applications."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "schools of government"@en .

aat:300448961 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Laboratories focused on analysis and experimentation in biology."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "biology laboratories"@en .

aat:300448962 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing academic enterprises that teach art."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "design schools"@en .

aat:300448963 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing academic enterprises that prepare educators."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "schools of education"@en .

aat:300448964 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gates or gateways marking the entrance to a site, such as a campus, park, estate, or other property."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "entrance gates"@en .

aat:300448965 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The areas or compilations of changes made to renew and improve a built work."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "renovations"@en .

aat:300448966 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings or areas of buildings that support student success by providing materials, activies, and learning strategies, including independent and self-directed learning activities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "learning centers"@en .

aat:300448967 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Works of art, traditionally from the 18th and 19th century in Great Britain, that depict \"country pursuits\" such as game hunting, fishing, dog racing, horse racing, and pugilism. The genre is sometimes expanded to works of art that depict any kind of amateur or professional sport."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sporting art"@en .

aat:300448968 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing establishments where people may go for fitness, yoga, nutritional counseling, physical therapy, chiropractic care, preventative medicine, and eastern medicine."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wellness centers"@en .

aat:300448969 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ancient buildings, or later buildings designed in the ancient style, where male competitors for games were trained, and where men engaged in social and intellectual pursuits. Gymnasia were generally located in the suburbs and tended to have a standard plan: they were large structures divided into spaces for various types of athletics, also having an open stadium, baths, palaestra, outer porticos used for athletic practice in inclement weather, and covered porticos where learned men gave public lectures and disputations. For more modern buildings inspired by ancient gymnasia and used for athletics, prefer \"gymnasiums (physical education buildings)\"; for more modern buildings used for preparing educational pursuits, use \"gymnasiums (prep school buildings).\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gymnasia"@en .

aat:300448970 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings for upper-secondary schools that prepare students for entrance to a university. Examples are in Germany, Scandinavia, and central Europe. For buildings in ancient Greece and Rome devoted to athletic training and intellectual pursuits, use \"gymnasia (ancient buildings).\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gymnasiums"@en .

aat:300448971 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for rooms or buildings having equipment for physical work outs, such as weights, treadmills, and the like. To distinguish facilities having work out equipment but also other amenities and classes, prefer \"fitness centers.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gyms"@en .

aat:300448972 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing indoor swimming pools, particularly large buildings or complexes containing multiple pools, and occasionally also an area of a sea, lake, or other outdoor body of water suitable for swimming."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "natatoriums"@en .

aat:300448973 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Large rooms or a building, as in a school, designed to combine the functions of gymnasium and auditorium, used for athletic games, performances, presentations, assemblies, and other social events."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gymnatoriums"@en .

aat:300448974 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The condition whereby a person supplies compulsory labor to another, due to obligation, imprisonment, or another reason. In modern usage, does not include the condition of voluntary employment for domestic servants, whether past or present."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "servitude"@en .

aat:300448975 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Any of various synthetic materials resembling grassy turf, used especially as a playing surface for sports."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "artificial turf"@en .

aat:300448976 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Library buildings having collections focused on rare books, manuscripts, and the like."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rare books libraries"@en .

aat:300448977 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Works including books, journal articles, speeches, reviews, research reports that analyze, assess or interpret an historical event, era, or phenomenon, by someone who did not experience first-hand or participate in the events or conditions, and generally utilizing primary sources to do so. Secondary sources often offer a review or a critique. Also included would be reference sources like encyclopedias."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "secondary sources"@en .

aat:300448978 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Concatenates a variety of constructions that embody a theme for a spectacular event such as an international exposition or trade fair. For semi-permanent structures in similar contexts that may be larger-scale and/or inhabitable by groups of people use 'theme buildings.'"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "theme structures"@en .

aat:300448979 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing a centralized system that cools the air for buildings, thus providing the air-conditioning portion of HVAC systems."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chiller plants"@en .

aat:300448980 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The process of shedding the values, practices, effects and influence of colonization within a formerly colonized culture, people or a former colonial power. This includes the changes in thought processes, attitudes, assumptions, power structures, and institutions of the colonized and colonizer respectively."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "decolonization"@en .

aat:300448981 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Center focused on training and performance of music."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "music centers"@en .

aat:300448982 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lodges or similar structures at a walled boundary, through which the road or driveway passes. Examples are at boundaries of estates, parks, or grounds, at driveway to the main house; often architecturally in concert with the main house. Popular in 17th-19th centuries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "entrance lodges"@en .

aat:300448983 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The act of exchange or contract by which parties trade goods or commodities for other goods of equivalent value. Distinguished from 'selling', which is defined as a transaction of goods or property for money. Use 'exchange' in specific instances where goods are exchanged for goods or exchange media, or where 'barter' is less appropriate."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "barter"@en .

aat:300448984 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for names or appellations used by visual artists, poets and writers to identify themselves as responsible for the works; typically used for East Asian artists."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "art names"@en .

aat:300448985 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In East Asia, names given to individuals at adulthood, in addition to one's given name. In China, historically, the assignation of a courtesy name occurred at age twenty."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "courtesy names"@en .

aat:300448986 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Titles or names for works that consist of the work type or otherwise are very generic. For example, if a chapel has the generic title \"Chapel\" rather than a more specific title, such as \"Chapel of St. Mary.\" In book cataloging, generic titles may be constructed by the cataloger to collect works by a prolific author (e.g., \"Speeches\" as a title for a group of works by William Shakespeare)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "generic titles"@en .

aat:300448987 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The culture and style related to the administrative region of Macedonia in modern Greece."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Macedonian"@en .

aat:300448988 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of decoration or signage made from plastic film that adheres to the surface of a window by means of static electricity so that it is easily removeable and reusable."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "window clings"@en .

aat:300448989 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Daguerreotypes consisting of two similar images taken at slightly different angles that when viewed in tandem produce the illusion of depth. These were not uncommon, but were not ideal compared to paper-print based stereoscopic photographs owing to the reflective surface of daguerreoptypes, and the expense and fragility of the medium."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stereoscopic daguerreotypes"@en .

aat:300448990 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing schools that award advanced academic degrees to students that have already earned an undergraduate degree."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "graduate schools"@en .

aat:300448991 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Schools that award advanced academic degrees to students that have already earned an undergraduate degree"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "graduate schools"@en .

aat:300448992 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Schools that award general education degrees in a wide variety of subjects that are not part of graduate education."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "undergraduate schools"@en .

aat:300448993 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Built works housing schools that award general education degrees in a wide variety of subjects that are not part of graduate education."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "undergraduate schools"@en .

aat:300448994 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "States that are recognized in law, but who in reality (de facto) have little or no control over their territory."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "de jure sovereign states"@en .

aat:300448995 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Formalized gatherings that mark the end of organized events such as exhibitions or tournaments."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "closing ceremonies"@en .

aat:300448996 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for structures used for protection from outside forces or the elements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "protective structures"@en .

aat:300448997 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Accessways for cars and pedestrians that end in a court or courtyard area."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "auto courts"@en .

aat:300448998 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Upright partition screens that are painted or upon which one or more paintings is mounted."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "painted screens"@en .

aat:300448999 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small pouch containers for sacred, protective, or lucky items."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fetish pouches"@en .

aat:300449000 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pouches serving as talismans used in Angola, Portugal, and Brazil, particularly during 17th-19th centuries. Most information about these objects comes from the records of the Portuguese Inquisition, which accused those who owned or made them of sorcery."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bolsas de mandinga"@en .

aat:300449001 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bound volumes containing assembled collections of two-dimensional visual works in any media. This refers to volumes populated with works, though these may have been originally constructed with blank pages that intended to have pictorial matter affixed, or assembled solely from plates of pre-printed or painted matter. For similar objects containing only photographs use \"photograph albums.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pictorial albums"@en .

aat:300449002 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Shape resembling or suggestive of a rose."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "roseate"@en .

aat:300449003 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Belonging to the ethnicity and culture that existed in Estonia and Latvia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Baltic German"@en .

aat:300449004 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Inhabited places comprising a significant number of dwellings or other structures located entirely, or mostly, underground. Distinguished from \"buried settlements,\" which were constructed above ground but buried afterwards. For one structure or a small number of dwellings underground, use \"underground building.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "underground settlements"@en .

aat:300449005 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Settlements that are located entirely or mostly in caves, usually having had development beginning with natural caves, which may have then been enlarged by excavation. For single dwellings or a relatively small number of structures in caves, use \"cave architecture\" or \"rock-cut architecture.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cave settlements"@en .

aat:300449006 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographic prints of the 19th century with a mounting format described variously as 3-1/8 x 1-5/8 inches and 3-1/8 x 1-3/8."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "promenade midget photographs"@en .

aat:300449007 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing learning environments for young children, preparing them for the elementary education system."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "child development centers"@en .

aat:300449008 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Institutions that provide learning environments for young children, preparing them for the elementary education system."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "child development centers"@en .

aat:300449009 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings or parts of buildings that house casts; usually art musuems or study collections that house plaster casts of ancient sculptures or other works. For museums of wax casts, use \"wax museums.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cast museums"@en .

aat:300449010 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Chapels located in a university or college."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "university chapels"@en .

aat:300449011 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Chapels located in a hospital or clinic."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hospital chapels"@en .

aat:300449012 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Chapels located in an airport."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "airport chapels"@en .

aat:300449015 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A formalized pool or reservoir. Derived from 'Bi'r' meaning well, cistern or water reservoir."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "birkats"@en .

aat:300449017 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Designates holy areas around shrines in Islamic architecture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "harams"@en .

aat:300449018 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Islamic library spaces that may be incorporated into such other institutions as mosques and madrasas, or exist as separate structures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "maktabas"@en .

aat:300449019 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Islamic elementary school buildings primarily used for teaching children in reading, writing, grammar, and Islamic studies, such as memorizing and reciting the Qur'an. These were historically built as part of religious and charitable complexes sponsored by rulers or local elites."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kuttābs"@en .

aat:300449020 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings or rooms used in teaching anatomy at early modern universities. The plan was typically an amphitheater, with a tiered structure around a central table upon which the dissection of cadavers was performed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "anatomical theaters"@en .

aat:300449021 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The idea that all individuals are equals and that whenever individuals behave positively or negatively this behavior is not judged as being representative of an entire race-based group. To be antiracist is to deracialize behavior."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "antiracist"@en .

aat:300449022 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Persons who engage in the care or well-being of one or more others. Caregivers may be family members or those who act as family though they are not related by heredity, though they may also be a parent, foster-parent, or social services professional."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "caregivers"@en .

aat:300449023 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to a Chicano and Latino subculture or lifestyle associated with a particular set of dress, behavior, and worldview that originated in Los Angeles. Cholo was once a derogatory term for people of mixed-bloodheritage in the Spanish Empire in Latin America and its successor states, but was reclaimed by Chicano youth in the 1960s and emerged as a popular identification in the late 1970s. Cholos/cholas are often stereotyped and unfairly associated with criminal behavior and gang activity. The cholo/chola subculture has spread to cities in the United States with large Chicano or Latino populations, including New York."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cholo"@en .

aat:300449024 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes nations of the world that are considered economically and technologically advanced. Generally consider this more current and accurate than \"First World.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "developed countries"@en .

aat:300449025 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Discrimination or exclusion based on racial or ethnic grounds, arising from systems, structures, or expectations that have become established within society or an institution. This may be applied to government, business, education, news and entertainment media, and other systems. Does not connote the intent, but rather to the structures and policies of the institution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "institutional racism"@en .

aat:300449026 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Style or culture of Texans of Mexican descent. Tejano derives from Coahuiltejano, a name given to the citizens of the Mexican State Coahuila y Tejas, now Texas."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tejano"@en .

aat:300449028 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for buildings or rooms primarily used for performances, which are the executions of a literary, artistic, musical, theatrical, or other creative work."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "performance halls"@en .

aat:300449029 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Wall brackets of a particular style distinctive to Venetian interiors; they typically were used to hang household items or clothing. There was often a piece of damask or other textile placed behind the bracket."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "soaze"@en .

aat:300449031 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Artworks or ornaments carved out of wood."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "woodcarvings"@en .

aat:300449032 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Location where the aldermen of a city joined and held meetings in the middle ages and ancien régime. Predecessor of the town halls and city halls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "aldermen’s houses"@en .

aat:300449034 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Liturgical vestments worn by all three classes of the Orthodox Church (deacon, priest, bishop). These correspond to the Western alb, but, unlike it, sticharions may be of any colour. The sticharion of the deacon is an open vestment, held together by buttons."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sticharions"@en .

aat:300449035 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sets consisting of a stand holding a holy water bucket, censer, and incense vessel."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "incense sets"@en .

aat:300449036 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Depictions, often sculptural, of the crucified Christ on Golgotha in the midst of crucified criminals and those present, including Mary, his mother, apostle John, Mary Magdalene, Roman soldiers etc."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Calvary groups"@en .

aat:300449037 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Black round hat with a wide flat brim, with hanging black tassels on a cord that is tied around the ball."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "priest's hats"@en .

aat:300449038 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Metal devotional medals that depict Christian figures of veneration including Jesus, saints, or the Virgin Mary."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "scapular medals"@en .

aat:300449039 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Fine gold or silver twisted thread that is used spirally in embroidery and is often made for stringing for robes or as a military braid."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cannetille"@en .

aat:300449040 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand tool for pressing the scutched flax into flax bales."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bale hammers"@en .

aat:300449041 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Furniture for supporting flax bales."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bale chairs"@en .

aat:300449042 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Externally driven machine with percussion hammers or rollers for deseeding flax stems."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "crushing machines"@en .

aat:300449043 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand tool with a long, curved handle and a notched wood block for breaking the wooden core of the flax stem."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "braking mallets"@en .

aat:300449044 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand tool with hinged top frame for breaking the wooden core of the flax stems."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "flax brakes"@en .

aat:300449046 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Externally driven device for breaking rippled seed bulbs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "boll breakers"@en .

aat:300449047 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand tool for turning field retting flax."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "forked turning rods"@en .

aat:300449048 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "One-wheeled vehicles for turning field retting flax."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "turning wheels"@en .

aat:300449049 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Externally driven devices for separating the wooden parts from the flax tows."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tow cleaners"@en .

aat:300449050 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Container part of a linseed sorter."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "linseed crates"@en .

aat:300449051 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Containers for the germination of linseed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "linseed propagators"@en .

aat:300449052 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Devices for removing weed seeds and earth lumps from linseed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "linseed sorters"@en .

aat:300449053 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Manually driven cabinets with paddle wheels and sieves, for separating by means of air displacement seed and impurities in crops such as flax."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "winnowing mills"@en .

aat:300449054 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Machines with iron pins for pulling, breaking and cleaning flax bulbs from flax or hemp stems."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ripple machines"@en .

aat:300449055 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tools with rotating and serrated cylinders for breaking flax stems."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "roller brakes"@en .

aat:300449056 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Container for dipping flax bundles when retting in running water."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "retting crates"@en .

aat:300449057 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand tool in wood with finger-thick teeth for untangling scutched flax."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "flax combs"@en .

aat:300449058 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pulled, unsprung, three-wheeled vehicle with an open loading surface for the transport of flax bundles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "flax carts"@en .

aat:300449059 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand tool for grabbing a cup of scutched flax."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "flax tongs"@en .

aat:300449060 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Slotted planks, usually used upright and often fitted to a base used with a bat to remove broken straw while scutching flax by hand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "scutching boards"@en .

aat:300449061 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand protection while scutching flax."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hand-guards"@en .

aat:300449062 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Wooden flat hand tool for scutching the flax stems by hand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "scutching blades"@en .

aat:300449063 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys consisting of a toy gun in combination with one or more targets."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "shooting toys"@en .

aat:300449064 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Elastic cords of 3 to 5 meters long tied into a circle, used in a game of Chinese jump rope, or elastics, involving jumping patterns for 3 or more people. Traditional Chinese elastics are different rubber elastics tied together, but many types of commercial elastics exist."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chinese jump ropes"@en .

aat:300449065 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small balls made of an elastic material such as rubber or silicone. After the ball has been thrown with some force, the ball bounces several more times."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bouncy balls"@en .

aat:300449066 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Large inflatable balls used in various recreational games and activities, originally intended for the beach."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "beach balls"@en .

aat:300449067 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys or sets where a ball has to be hit with rackets. Unlike professional sports equipment, this is marketed in all shapes and colors for recreational use."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tennis games"@en .

aat:300449068 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys or sets in which a shuttle must be hit with special long-handled rackets. Unlike professional sports equipment, this is marketed in all shapes and colors for recreational use."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "badminton games"@en .

aat:300449069 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Jokari is a paddle ball game principally for two players, but it can also be played solo, for example against a wall. The game consists of a rubber ball attached to an anchor by means of a long elastic band, which makes the ball come back when you hit it. Jokari is played with wooden paddles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jokari sets"@en .

aat:300449070 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys that children usually use on the beach or in the sandbox. Sand molds can have all kinds of shapes. By pressing sand into the shape, then turning it over and removing the shape, three-dimensional shapes are created in the sand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sand mold toys"@en .

aat:300449071 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The toy consists of a sturdy cord with two hard plastic balls at the ends. The cord is knotted to a plastic tab. This tab is held between the thumb and forefinger, so that the balls hang side by side in equilibrium. A quick up and down motion of the wrist causes the balls above and below the wrist to tap against each other, creating a click-clack sound."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "clackers"@en .

aat:300449072 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tracks on which marbles roll from top to bottom, often with switches, levers, loops, jumping jacks, etc."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "marble runs"@en .

aat:300449073 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small disc provided with holes, through which a string runs. The string is wound by rotating the disc. When one pulls the string apart, the disc rotates quickly and makes a buzzing sound."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bull-roarers"@en .

aat:300449074 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small children's velocipedes with two wheels at the back and one at the front. These usually lack brakes or gears and use frontwheel drive via cranked pedals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "children's tricycles"@en .

aat:300449075 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys that challenge children to make something creative, ranging from crafts, handicrafts, stringing beads to making mosaic figures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "creative toys"@en .

aat:300449076 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sets of dimensional letters or images, usually cast in rubber, with which children can make prints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stamp sets"@en .

aat:300449078 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Books with instructions for making crafts, some consist of text, while others are interactive, in which you can cut and color."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "craft books"@en .

aat:300449079 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Paper or cardboard printed with construction drawings to make 3D scale models. The construction drawing contains folding lines and adhesive edges. The model is cut or cut from the cardboard, then folded and finally glued together."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "papercraft models"@en .

aat:300449080 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sewing machines tailored to children, with which textiles can be sewn."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy sewing machines"@en .

aat:300449081 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Games or toys that are played individually or in a group and are intended to transfer mathematical skills."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "math games"@en .

aat:300449082 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Clocks with which children learn to tell the time in a playful way."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy clocks"@en .

aat:300449083 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Games that are played individually or in a group and which are intended to teach or practice a language skill."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "language games"@en .

aat:300449084 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Teaching aids in learning to read consisting of a board with ledges on which the letters of the word that is depicted above the ledge can be placed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "reading boards"@en .

aat:300449085 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Games of skill in which fish and other objects have to be fished out of the 'water'. The fish and objects usually have a metal ring with which they can be caught with a fishing rod with a magnet or hook. The fish and objects each have their own value that determines the player's score."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fishing games"@en .

aat:300449086 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy and miniature versions of musical instruments for children to learn how to make music while playing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy instruments"@en .

aat:300449087 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Posable recreational toy or character dolls that are intended to be dressed in a variety of outfits reflecting contemporary or collector trends. Contemporary fashion dolls are usually made from plastic or vinyl, and are mass produced, though many variations exist. The current versions of this type of doll emerged in the mid-20th century and were based on a German doll called Bild Lilli, the design of which was acquired by American company Mattel, that then marketed it as Barbie. Many imitations and"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fashion dolls"@en .

aat:300449088 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small versions of (high) chairs that are made to accommodate baby dolls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "doll chairs"@en .

aat:300449089 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Miniature versions of stores and shops made to play with dolls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "doll stores"@en .

aat:300449090 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Peg solitaire is a board game for one player involving the movement of pegs or marbles in and out of holes. The standard game uses a board with 37 holes with each hole containing a ball, peg, or pin, except in the middle hole, which is empty, the aim being to remove balls by steps until the board is empty but for the central hole, though there are variations of the method of play, and the size and shape of the board and number of pegs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "peg solitaire"@en .

aat:300449091 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy versions of irons with which children can learn to iron or pretend to be ironing. With older versions it was possible to really iron by placing the iron on a stove or by means of electricity. Often sold in combination with a toy ironing board."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "play irons"@en .

aat:300449092 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy versions of kitchenware including pots, pans, appliances or ovens that are intended for children's play. For toy dinner, tea or coffee sets, see toy tableware."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy kitchenware"@en .

aat:300449093 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys that are intended to reenact a religious ritual. In the Catholic context, for example, altars, monstrances and habits were made for children."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "religious toys"@en .

aat:300449094 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy versions of telephones intended for children's play."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy telephones"@en .

aat:300449095 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys that allow children to mimic household tasks, such as cooking and cleaning."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "household toys"@en .

aat:300449096 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy versions of stoves with for children's play. On older versions, cooking could actually be done on charcoal or by using electricity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy stoves"@en .

aat:300449097 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy versions of dining, tea or coffee sets tailored to children or dolls. Older dinner sets are usually made of porcelain, earthenware or tin.. Modern versions are usually made of plastic."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy tableware"@en .

aat:300449098 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Miniature reproductions of cars (whether or not they exist) that can be played with. Model cars are often classified by scale, but also by brand. Some have become a collector's item and thus some models are also marketed specifically to collectors."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "model cars"@en .

aat:300449099 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Realistic scale models of trains."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "model trains"@en .

aat:300449100 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Train-shaped toys specially made for children's play. For scale models of life-sized trains that run on powered tracks, use 'model trains.'"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy trains"@en .

aat:300449101 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy versions of an intelligent electromechanical machine with an often human or animal appearance or a simple toy figure that looks like a robot."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy robots"@en .

aat:300449102 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Three-dimensional representations of a circus consisting of various parts such as the tent, track, benches, acrobats, clowns, and circus animals, intended for children's play."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy circuses"@en .

aat:300449103 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Castles and fortresses made to the size of toy figures, which are sometimes included."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy castles"@en .

aat:300449104 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Vehicles made for children's play."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "vehicle toys"@en .

aat:300449105 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small vehicles based on full-sized models, often to scale."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "miniature vehicles"@en .

aat:300449106 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Thematic sets of toy figures with accessories."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "figural toy sets"@en .

aat:300449108 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Wooden discs in which figures have been applied in high relief that depict proverbs and sayings. They are mainly used to discuss a disagreement between spouses or to advise children. This custom is mainly found among the Bantu people of Central Africa."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "proverb lids"@en .

aat:300449110 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In architecture, a general term for a part or significant component of a built work that is seen as or considered as distinct within the overall design."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sections"@en .

aat:300449111 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In architecture, a part or component that is set at an angle, off the axis or off center of the primary line, such as in a facade or wing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "offset sections"@en .

aat:300449112 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who revere, pray to, or worship a divinity, being, or power as supernatural, divine, holy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "worshippers"@en .

aat:300449115 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state or quality of having only one part; undivided."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "monopartite"@en .

aat:300449116 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state or quality of having or hving been divided into eight parts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "octopartite"@en .

aat:300449117 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state or quality of having or being divided into two parts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bipartite"@en .

aat:300449118 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state or quality of having or being divided into three parts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tripartite"@en .

aat:300449119 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state or quality of having or being divided into four parts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "quadripartite"@en .

aat:300449120 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state or quality of having or being divided into five parts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "quintipartite"@en .

aat:300449121 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state or quality of having or being divided into six parts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sextipartite"@en .

aat:300449122 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state or quality of having or being divided into seven parts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "septipartite"@en .

aat:300449123 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who improve, repair, or correct the appearance of a visual works, for example in manuscript production, a painting workshop, or photographs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "retouchers"@en .

aat:300449124 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who improve, repair, or correct the appearance of a photograph, negative, or film."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "photographic retouchers"@en .

aat:300449125 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who project the image on a photographic negative onto light-sensitive print paper and chemically develop it into hard copy prints; typically used for people who are using the negatives created by other visual artists."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "photographic finishers"@en .

aat:300449126 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Visual artists who specialize in the medium of pigments incorporated in wax crayons; crayons are often known as a children's medium in current usage."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "crayon artists"@en .

aat:300449127 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who create rapidly executed freehand drawings, usually portraits, for various purposes, including forensic identification, courtroom renderings, and entertainment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sketch artists"@en .

aat:300449128 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The position or condition of being external to a reference point."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "outer"@en .

aat:300449129 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The position or condition of being inside relative to a reference point."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "inner"@en .

aat:300449132 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Notations associated with lines that indicate on a drawing the actual dimensions of whatever is represented in the drawing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "linear dimensioning"@en .

aat:300449133 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Notations indicating on a drawing the elevation of each point indicated by a datum mark."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "elevation dimensioning"@en .

aat:300449134 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Complexs comprising a forum and adjacent basilica, forming the civic administrative center of a Roman community. A typical plan included a rectangular open marketplace surrounded on three sides by rooms, and basilican great hall and with a rear range of rooms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "forum-basilica complexes"@en .

aat:300449135 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In the Catholic church, churches having been bestowed an honorific assigned conferred by papal brief and including a certain precedence before other churches along with the right to display the conopaeum (a baldachin resembling an umbrella)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lesser basilicas"@en .

aat:300449136 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Churches having been bestowed an honorific assigned to the most highly ranked Roman Catholic churches"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "major basilicas"@en .

aat:300449137 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The fact or condition of lacking knowledge or awareness, either generally or about a particular thing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ignorance"@en .

aat:300449139 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Wealth inherited or handed down, and which is deemed to be valuable; examples include great works of art reflective of a given culture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "patrimony"@en .

aat:300449140 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sacred objects or images that are not shaped to resemble a human or animal; rather, the works symbolize the sacred idea or entity, while not being a depiction or visual representation of something."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "aniconic works"@en .

aat:300449141 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Works, usually sacred objects or images, that resemble a human or animal figure, rather than abstract in design. Commonly dedicated to divinities or as memorials; may be executed according to a fixed or conventional type."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "iconic works"@en .

aat:300449142 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ceremonial performances of a purificatory rite by washing, usually with water; may also be an expiatory sacrifice."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lustrations"@en .

aat:300449143 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ceremonies that make a person or thing ritually clean. An example is the ritual cleansing of a woman after childbirth in accordance with Jewish religious laws."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "purifications"@en .

aat:300449144 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Jainism, twenty-four omniscient spiritual teachers who show the way to liberation, having overcome the cycle of incarnation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jinas"@en .

aat:300449145 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state or action of receiving instruction or acquiring knowledge."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "learning"@en .

aat:300449147 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Indian mythology and art, beasts resembling a crocodile or aquatic animal or monster, often with additional composite parts such as a curly foliate tale."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "makaras"@en .

aat:300449148 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Culture and styles of British citizens or residents of either African or African-Caribbean origin or descent. Black British is one of various self-designation entries used in official UK ethnicity classifications."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Black British"@en .

aat:300449149 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Flags that indicate the status of a piece of data within any particular system or database, usually a code appended to a term or record."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "data flags"@en .

aat:300449150 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Descriptive data that indicates usage that is contemporary, as distinguished from usage in past historical contexts, for these use \"historical terms.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "current terms"@en .

aat:300449151 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Descriptive data that indicates usage in past historical contexts, as distinguished from usage that is up to date or contemporaneous to the user, for these use \"current terms.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "historical terms"@en .

aat:300449152 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In a controlled vocabulary hierarchy, an ancestor record that provides a broader context for the record at hand, and that is defined as the primary ancestor record in the event of there being multiple ancestors in a polyhierarchical structure. The status of being the preferred parent may be defined by multiple parameters as indicated by the guidelines of the database or the hierarchy itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "preferred parents"@en .

aat:300449165 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings in which courses of high school or college-level study are provided for candidates for the priesthood, brotherhood, or sisterhood."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "juniorates"@en .

aat:300449166 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Formalized exhibitions that include works made by more than one person. This describes most accurately contemporary exhibitions that are devoted to visual works made by individual creators rather than historical or thematic exhibitions devoted to a broad subject."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "group exhibitions"@en .

aat:300449167 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Formalized exhibitions that include works by a single creator. This term is usually reserved to describe exhibitions of contemporary work by living artists, rather than historical retrospectives."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "solo exhibitions"@en .

aat:300449168 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Center focused on training and performance of dance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dance centers"@en .

aat:300449169 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Writing or painting sets comprised of a shaft that holds a brush and a small box or container at one end containing ink-saturated cotton. These were used as portable writing sets, primarily in medieval Japan, from the Kamakura period."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yatate"@en .

aat:300449170 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An allotropic form of carbon material derived from graphite consisting of sheets one atom thick arranged in a two-dimensional honeycomb lattice. It has exceptionally high tensile strength, electrical conductivity, transparency, and is the thinnest two-dimensional material known."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "graphene"@en .

aat:300449171 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A decorative engraving technique, in which a very precise and repetitive pattern is mechanically engraved into a surface (usually metal) with a rose engine lathe. This lathe produces precise geometric patterns and creates incisions to the surface at an even depth, which maximises light refraction. Guilloché engraving is often enhanced with a thin layer of translucent enamels, which increases the luminous quality of the engraved patterns. This combination is referred to as guilloché enamelling, and became a common decorative feature of Fabergé’s output."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "guilloché"@en .

aat:300449172 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Architectural ornaments, formed of two or more interwoven bands which wind around a series of central points, often left blank. This type of decorative frieze is characteristic of Greek and Roman architecture and borders of Roman mosaics."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "guilloches"@en .

aat:300449173 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Stone that is a composite made of crushed stone bound together by an adhesive such as polymer resin to create a solid surface. Common applications include indoor flooring, walls, and kitchen countertops."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "engineered stone"@en .

aat:300449174 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for stone that is used as derived from a natural source; distinguished from \"engineered stone,\" other composites, or \"artificial stone.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "natural stone"@en .

aat:300449175 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In the context of archives and manuscript collections, archival description encompasses the dual processes of cataloging and the production of finding aids."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "archival description"@en .

aat:300449176 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In archives and special collections description, remediation of practices or data that exclude, silence, harm, or mischaracterize marginalized people in the data created or used by archivists to identify or characterize archival resources."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "reparative description"@en .

aat:300449177 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The activity or process of the using a variety of data sources or methods to perform retrieval or research; examples could be sources for time, space, and actors. The desired result is qualitative research to reveal a comprehensive understanding of the issue at hand. Findings can be corroborated and any weaknesses in one set of data can be compensated for by the strengths of other data, thereby increasing the validity and reliability of the results."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "data triangulation"@en .

aat:300449178 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In India, spirits, demons, goblins, or ghosts of deceased persons."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bhutas"@en .

aat:300449179 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People, animals, or other beings who carry, bring, or transport something, particularly something heavy or cumbersome; may be used in actual or figurative contexts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bearers"@en .

aat:300449180 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Beings who hold up the weight of the world or planet earth; examples are Atlas in Classical mythology and the Diggajas in Hinduism."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "world bearers"@en .

aat:300449181 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Motif of wheels, circles or discs represented in Buddhist art as the wheel of the law; and in Hindu art as a symbol of dharma, the circles of existence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chakras"@en .

aat:300449182 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The sport or game of throwing rings of flattened iron, rope, or rubber at a target; typically throwing or aiming such rings at a peg placed in the ground or at another target."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "quoits"@en .

aat:300449183 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Motif of a mace representing one of the nine symbols for the cosmic principles of the universe, according to the Pāñcarātra literature. These nine weapons and ornaments symbolize the principles which they represent as the presiding deity. The gadā represents Mahat ('cosmic intelligence')."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gadā"@en .

aat:300449185 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Beings that are partly of divine nature, such as when having resulted from the intercourse of a deity and a mortal, when a human is raised to divine rank, or referencing a minor or inferior deity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "demigods"@en .

aat:300449186 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Personal spirits in some West African religious beliefs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ehis"@en .

aat:300449187 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Motif of a phallus, in Hindu art and culture the symbol of the god Shiva."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lingams"@en .

aat:300449188 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Motif of a the female reproductive organ, in Hindu art and culture the symbol of generation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yonis"@en .

aat:300449189 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Images generated using artificial intelligence or deep learning algorithms such as generative adversarial networks and transformers that simulate painting, photography, or other forms of image-making."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "AI art"@en .

aat:300449190 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Open, level spaces of ground that facilitate walking or transport by horses or vehicles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "esplanades"@en .

aat:300449191 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Large clear spaces between a citadel or other fortification and the nearest houses of the town."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "esplanades"@en .

aat:300449193 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographic negative transparencies that are designed to be developed immediately after exposure via an integrated packet of chemistry when pulled from a special film holder or camera that enables the integrated developer chemistry to be squeezed through rollers and onto a light sensitive emulsion. This method of image-forming was developed as a commercial product by the Polaroid corporation, and sold as individual plates that would yield both a positive print and a printable negative transparency, known as Polaroid 55."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "instant film negatives"@en .

aat:300449194 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Original buildings that remain extant on college or university campuses, particularly in the United States. The original buildings generally served multiple purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "old mains"@en .

aat:300449195 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Houses or other buildings that feature a large visible clock on the exterior."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "clockhouses"@en .

aat:300449196 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for specialized instruments used by engineers, architects, or drafters to make lines of constant width for architectural, engineering, or technical drawings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "technical pens"@en .

aat:300449197 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Negatives that depict other works, usually other photographs, that are used to strike copy prints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "copy negatives"@en .

aat:300450669 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family of tonal languages of the Otomi people inhabiting parts of central Mexico."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Otomi"@en .

aat:300450670 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Paper made from thin, flexible sheets obtained from the smooth, white, inner surface of tree bark, including Birch (genus Betula), paper mulberry (Broussonetia papyrifera), Breadfruit (genus Artocarpus), or others. A similar or identical material may be used as a textile as well, called \"bark cloth.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bark paper"@en .

aat:300450671 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to a paper made from a beaten bark material or from the lengua de vaca plant; was made by the Maya."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "huun"@en .

aat:300450672 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Paper made from the boiled and pounded inner bark of a wild fig tree. Most \"amate paper\" is fig bark paper."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fig bark paper"@en .

aat:300450673 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Paper made from the fibers of the hemp plant."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hemp paper"@en .

aat:300450674 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "One of the two principal sects of Jainism, which was formed ca. 80 CE due to a doctrinal schism."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Śvētāmbara"@en .

aat:300450675 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "One of the two principal sects of Jainism; meaning \"sky-clad,\" referring to the male ascetics possessing no clothing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Digambara"@en .

aat:300450676 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Storage media intended for video recordings that are wound and played back from open reels or spools."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "open reel videotapes"@en .

aat:300450677 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Landforms either of rock or earth, that are vertical, standing, often spire-shaped, natural formations, such as a natural chimney, monument, pinnacle, pohaku, or rock tower."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pillars"@en .

aat:300450678 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Areas forming an edge, verge, border, or margin of a feature such as a crater or canyon, chiefly with reference to features having a more or less continuous outer edge or border."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rims"@en .

aat:300450679 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Parks under the jusidiction or co-management of a tribe, a tribal government, or other tribal entity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tribal parks"@en .

aat:300450680 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Geological formations comprising a narrow, residual wall of hard sedimentary rock that remains standing after surrounding rock has been eroded away along parallel joints or fractures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fins"@en .

aat:300450681 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Concentrations of population defined by the United States Census Bureau for statistical purposes only."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "census-designated places"@en .

aat:300451025 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for buildings in which instruction in the various humanities is carried out."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "humanities buildings"@en .

aat:300451026 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Imitation grottoes representing the grotto in Lourdes, France where apparitions of the Virgin Mary were believed to have occurred in 1858."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lourdes grottoes"@en .

aat:300451027 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Residence halls associated with a college or university where students experience immersive language exposure."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "language houses"@en .

aat:300451028 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Area and facilities comprising the terrain and supporting infrastructure where skiing and other snow sports take place."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ski areas"@en .

aat:300451029 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings planned or used for as the primary center or structure in a group or complex of buildings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "main buildings"@en .

aat:300451030 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In a college or university, buildings or spaces where students may gather information and receive services."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "college centers"@en .

aat:300451031 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Establishments with washing machines and dryers for public use, usually with little assistance from the staff; machines are often coin-operated or by phone applications."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "self-service laundries"@en .

aat:300451032 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Built works used for personal or family purposes, in contrast to architecture used for commercial purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "domestic architecture"@en .

aat:300451033 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for built works containing facilities for laundering clothing, linens, and other textiles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "laundry buildings"@en .

aat:300451034 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Inland vessels used in the Netherlands from the seventeenth century to the nineteenth century to transport passengers on the waterways. The barge could be drawn by a horse, but it was also an option to sail. The boat was generally a stretched vessel about fifteen meters long with a falling prow, a tent, and later a wooden structure with windows that gave the passengers shelter."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "draw boats"@en .

aat:300451035 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Persons imprisoned for political activity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "political prisoners"@en .

aat:300451036 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings where printing takes place, usually referencing built works of relatively modest size. For large structures, use \"printing plants.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "printing houses"@en .

aat:300451037 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings or complexes having facilities or housing institutions dedicated to the practice or study physics."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "physics buildings"@en .

aat:300451038 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for buildings in which liberal arts instruction is carried out."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "liberal arts buildings"@en .

aat:300451040 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Avant-garde movement in painting in France from the later 1960s and early 1970s that is characterized by a radically different approach to painted surface and physical support that were uncoupled or, on the contrary, merged."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supports/Surfaces"@en .

aat:300451041 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Catalan avant-garde movement in art and literature, founded in Barcelona in 1948 and active until the early 1950s. It was a counter-movement that wanted to break open the imposed art under the Franco regime. The style is based on Surrealism and Dadaism, but combined with Cubism and contemporary post-war trends, which opened Spanish art to foreign influences and made it the precursor of Spanish informal art and Abstract Expressionism. The movement published a magazine with the same name."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dau al Set"@en .

aat:300451042 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Strategically positioned architecture at the service of national defence. The structures serve the functional purpose of manning weaponry and maintaining military communication and transport routes.The shape of these buildings changes according to the military technology of a period."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "military architecture"@en .

aat:300451043 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The psychological process of experiencing devotional feelings in a private sphere, whether or not encouraged by the church authorities, often with the aid of texts, figurines or images."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "private devotion"@en .

aat:300451044 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Seagoing vessels that are specially built and equipped to carry out hydrographic and other nautical surveys such as observation of currents and winds in coastal waters and on the high seas."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "surveying vessels"@en .

aat:300451048 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ancient Greek temples dedicated to a mother goddess, often to Cybele, Demeter, or Rhea."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "metroons"@en .

aat:300451049 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Deities or other supernatual beings who have one, single nature, who are unitary wholes; typically used to distinguish a unity from a \"duality (deity)\" or \"trinity (deity).\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "unities"@en .

aat:300451050 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Deities or other supernatual beings who embody two natures or traits in one combined entity, such as good and evil, darkness and light, chaos and order. Distinguished from \"unities (deities)\" or \"trinities (deities).\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dualities"@en .

aat:300451051 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Deities or other supernatual beings who embody three natures or beings in one combined entity. Distinguished from \"unities (deities)\" or \"dualities (deities).\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "trinities"@en .

aat:300451052 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Mouldings or casts of objects obtained by pressing some plastic substance around or over it, often layers of tissue paper. This refers especially in Archaeology to impressions or copies of inscriptions, designs, or other low relief objects taken by applying wet paper or other soft material in this way."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "squeezes"@en .

aat:300451053 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In a royal palace, castle, large manor house, or hall house, the largest and central room of the house, often used for receptions and banqueting in the Middle Ages. In the country houses of the 16th and early 17th centuries, the great hall was also used by the family for eating and relaxing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "great halls"@en .

aat:300451054 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In large residences, relatively large, long rooms used by the family for eating and relaxing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "living halls"@en .

aat:300451055 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Constructions, such as bridges or tunnels, that allow animals to pass over or under a roadway."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wildlife crossings"@en .

aat:300451056 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Proprietary storage drives marketed by Iomega from ca. 1994-2003. The system consisted of a drive mechanism and sealed 'superfloppy' magnetic disks originally with 100Mb, then 250Mb, and 750Mb capacities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zip drives (TM)"@en .

aat:300451057 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A proprietary magnetic disk digital storage format marketed by Iomega from ca.1994-2003. The system consisted of a drive mechanism and sealed 'superfloppy' magnetic disks originally with 100Mb, then 250Mb, and 750Mb capacities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zip disks (TM)"@en .

aat:300451058 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Official residences of a dean, who is an appointed head of a division, faculty, college, or school of a university."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "deans' houses"@en .

aat:300451061 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Enclosed concrete rooms for retting flax, as part of a rottery."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "retting tanks"@en .

aat:300451062 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bake ovens having a shape resembling a beehive."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "beehive ovens"@en .

aat:300451063 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ovens or kilns having a beehive-like shape and used for producing coke, a product derived from mineral coal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "beehive ovens"@en .

aat:300451064 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Thermal ovens, usually having heating elements at the top and bottom of the chamber, in which heated air passively circulates; in contrast to \"convection ovens\" that have a fan to force the air to flow throughout the oven cavity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "conventional ovens"@en .

aat:300451065 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Relatively large vessels used for boiling or cooking; examples include wide, metal vessels with side handles, a bail handle, a lid, and sometimes small feet."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stewpots"@en .

aat:300451066 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys or sets that mimic full-sized billiard tables that are made to measure for children or in miniature versions as a table game."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "billiard games"@en .

aat:300451067 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Institutions that display collections focused on peoples, societies, and cultures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ethnographic museums"@en .

aat:300451068 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Churches having a centralized plan that is polygonal in shape."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "polygonal churches"@en .

aat:300451070 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Churches having the form of a tower; examples are the tower churches popular in England and elsewhere in the 10th century."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "round-tower churches"@en .

aat:300451072 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Books having content relating to art; particularly used for books containing printed reproductions of works of art."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "art books"@en .

aat:300451075 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Facilities housing health care professionals in outpatient settings, including medical offices, clinics, ambulatory surgery centers, hospital outpatient departments, and dialysis centers."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ambulatory care facilities"@en .

aat:300451076 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings or other structures used for the housing of or caring for animals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "animal husbandry structures"@en .

aat:300451077 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Construction technique whereby architecture or other works, particularly works of wood, are manufactured using skilled joinry or other methods, but without the use of nails, glue, or other such materials."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "nailless"@en .

aat:300451078 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Trenches in which wood or hot coals are used to cook meat or root vegetables; may contain an apparatus for holding the food; may be buried below ground."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "barbeque pits"@en .

aat:300451080 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Indian architectural contrexts, relatively small reservoirs or tanks in which rainwater is collected for drinking."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kunds"@en .

aat:300451081 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Structures used for the storage and maintenance of official records."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "halls of records"@en .

aat:300451082 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Indian architecture, the shrine where a figure of the deity resides."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "srikovils"@en .

aat:300451083 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Identifying codes for world languages or language families, living and extinct, maintained by the International Organization for Standardization."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ISO language codes"@en .

aat:300451085 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Series of spoken word digital audio files that a user can download to a personal device for easy listening. These are usually arranged in chronological or thematic groups, the entire collection being the series. For subgroups of podcasts within the series, use \"podcast seasons\"."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "podcast series"@en .

aat:300451086 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Numbered groups within a series of spoken word digital audio files known as podcasts. These are usually arranged in chronological or thematic seasons. For the entire collection of grouped podcasts under a singular title, use 'podcast series.'"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "podcast seasons"@en .

aat:300451087 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Information advisories in any form on the content of information artifacts such as audio-visual recordings or printed matter that may contain presentations unsuitable for all users. These may appear as labels, printed matter, or preambles within the content itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "explicit content warnings"@en .

aat:300451088 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Content of information artifacts such as audio-visual recordings or printed matter that is unsuitable for all users. This refers most often to sexual or violent subject matter."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "explicit content"@en .

aat:300451089 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Powerful Levantine culture in the Iron Age."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Omride"@en .

aat:300451090 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Standard literary Arabic used across the Middle East, North Africa, and Horn of Africa; one of the six official languages of the United Nations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Modern Standard Arabic"@en .

aat:300451091 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rooms, buildings, or groups of buildings where tests and studies are done in the area of medicine or on specimens from patients at medical facilities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "medical laboratories"@en .

aat:300451092 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Theaters located at a college or university; usually affiliated with the curriculum."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "university theaters"@en .

aat:300451312 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Prints that are embellished with metallic leaf, spangles, or pieces of fabric. Examples are known since the 15th century, but the most common examples are 19th century popular plain or hand-colored prints representing theatrical figures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tinsel prints"@en .

aat:300451313 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Prints into which embellishments have been introduced by cutting portions of the original prints out to accomodate the insertion of textiles, affixed by glue or other means, usually to contribute to the depiction of costume. Dressed prints of the late 17th and early 18th century usually depict full-length figures, often of named individuals, and were the work of dedicated amateurs. The practice re-emerged in early 19th century England. For later 19th century prints often of theatrical figures embellished with metallic foil and sometimes textiles, use 'tinsel prints.'"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dressed prints"@en .

aat:300451314 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Structures or parts of a structure in a monastery containing the private room or rooms of the abbot."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "abbot's quarters"@en .

aat:300451315 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Building projects intended to improve urban environments; may include projects to renovate or replace deteriorated buildings, streets, and utilities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "urban renewal projects"@en .

aat:300451316 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A variety of Arabic spoken in the Hejaz region in Saudi Arabia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arabic, Hijazi"@en .

aat:300451317 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General family name for Arabic types spoken across the Arabian Peninsula."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arabic, Arabian Peninsula"@en .

aat:300451319 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Text, image, diagrams, etc. drawn from a book, presented as an example of the larger work, but that does not present the totality of the work itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "book sample content"@en .

aat:300451320 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The totality of text, image, diagrams, etc. drawn from a book, collected in any form, usually as a digital surrogate or collection of files that represent the complete work."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "book full content"@en .

aat:300451321 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Printed periodicals with or without text in which the essential information is conveyed through a collection of photographic images. These may be authored by one or more artists or photographers, or arranged by an editor. Generally the images in a photo zine are meant to be viewed in context, as parts of a larger whole. These often are produced in inexpensive formats, usually with small circulation. For similar singular publications bound as books, use 'photobooks.'"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "photo zines"@en .

aat:300451322 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In botany, a stamen is the male or fertilizing organ of a flowering plant, consisting of two parts, the anther, which is a double-celled sac containing the pollen, and the filament, a slender footstalk supporting the anther."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stamens"@en .

aat:300451323 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Remainders of plants or parts of plants, regardless of the stage of decomposition or preservation, including fossils."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "vegetal remains"@en .

aat:300451324 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Remainders of animals, regardless of the stage of decomposition or preservation, including fossils."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "animal remains"@en .

aat:300451325 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Beggar's bowls of Sufi mystics; kashkuls are containers carried by wandering Sufi dervishes to collect money, food, and other goods. Often boat-shaped."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kashkuls"@en .

aat:300451326 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Structures containing the seeds of a flowering plant."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pericarps"@en .

aat:300451327 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hard or woody pericarps enclosing the kernel of a nut."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "nutshells"@en .

aat:300451328 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Material comprising the hard or woody pericarp enclosing the kernel of a nut."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "nutshell"@en .

aat:300451329 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Material comprising or made from the flowering plant structures containing the seeds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pericarp"@en .

aat:300451330 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The ovoid, woody shell found beneath the fibrous husk of the fruit of the coconut palm tree."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "coconut shell"@en .

aat:300451331 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hard shell-like seed pod of the native palm of the Seychelles Islands in the Indian Ocean."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "coco de mer seed pod"@en .

aat:300451332 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Versions of scholarly or scientific documents that precede formal peer review or publication, but that may be distributed for assessment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "preprints"@en .

aat:300451333 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Accepted papers produced prior to a meeting or conference for distribution at the meeting, either in printed or electronic form. These may be considered pre-peer review and are not ready for publication."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "conference preprints"@en .

aat:300451334 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Versions of scholarly or scientific documents or manuscripts that have been peer reviewed, revised and accepted for publication, but that have not been formatted for final printing, presentation, or distribution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "postprints"@en .

aat:300451335 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Accepted versions of papers that are sometimes peer-reviewed, and that are presented in conference proceedings, distributed to conference participants or made publicly available elsewhere."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "conference postprints"@en .

aat:300451336 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A genus of palm having one species."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lodoicea"@en .

aat:300451337 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of palm endemic to the islands of Praslin and Curieuse in the Seychelles. Noted for having the biggest seed in any living plant species."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lodoicea maldivica"@en .

aat:300451339 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A coarse, hard-wearing cotton fabric made from colored yarn, often blue in color, and typically used to make work clothes. Later, a heavy cotton fabric similar to this, particularly North American blue denim."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dungaree"@en .

aat:300451340 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of cotton or mixed-fiber fabric typically having a checked or striped pattern woven from contrasting white and coloured yarns."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gingham"@en .

aat:300451341 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Cloth exported from India as a product that could be traded by European merchants in exchange for African goods. Broadly covers a wide range of cheap, brightly colored fabrics, often striped, checkered, or plain."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Guinea cloth"@en .

aat:300451342 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A kind of cotton textile formerly imported from India commonly white with narrower blue stripes, often of coarse or medium quality, patterned on the loom."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "nicanees"@en .

aat:300451343 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Durable woolen twill fabic made in England during the 17th and 18th centuries, composed of mixed combed and carded wool."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "perpetua"@en .

aat:300451344 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of fine white linen made first in Silesia, then in Hamburg, Flanders, and Germany."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "platilla"@en .

aat:300451346 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographs of a person's face or head and shoulders; most specifically portrait photographs used to identify actors, models, or other professionals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "headshots"@en .

aat:300451347 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Names that a person was given at birth and no longer uses, particularly in referencing transgender individuals who have transitioned."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "deadnames"@en .

aat:300451348 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Professional fabricators participate in producing a finished product; they are responsible for a step or process, for the creation of parts, or for assembling parts into a finished product. In the creation of artworks, fabricators are artisans or other people involved with producing large or technically difficult artworks, where resources are required beyond an individual artist's to produce the work."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fabricators"@en .

aat:300451349 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The process or activity of producing large or technically difficult artworks, where resources are required beyond an individual artist's to produce the work; typically requiring professional fabricators to participate in making the finished product."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "art fabrication"@en .

aat:300451350 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Solid wooden boards that are struck with a hammer or other implement to produce sounds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sounding boards"@en .

aat:300451351 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Heavy solid boards of wood used in Buddhist monasteries, struck with a mallet or heavy knotted rope to produce sound. Often inscribed; may be hung or suspended near a door or hall."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hans"@en .

aat:300451352 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Constructions containing the stairs, supporting framework, balustrade, well opening, adjacent walls, and other components for a flight or series of flights of stairs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "staircases"@en .

aat:300451353 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Areas or constructions comprising one or more flights of stairs, with the necessary landings and connecting platforms to form a continuous and uninterrupted passage from one level to another, as defined by the International Code Council."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stairways"@en .

aat:300451354 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for buildings that house medical offices or facilities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "medical buildings"@en .

aat:300451355 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Process of creating mosaics using tiny pebbles set over three foundation layers: the bottom layer of large pebbles set on packed soil, second, of stone and gravel, crushed terracotta and Santorini powder from lava, and the top being a mixture of brick powder and fine sand that sets the pebbles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "opus lapilli"@en .

aat:300451356 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Architectural towers designed to allow a through flow of fresh air or to divert polluted air outdoors. They may employ wind turbines or use wind-induced natural ventilation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ventilation towers"@en .

aat:300451357 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Structures or parts of structures equipped for taking X-rays for medical diagnosis or other purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "X-ray facilities"@en .

aat:300451358 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Roman and other ancient structures, the rooms or buildings designed or used for meals and banquets."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cenatoriums"@en .

aat:300451359 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In philosophical and religious traditions, the concept of or belief in the continued existence of a material thing into infinity, or of the existance of an immaterial soul or mind beyond the physical death of the body."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "immortality"@en .

aat:300451360 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Diantang, a building type sometimes referred to as dian 殿 or even diange 殿閣, is arguably the highest ranking structure in the traditional Chinese building hierarchy. Meaning, literally, \"palatial-type\" or \"rear\" (dian 殿 Kroll 2015, 86) \"hall\" (tang 堂 Kroll 2015, 444), in historical sources diantang is likely a compound word inclusive of dian and tang-type structures, and can be used generally to refer to all main buildings in an architectural complex. When describing a single building it denotes a higher rank structure than ting 廳, 堂 tang, or tingtang 廳堂. Diantang is usually used to describe imperial buildings and the main buildings in religious complexes. The earliest use we have found for diantang is in the \"Rhapsody of the Goddess\" (Shennüfu 神女賦) traditionally attributed to Song Yu 宋玉 (298-263 BCE), but the compound became more common after the Northern and Southern Dynasties. Diantang is a major term used throughout the Song Dynasty Yingzao fashi building manual. Diantang, a building type sometimes referred to as dian 殿 or even diange 殿閣, is arguably the highest ranking structure in the traditional Chinese building hierarchy. Meaning, literally, “palatial-type” or \"rear\" (dian 殿 Kroll 2015, 86) \"hall\" (tang 堂 Kroll 2015, 444), in historical sources diantang is likely a compound word inclusive of dian and tang-type structures, and can be used generally to refer to all main buildings in an architectural complex. When describing a single building it denotes a higher rank structure than ting 廳, 堂 tang, or tingtang 廳堂. Diantang is usually used to describe imperial buildings and the main buildings in religious complexes. The earliest use we have found for diantang is in the \"Rhapsody of the Goddess\" (Shennüfu 神女賦) traditionally attributed to Song Yu 宋玉 (298-263 BCE), but the compound became more common after the Northern and Southern Dynasties. Diantang is a major term used throughout the Song Dynasty Yingzao fashi building manual."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "diàntáng"@en .

aat:300451364 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Mamian 馬面, literally \"horse face,\" is a defensive structure within a traditional Chinese city wall. The mamian is prominent on the outer surface of the wall and can be taller than or equal to the height of the city wall. According to literature and archeological evidence, most mamian are solid masonry. It is named for its narrow façade and long shape like a horse head. Its main function was to increase the defensive capacity of the city wall by eliminating blind corners, which were vulnerable to attacks from the side. Mamian also played a role in strengthening the city wall. Mamian were clearly defined in texts as early as the Song dynasty and remained in use through the Qing dynasty."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mǎmiàn"@en .

aat:300451366 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In traditional Chinese architecture, zhuchu (literally the stone plinth or foundation (chu 礎) of a pillar (zhu 柱) (Kroll 2015, 58; Hanyu dacidian online)) generally refers to the stone that supports a pillar. It consists of an upper part aboveground which supports the pillar foot (zhujiao 柱脚) and a lower part buried underground, the top of which is generally level with the surface of the building platform. The style and material of pillar bases differs according to time and region. In early human settlements, there is evidence that wooden planks or wooden blocks were used as pillar bases. In the southern part of China, two separate parts work together to support the pillar. The upper one is called gudeng 鼓磴 (lit. “drum step”), the lower one is sangshi 磉石 (lit. “pillar base stone”), both are made of stone. The stone “drums” of traditional architecture in the southern part of China and the stone blocks supporting the stone drums jointly assume the role of pillar bases. The pillar base can effectively prevent the decay of the wooden pillar foot by raising it off the wet ground. Furthermore, by stopping the absorption of moisture around the pillar foot, it helps prevent the shrinkage of the wood (ATTCAT 2019). 9"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "zhùchǔ"@en .

aat:300451368 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Yixinggong 翼形栱, which cannot be found in Chinese literature until 1930s, is a term coined by Liang Sicheng that combines yixing 翼形 (literally, wing-shaped (Hanyu dacidian, online; Kroll 2015, 551) and gong 栱 to refer to the wing-shaped brackets in Tang, Liao, and Song architecture. In terms of shape, yixinggong 翼形栱 is based on actual examples which were referred to as yixinggong 翼形栱 by Liang Sicheng. In terms of function, the two ends of yixinggong 翼形栱 do not support a bearing block and would therefore be considered more decorative than structural. With the publication of Liang Sicheng's investigation reports on Song, Liao, and Jin architecture in the Journal of the Society for the Study of Chinese Architecture (Zhongguo Yingzao xueshe huikan 中國營造學社彙刊) between 1932 and 1935, the term yixinggong 翼形栱 was used repeatedly and it gradually became a term with a much clearer definition which was adopted by other scholars. It is important to note that the consensus reached by later architectural historians about the meaning of yixinggong differs from Liang Sicheng's use of the term. The discrepancy mainly lies in different understandings of what is meant by “wing-shaped” (yixing). Prior to 1979, the term yixinggong 翼形栱 (“wing-shaped bracket arm”) was applied to all irregularly-shaped brackets. Now some scholars also use yixinggong 異形栱 (“irregularly shaped bracket arm”) instead; see yixinggong 異形栱."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yìxínggǒng"@en .

aat:300451369 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Yixinggong 異形栱 literally means “irregularly-shaped bracket arm,” and are different from regular arch-shaped bracket arms (gong 栱), the ends of which support small bearing blocks (dou 斗), as defined in the Yingzao fashi. Homophonous with “wing-shaped bracket arms” (yixinggong 翼形栱), the use of irregular (異 yi) instead of wing (翼 yi) was first seen in 1955 and became more widespread after 1978, but usage was not consistent in all sources. The two forms of “yi” were sometimes used interchangeably, but “irregular” 異 was also used to as a means to differentiate a variety of irregularly shaped gong (rhomboidal, cloud, etc.) in buildings constructed after the Yuan dynasty from those bracket arms with an actual wing-like shape. Generally speaking, today scholars use this term to refer to all irregularly-shaped bracket arms, wing-shaped or otherwise, where the two ends do not support any block (dou 枓)(張 1979；南京工學院建築系 1987；郭 2003). Yixinggong 異形栱 literally means “irregularly-shaped bracket arm,” and are different from regular arch-shaped bracket arms (gong 栱), the ends of which support small bearing blocks (dou 斗), as defined in the Yingzao fashi. Homophonous with “wing-shaped bracket arms” (yixinggong 翼形栱), the use of irregular (異 yi) instead of wing (翼 yi) was first seen in 1955 and became more widespread after 1978, but usage was not consistent in all sources. The two forms of “yi” were sometimes used interchangeably, but “irregular” 異 was also used to as a means to differentiate a variety of irregularly shaped gong (rhomboidal, cloud, etc.) in buildings constructed after the Yuan dynasty from those bracket arms with an actual wing-like shape. Generally speaking, today scholars use this term to refer to all irregularly-shaped bracket arms, wing-shaped or otherwise, where the two ends do not support any block (dou 枓)(張 1979；南京工學院建築系 1987；郭 2003). Yixinggong 異形栱 literally means “irregularly-shaped bracket arm,” and are different from regular arch-shaped bracket arms (gong 栱), the ends of which support small bearing blocks (dou 斗), as defined in the Yingzao fashi. Homophonous with “wing-shaped bracket arms” (yixinggong 翼形栱), the use of irregular (異 yi) instead of wing (翼 yi) was first seen in 1955 and became more widespread after 1978, but usage was not consistent in all sources. The two forms of “yi” were sometimes used interchangeably, but “irregular” 異 was also used to as a means to differentiate a variety of irregularly shaped gong (rhomboidal, cloud, etc.) in buildings constructed after the Yuan dynasty from those bracket arms with an actual wing-like shape. Generally speaking, today scholars use this term to refer to all irregularly-shaped bracket arms, wing-shaped or otherwise, where the two ends do not support any block (dou 枓)(張 1979；南京工學院建築系 1987；郭 2003)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yìxínggǒng"@en .

aat:300451370 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Yangma wall (yangmacheng 羊馬城) literally meaning “goat and horse fortification,\" first appeared in Tang dynasty records and then also in Song dynasty textual sources. It refers to a wall built either between the city wall and the moat or around a beacon tower. It is usually smaller than the city wall. The yangma wall forms a closed circle around the city wall or the beacon tower, or may be located in certain parts of the city wall to form a closed circle together with the city wall. Yangma wall serves as a defensive line between the city wall and the moat in order to prevent the enemy from invading. It was also used for the temporary placement of goats, sheep, and horses when residents outside the city entered the city for protection. A second term, yangmaqiang 羊馬墻, literally meaning “goat and horse wall\" also survives in Tang and Song records and the two terms are interchangeable in texts until the Qing dynasty. Yangma wall (yangmacheng 羊馬城) literally meaning “goat and horse fortification,\" first appeared in Tang dynasty records and then also in Song dynasty textual sources. It refers to a wall built either between the city wall and the moat or around a beacon tower. It is usually smaller than the city wall. The yangma wall forms a closed circle around the city wall or the beacon tower, or may be located in certain parts of the city wall to form a closed circle together with the city wall. Yangma wall serves as a defensive line between the city wall and the moat in order to prevent the enemy from invading. It was also used for the temporary placement of goats, sheep, and horses when residents outside the city entered the city for protection. A second term, yangmaqiang 羊馬墻, literally meaning “goat and horse wall\" also survives in Tang and Song records and the two terms are interchangeable in texts until the Qing dynasty."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yángmǎchéng"@en .

aat:300451371 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Si'e (literally “fourfold” “slopes” Kroll 2015, 429, 98) is a roof type usually translated “hip roof” in a literal translation of the specific roof type in English. Until the Tang and Song Dynasties, it was generally believed that “e 阿” referred to the roof eaves, and that \"si`e \" (four eaves) therefore meant the eaves on all four sides of a building. In the Yingzao fashi 營造法式 (1103), the si`e hall（si’e diange 四阿殿閣) is also called \"five-ridge hall”（wuji dian 五脊殿) as well as \"wudian” 吳殿) , referring to a roof with a central ridge, and four sloping ridges where the surfaces of the roof slopes meet. According to references in ancient texts, the term si`e 四阿 first appeared in the Spring and Autumn Period and was used until the Qing Dynasty. However, in records before the Tang Dynasty, it is difficult to understand the precise meaning of the term. In the Qing Dynasty, this kind of roof was generally called wudianding 廡殿頂. Si'e (literally “fourfold” “slopes” Kroll 2015, 429, 98) is a roof type usually translated “hip roof” in a literal translation of the specific roof type in English. Until the Tang and Song Dynasties, it was generally believed that “e 阿” referred to the roof eaves, and that \"si`e \" (four eaves) therefore meant the eaves on all four sides of a building. In the Yingzao fashi 營造法式 (1103), the si`e hall（si’e diange 四阿殿閣) is also called \"five-ridge hall”（wuji dian 五脊殿) as well as \"wudian” 吳殿) , referring to a roof with a central ridge, and four sloping ridges where the surfaces of the roof slopes meet. According to references in ancient texts, the term si`e 四阿 first appeared in the Spring and Autumn Period and was used until the Qing Dynasty. However, in records before the Tang Dynasty, it is difficult to understand the precise meaning of the term. In the Qing Dynasty, this kind of roof was generally called wudianding 廡殿頂."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sì'ēdǐng"@en .

aat:300451372 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Wudianding 廡殿頂 (also wudian 廡殿), literally the roof of a royal hall (Kroll 2015, 481, 86), is usually translated as a “hip roof”. It is a type of roof which consists of a central ridge, four sloped ridges, and four sloped surfaces, and is typically covered in ceramic roof tiles. This form of roof was mostly used for palace or other large-scale buildings. The term wudian is first seen in documents of the Qing dynasty. In the Tang and Song dynasties, this roof type was called si`e 四阿. According to the Yingzao fashi 營造法式, \"si`e palatial halls\"（si’e diange 四阿殿閣）can also be called \"five-ridge halls\" (wujidian 五脊殿) as well as \"Wu halls\" (Wudian 吳殿). The Yingzao fayuan 營造法原, which discusses the architectural practices of the Jiangnan region during the Yuan and Ming Dynasties, used the term \"siheshe\" 四合舍 for this kind of roof (姚 et al. 1986, 37). Wudianding 廡殿頂 (also wudian 廡殿), literally the roof of a royal hall (Kroll 2015, 481, 86), is usually translated as a “hip roof\". It is a type of roof which consists of a central ridge, four sloped ridges, and four sloped surfaces, and is typically covered in ceramic roof tiles. This form of roof was mostly used for palace or other large-scale buildings. The term wudian is first seen in documents of the Qing dynasty. In the Tang and Song dynasties, this roof type was called si`e 四阿. According to the Yingzao fashi 營造法式, \"si`e palatial halls\"（si’e diange 四阿殿閣）can also be called \"five-ridge halls\" (wujidian 五脊殿) as well as \"Wu halls\" (Wudian 吳殿). The Yingzao fayuan 營造法原, which discusses the architectural practices of the Jiangnan region during the Yuan and Ming Dynasties, used the term \"siheshe\" 四合舍 for this kind of roof (姚 et al. 1986, 37)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wǔdiàndǐng"@en .

aat:300451373 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Yámù are one of the many burial forms in traditional China. They are excavated from the hillsides and cliffs near rivers and are generally located within the Sichuan Basin in southwest China, including in Sichuan, Chongqing, and Guizhou provinces. Yámù usually contain a tomb passage, a tomb door, a front chamber and one or more side halls and burial chambers. Some yámù are furnished with carved stone coffins, stoves, and niches, which are architecturally integrated into the caves. Some tombs also have architectural elements resembling timber buildings and walls decorated with carved images. The burial objects are largely the same as those found in brick or stone chamber tombs from the same period (Luo 1988, Fan 2006). However, the distinction between these cliff tombs and cliff burials in more central and eastern areas of China has not been given enough academic attention."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yámù"@en .

aat:300451376 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Usually denoting a towering structure containing objects associated with the Buddha or his teachings, the underlying concept behind the Chinese character ta 塔, as well as the etymology of the term, are still debated. Our earliest textual sources suggest that ta was the abbreviation of a transliterated “foreign term” (“barbarian speech” 胡言, “outer states speech” 外國言, “Sanskrit phrase” 梵之稱), likely something akin to “buddha,” into Chinese characters. As an architectural form, the ta is a structure organized around a vertical axis and/or spire."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tǎ"@en .

aat:300451377 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tingtang is a building type of lower rank than diantang 殿堂 (dian 殿 or diange 殿閣) in the traditional Chinese building hierarchy. Ting itself can refer to a reception hall in government and domestic building compounds (Kroll 2015, 454). Tingtang is a combination of, or general term for, ting and tang and is one of the most important terms in the Song Dynasty Yingzao fashi. Tingtang was also widely used for main buildings and could describe buildings in houses and gardens as well as ancestral shrines."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tīngtáng"@en .

aat:300451384 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Literally meaning \"bay-dividing plank partition\" (Kroll 2016), scholars generally agree that jiejian banzhang 截間版帳 is a room dividing partition, and some scholars believe that it is a partition specifically running perpendicular to the main roof ridge (Zhao 2005, 102). The term is only found in Yingzao fashi 營造法式 (1103; Li 2009, 6.6a), and is considered to be a type of xiaomuzuo 小木作 (fine carpentry or joinery). According to the description in the Yingzao fashi, jiejian banzhang is six to ten feet high and is composed of planks or boards (ban 版) and covering strips (yatou hufeng 牙頭護縫), which can be used on both sides of the board. These components are fixed by a board casing (nanzi 難子) between the columns. If the jiejian banzhang is more than seven feet high, column-top-ties (e’fu 額栿) along with posts (tuanzhu 摶柱), and a waist bar (yaochuan 腰串) should be added to the framework. If its width is wider (or deeper) than usual, a bay muntin (qianzhu 槏柱) should also be added."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jiéjiān bǎnzhàng"@en .

aat:300451386 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning literally “well” “structure” (Kroll 2015, 218; 128) Jinggan originally refers to the wall of a wooden well or the railing of a well, but also indicates a construction technique, known as “jinggan method” (jinggan shi 井幹式). According to textual descriptions, archaeological discoveries, and surviving images, the jinggan method involved stacking layers of timbers to form the wall of a building, in which the ends of the timbers were fit together with mortise and tenon joinery. The jinggan construction method is still being used in some areas of northeast and southwest China that are rich in timber. The first appearance of the term jinggan 井幹 is in Zhuangzi’s 莊子 “Autumn Floods” (Qiushui 秋水) from the Warring States Period. In this text, a frog jumps into a “jinggan,” which likely refers to the wall of a wooden well (jingbi 井壁) or the railing of a well (jinglan 井欄). One of the most famous references to the jinggan method describes the architecture of the Jinggan Pavilion (Jinggan lou 井幹樓) in Jianzhang Palace (Jianzhang gong 建章宮) of the Han dynasty."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jǐnggàn"@en .

aat:300451387 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Artificially dwarfed trees and shrubs that are grown in containers and pruned so as to resemble mature, full-sized plants."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bonsai"@en .

aat:300451388 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Plants and their parts having a fleshy and juicy substance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "succulent plants"@en .

aat:300451389 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Benches with raised iron pins in the form of a comb, so that one or two people can manually remove seed bulbs from flax or hemp stems while seated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ripple banks"@en .

aat:300451390 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pulled or self-propelled device for pulling flax out of the ground and depositing it mechanically."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pulling machines"@en .

aat:300451391 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lead sealing that can be attached to objects (for example trade goods) as a quality control measure."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sealing lead"@en .

aat:300451393 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand tool for pressing seals of lead, wax, or other materials."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sealing tongs"@en .

aat:300451394 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Flat frames or shallow boxes used for transporting materials over short distances. These are generally dependent on two people to move."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hand barrows"@en .

aat:300451395 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Electrically or mechanically driven device for tying stems such as flax into bundles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "binders"@en .

aat:300451396 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand tools for raking flax waste, used in an indoor context, such as a shed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "flax rakes"@en .

aat:300451397 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Devices with receptacles and distribution mechanisms for sowing seed manually over a field. These consist of a sowing tray, in metal or linen, suspended over the shoulder by one or more straps. Through an adjustable opening, the seed would pour onto a spreading disc that was made to rotate. With each movement, the seed was thrown wide before of the sower."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "seed fiddles"@en .

aat:300451398 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Machines or apparatuses for scutching flax, cotton, etc. These may be of varied design."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "scutching machines"@en .

aat:300451399 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Externally driven machines with drums for scutching materials such as flax and hemp."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "turbine scutchers"@en .

aat:300451401 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys for small children where multiple activities are included in one toy. Each activity helps develop motor skills and hand-eye coordination. Examples of activities are a bead maze, shape sorter, musical toys, and abacus. Activity toys can take multiple forms. Modern versions may be electronic."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "activity toys"@en .

aat:300451402 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Push toys, or walkers, are used to help children to develop balance and steering. These may have two or more wheels, and come in many forms, usually a base with wheels attached to an upright handle for grasping and pushing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "push toys"@en .

aat:300451403 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A baby floor mat, also called a playmat, play gym, or an activity mat is a brightly colored, interactive mat for a baby or toddler. They come in a variety of sizes, shapes, and themes, but almost all have a few small toys, such as mirrors, rattles, jingles, or crinkle toys."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "baby play mats"@en .

aat:300451404 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys in which three-dimensional geometric figures must be sorted into holes in a cube or sphere with the same shape dimensions. The object can also take other forms.The toddlers manipulate the shapes to correctly fit into their holes and then once the container fills, they dump them back out again."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "shape sorters"@en .

aat:300451405 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two or more public libraries cooperating in a system that is approved by a dedicated commission to provide library service and resources accessible to all residents of the designated area."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "library systems"@en .

aat:300451406 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two or more public schools cooperating in a system that is under the administration of an executive officer and approved by a dedicated commission to provide schools and education to all residents of the designated area."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "school systems"@en .

aat:300451407 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two or more affiliated universities and colleges that are cooperating in a system, usually geographically distributed and typically supported by an individual state, territory, or federal district."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "university systems"@en .

aat:300451408 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Time time measured by Earth’s rotation relative to the Sun."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "solar time"@en .

aat:300451409 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Time that is measured by direct observation of the Sun, by a sundial, astrolabe, or directly by another device. It differs from \"mean solar time,\" which is keyed to time zones and kept by most clocks and watches. Apparent or true solar time is particular to the very site at which it is observed. Thus it remains distinctive and critical for various reasons, including the observation of Islamic rituals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "apparent solar time"@en .

aat:300451410 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Time that is kept by most clocks and watches and keyed to timezones; that is, the solar time that would be measured by observation if the Sun traveled at a uniform apparent speed throughout the year rather than, as it actually does, at a slightly varying apparent speed that depends on the seasons."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mean solar time"@en .

aat:300451411 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Artistic movement that flourished in Mexico in the early 1920s, following the Mexican Revolution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mexican Modernist"@en .

aat:300451412 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Artists identified as the creator, performer, or originator of a work. Typically based on the attribution to an individual based on the style or other characteristics of their works. The person may be named or anonymous, known only by the attribution to their oeuvre of works. May also reference writers, composers, actors, or musicians."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hands"@en .

aat:300451413 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Scribes, composers, or other writers whose works may be identified by the distinctive style of their handwriting or calligraphy. May be anonymous or named."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "scribal hands"@en .

aat:300451414 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In archaeology and related disciplines, the location where an object, artifact, or another thing or things were found."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "find-place"@en .

aat:300451415 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing performing arts enterprises that teach dance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dance schools"@en .

aat:300451416 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Institutions or companies that teach dance in a school setting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dance schools"@en .

aat:300451417 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People of great wealth and typically of great influence and prestige in society. Those whose assets are worth at least a million pounds, Euros, dollars, or another roughly equivalent currency."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "millionaires"@en .

aat:300451418 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Those whose assets are worth at least a billion (a thousand million) pounds, Euros, or dollars, or another roughly equivalent currency. People of great wealth and typically of great influence and prestige in society."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "billionaires"@en .

aat:300451419 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Healthcare facilities in the historic Islamic world, committed to serving all people regardless of their race, religion, citizenship, or gender. Generally included a pharmacy, kitchen, lecture hall, library, and mosque."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bimaristans"@en .

aat:300451420 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of velocipede generally intended for children, the body of which appears in the shape of a horse's body. Tricycle horses appeared in the mid 19th century and wheels of early examples were usually made of wood but during the 1860s these were superseded by metal spokes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tricycle horses"@en .

aat:300451421 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Strings that are tied in a loop and that may be colored, intended to be used to make string figures or to play the game of cat's cradle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "finger strings"@en .

aat:300451422 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Shooting toys, originally consisting of a tube made from hollowed elder branches or wood used to shoot elderberries, wads of paper, or cork powered by a sudden exhalation of breath. PVC, copper, or other materials may be used for the tube. For weapons using the same method of propulsion, use 'blowpipes.'"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "peashooters"@en .

aat:300451423 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys that are intended for outdoor play."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "outdoor toys"@en .

aat:300451424 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Game sets in which economics, trading, business and making money are central. Many of these games use dice to determine movement of players across a board, such as in the game of Monopoly."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "business and trading games"@en .

aat:300451425 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Games that require players to solve or navigate a challenge to progress to another more difficult challenge. These are usually single-player games, but may involve multiple players depending on the game type."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "puzzle games"@en .

aat:300451426 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Board games where the objective is to move game pieces from a start to a finish point. These may have linear tracks or other designs that form a guide for the advance the player's pieces."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "race games"@en .

aat:300451427 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Game sets in which players enter or move pieces upon a two-dimensional board with the aim of getting them into a specified pattern, configuration or spatial position."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "space games"@en .

aat:300451428 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Game sets in which game pieces are moved and game pieces are usually captured from the opponent."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "displace and capture games"@en .

aat:300451429 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A game played with a collection of small rods or strips, which may be made from a variety of materials, and that players try to remove one at a time from a thrown heap, without disturbing other pieces."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jackstraws"@en .

aat:300451430 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sets of pieces gathered together for the game of jackstraws, in which small strips or rods of material are removed from a pile."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jackstraws"@en .

aat:300451431 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Whistles that emulate the chirping of birds by passing air through a reservoir filled with water."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bird whistles"@en .

aat:300451432 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy and miniature versions of accordions intended for children's play."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy accordions"@en .

aat:300451433 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy and miniature versions versions of pianos. These are constructed with chimes or pieces of metal struck to make sound when the keys are depressed rather than strings, as is the case in conventional piano instruments."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy pianos"@en .

aat:300451434 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy and miniature versions versions of drums, scaled for use by children as playthings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy drums"@en .

aat:300451435 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Doll furniture consisting of a cot, a mattress and bed linen. These may be made from a variety of materials, such as wood, metal, reed or plastic."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "doll beds"@en .

aat:300451436 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The style and culture belonging to the Shaybānid dynasty; after moving into Transoxiana, they were called \"Uzbek,\" although that term has broader meaning."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shaybanid"@en .

aat:300451437 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings in which money or other valuables are stored, usually of relatively modest size and used for historical buildings. Examples are found in Classical Greece and Rome."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "treasure houses"@en .

aat:300451438 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Complexes of built works including or focused on a mosque."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mosque complexes"@en .

aat:300451439 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Complexes of strucutures comprising or surrounding a fort or fortification."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fort complexes"@en .

aat:300451440 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Basins used as fixtures for bathing; usually in the context of ancient architecture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bath basins"@en .

aat:300451441 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus of flowering plants containing around 20 species of trees native to India, China, Ceylon to Malaysia, and Sulawesi."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Saraca"@en .

aat:300451442 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of flowering rainforest tree having original distribution original distribution was in the central areas of the Deccan plateau, as well as the middle section of the Western Ghats regions. It is important to the cultural traditions of the Indian subcontinent and adjacent areas."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Saraca asoca"@en .

aat:300451443 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Type of fine-grained, hard, reddish or buff-colored sandstone quarried at Chunar in the Mirzapur District of Uttar Pradesh, and widely used in the architecture of India."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chunar sandstone"@en .

aat:300451445 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Edicts, laws, announcements, or narrative histories, usually issued by a sovereign to his subjects, and carved into rocks for posterity and for display to the public; rock edicts may be carved into cliff rock, onto pillars or stelae, and in caves."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rock edicts"@en .

aat:300451446 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pillars or columns, usually monumental in scale, with inscribed proclamations, laws, or histories that have been issued by a sovereign or other authority to their subjects."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "edict pillars"@en .

aat:300451447 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ancient structures using evaporative cooling systems, employing windcatchers and water brought from nearby springs to produce cold or even freezing conditions for storing food or ice. Developed in ancient Persia; yakhchals had a domed-shape structure above ground and a large subterranean storage space."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yakhchals"@en .

aat:300451448 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pavilions, typically opulent or ornamental one, used as a place of relaxation or shelter for personal use; may be associated with or connected to a palace."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "personal pavilions"@en .

aat:300451449 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Spaces in another building or stand-alone, used for prayer. Examples are the musallā prayer halls in mosques."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "prayer halls"@en .

aat:300451450 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Houses or other buildings for the reception of strangers, typically where lodging and entertainment are provided."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hostelries"@en .

aat:300451451 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Building complexes focused on a mausoleum, but also containing other structures; mausoleums are generally large and ornate."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mausoleum complexes"@en .

aat:300451452 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Persian and Indian architecture, verandas or open halls for the reception of visitors."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dalans"@en .

aat:300451453 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Outer garments intended for women."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "libas amra"@en .

aat:300451454 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bone derived from the bodies of human beings, used as a material."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "human bone"@en .

aat:300451456 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Puzzles, usually made of wood and intended for young children, where cut-out shapes must be placed in a matching hole. Often the puzzle pieces have knobs or pegs that make them easier to grasp."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "peg puzzles"@en .

aat:300451457 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sets of miniature toys that together form a farm. These may include scaled down buildings, equipment or animals, intended as playthings for children."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "farm toys"@en .

aat:300451458 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Puzzles for young children consisting of large puzzle pieces to be assembled on the floor."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "floor puzzles"@en .

aat:300451459 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sets of miniature figures in the shape of cowboys or Native Americans, intended as playthings. These are often sold in sets as a commercial product."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cowboy and Indian figures"@en .

aat:300451460 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two-dimensional figures that can be dressed in corresponding garments, intended for children's play. These are most often made of paper or cardboard, but may be made of other materials, such as vinyl."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dress-up dolls"@en .

aat:300451461 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys intended to be used by children to play in the sand, such as at the seaside. These may include sand mold toys, toy pails, or toy shovels, often in sets."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "beach toys"@en .

aat:300451462 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Operation in which a rough diamond is cut into a faceted stone that can be used in jewelry."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "diamond cutting"@en .

aat:300451463 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The variety of faceted shapes in which diamonds or other transparent gemstones can be cut."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "diamond cuts"@en .

aat:300451464 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Intermediary gem cut between the 8/8 and the brilliant. The crown and pavilion contain 16 facets and the table facet, but only one facet is polished on the rib of the pavilion instead of two."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "16/16 cut"@en .

aat:300451465 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Extremely simplified form of the brilliant cut, used mostly for small diamonds. 8 facets surround the table and 8 facets form the pavilion."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "single cut"@en .

aat:300451479 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing. Toy and miniature versions versions of a trumpet with which children play music while playing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toy trumpets"@en .

aat:300451480 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of rectangular gem cut in which the corners are cut off. Mainly used for emeralds, but can also be used for other gemstones and diamonds. In comparison to a baguette cut, the emerald cut has more horizontally broken facets and also facets in the corners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "emerald cut"@en .

aat:300451481 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The heart cut creates a diamonds or other gems shaped like the traditional symbol of love, an idealized heart. It is a so-called 'fancy cut'."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "heart cut"@en .

aat:300451482 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Type of glass negative where the glass plate is first coated with a mixture of albumin and potassium iodide. This is then dipped in a silver nitrate solution. Albumen glass negatives were not widely used and are difficult to visually distinguish from wet collodion glass negatives."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "albumin negatives"@en .

aat:300451483 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A rose cut fashioned with 24 facets on the top side and a flat bottom side. This particular cut originated in Amsterdam in the 17th century, hence the name. The name, however, is only commonly used from the 18th century. The cut is to be distinguished from an 'Antwerp rose cut'."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Amsterdam rose cut"@en .

aat:300451484 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A rose cut fashioned with 12 facets on the top side and a flat bottom side. This particular cut forms the transition between a six-facet and a full rose. The Antwerp rose originated in Antwerp in the 17th century, hence the name. To be distinguished from an 'Amsterdam rose cut'."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Antwerp rose cut"@en .

aat:300451485 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A gem-cut type that results in the shape of a long, narrow rectangle, bordered by four facets each step cut in the shape of an isosceles trapezoid."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "baguette cut"@en .

aat:300451486 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The most common style of cutting for both diamonds and colored stones. The standard round brilliant consists of a total of 58 facets: 1 table, 8 bezel facets, 8 star facets and 16 upper-girdle facets on the crown; and 8 pavilion facets, 16 lower-girdle facets, and usually a culet on the pavilion, or base."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "brilliant cut"@en .

aat:300451521 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Process of using dried palm fronds to make objects. For example, this is one of the most important and main traditional crafts in Al-Ula city, and is exclusively practiced by women. Numerous tools and products are made out of dried palm fronds to be used in housework, agriculture, and may be used in everyday life in urban zones as well. (Nasif, 1995)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-khoos"@en .

aat:300451523 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Mats woven from palm fronds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-khasfah"@en .

aat:300451525 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of basket woven from palm fronds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-dawwarah"@en .

aat:300451527 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of trough or long narrow open container that holds water or food for animals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-ma‘laf"@en .

aat:300451529 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Containers made of palm fronds to preserve dates."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-meglad"@en .

aat:300451531 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Baskets for preserving dates or grapes or carrying crops."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-muktal"@en .

aat:300451532 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pads woven from palm fronds to be put under a hot pan to protect the surface on which it rests."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-jiwan"@en .

aat:300451534 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small baskets made of palm fronds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-quffah"@en .

aat:300451535 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small tables made of palm fronds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-nafyah"@en .

aat:300451537 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Handles used for carrying waterskins."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-mibhar"@en .

aat:300451539 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand fans made from palm fronds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-mahfah"@en .

aat:300451541 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Brooms made from palm fronds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "al-miknasah"@en .

aat:300451544 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Titles in fielded data that are formatted to sort in a particular way for display in a list or group, usually alphabetically, by the first word after an article, i.e.: \"title, the\"."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sort titles"@en .

aat:300451545 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gem cut in the shape of a square with rounded corners and small facets on the top (table). A gem cut in the shape of a cushion is often compared to the princess cut because of their square appearance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cushion cut"@en .

aat:300451546 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Cut having a boat-shaped profile with pointed ends. A variant of the 57-facet brilliant-cut."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "marquise cut"@en .

aat:300451547 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The pear cut is a combination of a round cut and the marquise cut for gems. The combination of these two cuts is therefore called pear or pearldrop. The pear cut should be cut symmetrically. The rounded corners must be equal to each other and must not have straight facets. With the tip of the pear cut set in a ring pointing towards the nails, the fingers appear longer and narrower, and as a result these shapes are often used on engagement rings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pear cut"@en .

aat:300451548 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The princess cut is a combination of a brilliant-cut variation with a square or, less often, rectangular outline. The design evolved to increase the brilliance of square and rectangular gem cuts by increasing the number of facets on the crown and pavilion. There is no standardized arrangement although the majority have 76 facets."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "princess cut"@en .

aat:300451549 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The radiant cut produces the optical effects of a brilliant cut from a cut-cornered square or rectangular stone gem cut. There are various facet combinations with no set standard. The average number of facets is 70."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "radiant cut"@en .

aat:300451550 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An early style of gem cutting that is thought to have originated in India and to have been brought to Europe by the Venetians. The rose cut is recognizable by its flat bottom and a crown side with triangular facets that come together in a point. It started out as a relatively simple cut, but became more sophisticated over time."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rose cut"@en .

aat:300451551 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A gem or diamond cut style existing as early as the early fifteenth century. The natural octahedron was made flat on both the table and pavilion sides."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "table cut"@en .

aat:300451552 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Nickel plating is a process by which a metal (usually steel) is provided with a nickel layer. This forms a protective layer that protects against rust and other corrosion. There are two methods, a chemical (also called electroless or autocatalytic) nickel plating: where the object is immersed and autocatalytic chemical reactions take place in a bath, and galvanic or electrolytic nickel plating: where the object is hung in a bath and nickel plated by electrolysis, acting as a cathode."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "nickel-plating"@en .

aat:300451553 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A gem or diamond cut style with a squared table, bordered by four long narrow facets that are step cut in the shape of equal isosceles trapezoids. When there are several rows of steps and the corners are chamfered, use emerald cut."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "square cut"@en .

aat:300451554 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An early and simple rose cut, fashioned with 3 facets and a flat bottom side. Often used on very flat stones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "three-facet"@en .

aat:300451555 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A gem cut named for its creator, Vincenzio Peruzzi, a 17th century Venetian lapidary. He applied a brilliant form of cutting, i.e. with 56 facets, a table and a culet. It was seen as an improvement over the earlier Mazarin cut."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peruzzi cut"@en .

aat:300451556 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Chinese porcelain in a famille rose style, painted with ruby ​​red enamel on the underside. It was first manufactured during the reign of Emperor Yongzheng (1723-1735)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ruby-back porcelain"@en .

aat:300451557 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An early rose cut fashioned with six facets and a flat bottom side."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "six-facet"@en .

aat:300451558 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The first known cutting style of a diamond or other gems. Its fashioning involved polishing the facets to mimic the faces of the octahedron, improving the symmetry and transparency of crystals. The exact date of its introduction is not known; however, most sources agree that it emerged near the end of the 14th century."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "point cut"@en .

aat:300451559 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The cubic crystalline form of zirconium dioxide (ZrO2). As a synthetic gem it is often used as an inexpensive replacement for a diamond. Color most often ranges from white to light yellow and brown, but as a synthetic, can be produced in a large variety of colors. Its refractive index and colour dispersion are fairly close to the diamond, but its hardness is lower and its specific gravity much higher."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cubic zirconia"@en .

aat:300451560 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A product that consists of silver powder, organic binder and water. It can be used to model jewelry, which after firing are 99.9% pure silver."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "silver clay"@en .

aat:300451561 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A synthetic gemstone that has a structure same as that of a garnet, but has no chemical counterpart in nature. It has been produced since 1969 in a range of colours, but especially as a colourless stone to simulate a diamond."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yttrium-aluminium-garnet"@en .

aat:300451562 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A vitreous, transparent or translucent mineral that is a silicate of aluminium and sodium containing sodium chloride, usually of a greenish blue color but also grey, yellow, green, or pink, and is often mottled with white veins or patches. It is found in certain igneous rocks. A component of lapis lazuli, it is also used alone as a gemstone."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sodalite"@en .

aat:300451563 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A rare silvery-white hard ductile metallic element of the transition series, atomic number 45; Symbol Rh. Rhodium is resistant to acids, and may occur naturally in pure form, but is normally obtained from nickel ores. It is used especially as a catalyst and in platinum alloys."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rhodium"@en .

aat:300451564 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The calcareous, horny, or shelly plates secreted by some gastropod mollusks, which serve to close the shell when the animal is retracted. These are found in many marine and some terrestrial and freshwater species. Opercula are used in jewelry or as decoration on art and ceremonial objects."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "opercula"@en .

aat:300451565 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A compound of shellac, rosin, brick- and glass dust, often with other additives that is used to hold diamonds or other gemstones in place as they are cut. It softens when heat is applied but has a hard consistency when cooled. The composition is intended to lessen shock waves that might cause a stone to be shattered during a cleaving blow."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cleaver's cement"@en .

aat:300451566 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An agglomerate biotite-peridotite, igneous, or eruptive rock, or ‘blue ground’, which is the matrix of diamond at Kimberley, South Africa and elsewhere."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kimberlite"@en .

aat:300451568 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Culture and style of the Indo-Aryan ethnic group native to the Indian state of Odisha who speak the Odia language."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Odia"@en .

aat:300451569 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Culture or style of the group who speak the Telugu language and are from the Indian areas of Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, and Yanam in Puducherry."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Telugu"@en .

aat:300451570 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Any of various shades of blue used in historical dwellings in the Southern United States, originating in the dwellings of enslaved people on the properties; shades of blue were customarily used in Gullah culture to deter ghosts or other malevolent spirits. The tradition of using blue on the ceilings of porches and other architectural or furnishings elements became common in the South outside of the enslaved people's homes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "haint blue"@en .

aat:300451571 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Culture and style of the ethnic group primarily found in the state of Odisha."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sora"@en .

aat:300451572 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Theatrical entertainments, often plays, in which actors or puppets appear as shadows cast upon a screen placed between the stage and the audience."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "shadow plays"@en .

aat:300451573 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rituals surrounding the exit from a locale or building. May involve burnt offerings, pit offerings, and broken pottery or ornaments. Examples are in pre-Columbian cultures where it was believed that living spirits inhabit inanimate objects, and that damaging or destroying the object can release the spirit."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "abandonment rituals"@en .

aat:300451574 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who assess the characteristics of diamonds such as weight, cut, color, and clarity to be noted on diamond certificates."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "diamond graders"@en .

aat:300451575 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People whose craft it is to cut and polish diamonds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "diamond cutters"@en .

aat:300451576 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People tasked with gauging measures and weights, an exciseman who inspects dutiable bulk goods. The function was established in the Middle Ages and existed until the beginning of the 19th century."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gaugers"@en .

aat:300451577 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who specialize in fixing precious and semi-precious gems into various items of jewelry, accessories and silverware using a range of setting and carving techniques."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "setters"@en .

aat:300451578 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Designs formed by manipulating string on and around the fingers, or other body parts. They may consist of singular images or be created and altered as a game, known as a string game or in sequence as a string story. String figures have also been used for divination, such as to determine the gender of an unborn child. The game of cat's cradle involves the use of string figures. String figures can be pinned down for collecting purposes. For the strings used to create these, use 'finger strings'."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "string figures"@en .

aat:300451598 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Style of Western architecture that invokes or incorporates elements of architecture of non-Western cultures, such as Egyptian, Chinese, Moorish, or others."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Exotic Revival"@en .

aat:300451599 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the architectural style that developed in Russia in the early to mid 19th century. It became the country's official architectural style, commonly seen in ecclesiastical buildings, and combined characteristics from early Russian and Byzantine architecture. A major proponent of this style was Konstantin Ton, who is generally considered to be the movement's founder."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Russo-Byzantine"@en .

aat:300451601 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the architectural style that emerged in Russia in the late 17th century that combined traditional Russian architecture with western European Baroque. Characteristics of this style include tiered, octagonal churches, centralized tower structures, symmetry, and limestone ornamentation. Naryshkin Baroque is named after the Naryshkin family whose estates held many churches of this style."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Naryshkin Baroque"@en .

aat:300451603 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small, stand-alone dwellings, sometimes temporary. They are used as permanent structures for staff or servants, especially in South Africa, New Zealand, and Australia. Also refers to small temporary structures in these places, as built for the unhoused or others who lack permanent housing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wendy houses"@en .

aat:300451604 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photography of (potentially) identifiable individuals, groups of individuals, or animals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "portrait photography"@en .

aat:300451605 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A class of probabilistic, iterative, and generative models that turn noise into a representative data sample used by AI to create high-quality images."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "latent diffusion models"@en .

aat:300451606 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Textual statements used to initiate the creation of a process by a system, especially the creation of images or other content by Artificial Intelligence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "text prompts"@en .

aat:300451607 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing libraries containing paper and electronic books, newspapers and magazines, and multimedia materials such as videos and sound recordings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "multimedia libraries"@en .

aat:300451608 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Broad class of coating applied specifically for decorative purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "decorative coating"@en .

aat:300451609 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tombs that are excavated from hillsides or cliffs. These may contain tomb passages, and sometimes multiple chambers, carved stone coffins and niches, which may be architecturally integrated into the caves."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cliff tombs"@en .

aat:300451613 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Furniture designed for fastening material into groups, such as for tying flax or other plant materials into bundles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "binding tables"@en .

aat:300451615 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Games or toys that are modelled on pinball machines but tailored to children. These may consist of a rectangular sloped box in which balls or marbles are shot up upwards from a sprung lever."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pinball toys"@en .

aat:300451616 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Card games in which players compete to collect four matching cards."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "quartets"@en .

aat:300451617 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Covered structures or interior spaces comprising a long rectangular area, with one short side having the form of a hemispherical apse."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "apsidal halls"@en .

aat:300451618 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Any forms or styles used in the fashioning of gemstones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gemstone cuts"@en .

aat:300451619 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A game in which two players alternately take a loop of string in complex symmetrical figures from each other's fingers."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cat's cradle"@en .

aat:300451620 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Summaries giving details of title deeds and documents that prove an owner's right to dispose of land, together with any encumbrances that relate to the property."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "abstracts of title"@en .

aat:300451621 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Low boot, generally made of leather or synthetic material, covering the ankle. It may have a zipper closure, be laced or simply slipped on. Used to protect the foot from the elements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ankle boots"@en .

aat:300451622 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tools or appliances used to cut the areca nut for preparing betel-chew."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "areca nut cutters"@en .

aat:300451625 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Prosthetics that are created to replace natural joint structures in the body when they become worn out."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "artificial joints"@en .

aat:300451626 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Low-profile platforms on casters that allows a user to recline and slide under a vehicle to perform maintenance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "automotive creepers"@en .

aat:300451627 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hand tools used to pound tree bark, to soften the bast in the processing of bark cloth. These are common to many cultures, and are varied in form. Many Polynesian beaters are four-sided, with three of the sides grooved to better-pound the bast and with the last side flattened to create a smooth final texture for the textile."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bark beaters"@en .

aat:300451628 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Storage containers for areca nut and other ingredients like slaked lime and betel leaves that were used in betel chewing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "betel containers"@en .

aat:300451629 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Dictionaries that provide terminology and definition in two languages."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bilingual dictionaries"@en .

aat:300451630 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Casts of biological specimens used for scientific study."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "specimen casts"@en .

aat:300451631 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Short cylindrical metal tools, with one end with a left-hand spiral flute and the other end with a pentagonal shaped head that can be inserted into a wrench or socket. Used to remove rounded-off or rusted bolts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bolt extractors"@en .

aat:300451633 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Firearm accessories that replace the stock on a semi-automatic firearm. The recoil from the weapon in addition to a springing mechanism to generates motion that allows the rifle to bump the trigger against a shooter's finger, enabling rapid firing without the shooter continuously pulling the trigger."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bump stocks"@en .

aat:300451634 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Handheld objects of generally round shape made of clustered fibrous or metallic material, used by cheerleaders in their routines."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cheerleading poms"@en .

aat:300451635 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Devices used to cut into large round cheeses, often consisting of a revolving base and an attached cutting arm."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cheese wheel cutters"@en .

aat:300451636 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Forks with several long metal tines, slightly curved and pointed, fixed to a crossbar and attached to a wood or metal handle, generally with a hand grip. Used to fill bags of coal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "coal forks"@en .

aat:300451637 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Culture or style of an Anatolian people contemporary with the Hittites, known from hieroglyphic and cuneiform inscriptions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Luwian"@en .

aat:300451638 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Language of an Anatolian people contemporary with the Hittites, known from hieroglyphic and cuneiform inscriptions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Luwian"@en .

aat:300451640 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Receptacles used in the process of maple sugar or syrup, after it has been heated to concentrate the sugar content."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cooling troughs"@en .

aat:300451641 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Assortments of various items, often consisting of clothing and accessories, tools and products such as neutralizing chemical agents, often contained in a box, bag, package or bottle made of different materials, such as metal and plastic or glass, and of different shapes. Used to protect clothing, to decontaminate and to handle spills."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "decontamination kits"@en .

aat:300451642 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Work made substantially or wholly of glass materials, serving a primarily decorative function rather than a utilitarian function. Produced by artisans with an enhanced skill set to create compared to glass utilitarian objects. Includes work of stained glass, fused glass, slumped glass, blown glass, etc."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "decorative glass"@en .

aat:300451643 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sets of comestibles that have had the water removed from them so as to preserve and reduce their bulk. These constitute full meals that can be reconstituted when needed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dehydrated meals"@en .

aat:300451644 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Electronic protection accessories that are plugged into computers or other devices to enable the use of protected software or other restricted functions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dongles"@en .

aat:300451645 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Plasticized cardboard boxes with protective lining, often packaged with a plastic straw that can be inserted through a foil-covered hole in the top. Used to carry and consume drinks, generally juice."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "drink boxes"@en .

aat:300451646 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bags of varied sizes and shapes, often made from flexible materials, such as polyester or nylon, which seals the bag against the infiltration of moisture. These are used in outdoor activities, such as kayaking and canoeing, to protect personal belongings from getting wet."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dry bags"@en .

aat:300451647 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Electronic circuits that are used for signal processing. These may be analog or digitally based, and most commonly govern frequency selectivity of a tuning circuit, but have various applications."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "electronic filters"@en .

aat:300451648 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Badges or ornaments of varied materials, generally round or rectangular that bear an inscription or a design. These are designed to fasten to clothing, used to show support or presence at an event."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "event buttons"@en .

aat:300451649 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tools or devices used to pull fasteners from materials."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "extraction tools"@en .

aat:300451650 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Instruments that consist of a metal plate, two heel abutments opposite to each other and an adjustable ball joint abutment with a sliding pointer and sliding indicators with number and letter markings for the length, width and arch length of the human foot. Used to measure a person's shoe size."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brannock devices"@en .

aat:300451651 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Protective guards worn over or on the face, often made of transparent materials, usually plastic."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "face shields"@en .

aat:300451652 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Assortments of individually packed food in retortable pouches or cans. Used by military troops during operational activities, to hold and to serve easy-to-prepare meals to be consumed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "field rations"@en .

aat:300451653 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tools consisting of a either a flat rectangular blade, made of metal or rubber; or of a series of wire or rubber bristles attached to a long wooden or metal handle that are used to suppress a fire by beating out flames in brushes or crops."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fire beaters"@en .

aat:300451663 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In architecture, the sum of all areas on all floors within the outside faces of the exterior walls of a built work, including vertical penetration areas that connect one floor to another."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gross square feet"@en .

aat:300451664 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tibetan amulet boxes that were carried or worn, used as portable shrines and protective talisman for travelers."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gaus"@en .

aat:300451665 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Indian Buddhist masters who have attained superhuman abilities through tantric practices. Many schools of Tibetan Buddhism trace the origins of their teachings to the eighty-four mahasiddhas."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mahasiddhas"@en .

aat:300451666 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Personal decoration as a token of affection or recognition of services, which may be awarded by a knightly order or by the state."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "insignia of knighthood"@en .

aat:300451667 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genre in the visual arts in which the interior of churches is depicted."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "church interiors"@en .

aat:300451668 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Geometrical diagrams used as an aid to worship or meditation in tantric traditions. The assumed benefits to the user are derived from Hindu pantheon and tantric texts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yantras"@en .

aat:300451669 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People, usually priests, who makes statements prompted by his or her deity. This was especially common in ancient times, sometimes in a specific sacred place. The oracle's utterances serve as a directive to the inquiring party."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "oracles"@en .

aat:300451670 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The recruitment, transportation, transfer, harbouring or receipt of people through force, fraud or deception, with the aim of exploiting them for profit. It is the trade of humans for the purpose of forced labour, sexual slavery or commercial (sexual) exploitation for the trafficker or for others. Men, women and children of all ages and from all backgrounds can become victims of this crime."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "human trafficking"@en .

aat:300451671 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The practical implementation of ideas that result in the introduction of new goods or services. Innovation can refer to something new, or the practice of developing and introducing new things. Innovation is most commonly associated with business and technology, but it happens in any field where people introduce change."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "innovation"@en .

aat:300451672 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Children 's rattle also functioning as an amulet in the period between 14th and 19th century in Europe. Most tinkle bells are made of precious material with an object that offers protection and prosperity to the child. The tinkling bells usually featured jingle bells and the handle often incorporated a whistle. It was believed that the sounds warded off evil spirits."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jingle rattles"@en .

aat:300451673 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Important form of classical Japanese dance drama that has been performed since the 14th century. It is often based on stories from traditional literature with a supernatural being transformed into human form as a hero telling a story. Noh integrates masks, costumes and various props into a dance-based performance, Emotions are mainly conveyed through stylized conventional gestures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Noh"@en .

aat:300451674 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Form of comic Japanese theater performed since the 14th century. It developed alongside Noh theatre and often used to be performed as a light-hearted interlude, consisting of one scene, between acts of Noh. In the present, these plays are also often performed separately. This type of theater relies on mime, dialogue and fixed stereotypes of characters."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kyogen"@en .

aat:300451675 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Object wielded by a medium to make wise statements or communicate with divine beings on behalf of individuals of the populace."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "oracles"@en .

aat:300451676 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refuse heaps which mark ancient settlements, often containing bones, shells, potsherds, and stone implements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rubbish pits"@en .

aat:300451678 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small transparent vessels with straight sides, suitable for holding a sample in a laboratory instrument."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cuvettes"@en .

aat:300451680 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Residences that are separated by a shared wall, often a timber wall. May have been the result of dividing a larger residences into two. Distinguished from double houses (semi-detached houses), which comprise two separate but immediately adjacent residences, often on two properties, separated by party walls of masonry construction. A more easliy obvious distinguishing feature could be the front door; half-houses may have a single front door with a shared entry hall, while a double house will typically have two separate front doors."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "half-houses"@en .

aat:300451681 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pictorial postcards designed to be sent serially, with the completed set forming one picture when assembled. These may be arranged in a variety of ways, as a grid, horizontally, or vertically."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "installation postcards"@en .

aat:300451682 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pictorial postcards that may include hidden pictures, jigsaw puzzles, or any card which includes a puzzle to solve."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "puzzle postcards"@en .

aat:300451684 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Culture and style of the region of Cordoba, in Spain."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Cordoban"@en .

aat:300451685 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Armor pieces for the foot existing in various forms in Europe from Medieval times to the 16th century, comprised of mail or plates enclosing the ankle and lames covering the foot, plus a plate toecap. Some types had a detachable long point for the toecap."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sabatons"@en .

aat:300451686 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sheaths or tubular coverings for the toes. Examples include gold toe stalls used in Egyptian burial practices, and toe stalls used for medical purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "toe stalls"@en .

aat:300451687 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sheaths or tubular coverings for the fingers. Examples include gold finger stalls used in Egyptian burial practices, and finger stalls used for medical purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "finger stalls"@en .

aat:300451688 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Covers that fit over the nails of the fingers or toes. Examples include caps used for decorative purposes or in burial rituals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "nail caps"@en .

aat:300451690 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Construction technique using palm fronds and other parts of the palm, sometimes with materials from other plants. Examples are the structures made from date palms in the Middle East."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "palm leaf architecture"@en .

aat:300451691 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Manuscripts having dried palm leaves as writing supports. Although the earliest known extant manuscript leaf is from the 10th century, palm leafs were probably used for manuscripts as early as the 2nd century CE."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "palm-leaf manuscripts"@en .

aat:300451692 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Places designed for those with decreased cognizance from conditions such as Alzheimer's disease. These combine residential, commercial, and “public” buildings where residents live and associate with others in environments resembling pre-industrial small towns that include pedestrian-friendly navigation routes, family-centered houses, and shared communal spaces. Healthcare facilities are often disguised."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dementia villages"@en .

aat:300451693 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Courses of sailing or navigation other than by land, usually by sea or water from one place to another, specifically to a distant place or country. By extension, this has been applied to journeys into space or to extraterrestrial bodies."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "voyages"@en .

aat:300451694 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Journeys at sea for the purpose of hunting whales for their meat, baleen or whalebone, and blubber. Practiced as an organized activity as early the first century CE, by the 16th century whaling voyages as part of the industry of whaling became commonplace in the Basque region of Europe, and then the world. Whaling voyages peaked in the 19th century when the needs of industrialized society demanded whale oil in vast quantities, in advance of the development of fossil fuels. Recognition of the near-extinction of certain species of whale has caused the almost complete cessation of whaling and whaling voyages, except those allowed for by indigenous peoples in some parts of the world."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "whaling voyages"@en .

aat:300451695 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Palaces, generally rural, that provide retreats from winter weather for rulers and their courts or others."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "winter palaces"@en .

aat:300451696 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genre designating single or collected dramatic works, intended to be acted on the stage."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "plays"@en .

aat:300451697 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genre designating works containing references to or portrayals of short popular traditional songs that normally frame a narrative element."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ballads"@en .

aat:300451698 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Excerpts or short promotional works in any time-based media, particularly audio, that act as a preview of a longer broadcast."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "trailers"@en .

aat:300451699 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographs that give the illusion of depth or movement merging two or more pictures. This is achieved by separating images into strips and covering them with a transparent sheet made up of repeating rows of convex lenses (lenticules) on the front surface of the material, and a flat surface on the back side. Movement displacement of the viewer and the photograph reveals the illusion."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lenticular photographs"@en .

aat:300451702 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Physical or intellectual conditions that limit a person's movements, senses, or activities in a conventional sense."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "disabilities"@en .

aat:300451703 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Motility or other conditions that limit a person's movements, senses, or activities in a conventional sense."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "physical disabilities"@en .

aat:300451704 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Scholarship and teaching on the understanding of disability by incorporating social, cultural, historical, legal, and political perspectives, including the connections between disability and other identities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "disability studies"@en .

aat:300451706 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In the traditional Indian caste system, members of the highest group of castes, originally that of priests and scholars."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brahmins"@en .

aat:300451707 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Muslim theologian who is expert in the religious law of Islam."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fuqahā"@en .

aat:300451708 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Culture or styles of Ifriqiya, an historic region that was part of the Aghlabid state from ca. 800 CE."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ifriqiyan"@en .

aat:300451709 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Military officers serving as the chief political executive of an area under military government."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "military governors"@en .

aat:300451710 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People having this title of leadership; from Turkic and Mongolian languages, combining the title \"khan\" with the prefix \"il,\" to signify a khan of the people or of the nation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ilkhāns"@en .

aat:300451711 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Turkish governors of a province or district."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "beys"@en .

aat:300451712 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Muslim religious spaces for prayer, including a prayer hall or complex."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "zawiyas"@en .

aat:300451713 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who are experts in the implementation, design, configuration, and customization of metadata, which is data that describes other data and provides a structured reference."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "metadata specialists"@en .

aat:300451714 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Standardized Turkish language used in the Ottoman Empire; extensive borrowing from Arabic and Persian;Ottoman Turkish alphabet was used for for written communication."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ottoman Turkish"@en .

aat:300451715 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for people having a job or position where others are subordinate to them. They may be in charge of a particular activity or function within an organization."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "heads"@en .

aat:300451718 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Art that depicts mythological subjects or figures drawn from oral or written tradition, regardless of culture of origin."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mythological art"@en .

aat:300451719 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Movable or fixed objects or equipment, functional or ornamental, lcoated in public spaces, usually outdoors. Examples are benches, bike racks, signs, steet lights, and water fountains."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "urban furniture"@en .

aat:300451724 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Prepared sets of photographic images, sold for viewing in an accompanying viewing instrument, the kromscop, that accurately combined and projected three-separation color images. The individual images were photographed and re-presented through color filters. These images were also frequently stereographic. This was of particular importance in the development of full-color projection. Developed by Frederic Eugene Ives. Some of these early prints are preserved in the Smithsonian Institution, Washington, D.C."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kromograms"@en .

aat:300451727 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Electronic components fabricated as a single unit, in which transistors, diodes, capacitors and resistors are connected on a substrate of semiconductor material, typically silicon. These circuits may be as small as a few square millimeters. Individual circuit components are generally microscopic in size."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "integrated circuits"@en .

aat:300451732 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Cowboys, or horsemen of the South American pampas, specifically Argentina, as well as Uruguay, Brazil, and Patagonia. Historically, gauchos were migratory horseman of the 18th-19th centuries who engaged in cattle work. They have become folkloric or iconic figures in the same sense as North American cowboys."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gauchos"@en .

aat:300451733 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In the western United States, a horseman skilled at handling cattle, a laborer in the cattle industry of the trans-Mississippi west, and a romantic figure in American folklore."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cowboys"@en .

aat:300451740 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Short, usually hollow cylindrical sticks or rods carried by each member of a relay race team, which is passed to succeeding runners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "batons"@en .

aat:300451745 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Those parts of a financial system that raise capital by dealing in shares, bonds, and other long-term investments."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "capital markets"@en .

aat:300451762 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Stock market conditions characterized by the falling price of stock, as opposed to a bull market when prices are rising."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bear markets"@en .

aat:300451763 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Stock market conditions characterized by the rising price of stock, as opposed to bear markets, when they are falling."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bull markets"@en .

aat:300451764 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Broadly, rugs woven in Iran, in brightly coloured patterns of animals, plants, figures, or geometric shapes. The history of Persian carpet manufacture is divisive, and many assumptions are made without historical evidence. Scholars disagree on many aspects of their production. Particular styles are most often identified by the region in which they were made."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Persian rugs"@en .

aat:300451765 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rugs or carpets produced during the period of the Safavids, the dynasty that took control of Persia (now modern Iran) in the early 16th centuryand ruled from 1501 to ca. 1739. Many consider this the high point in Persian carpet design and manufacture. No certain examples of 9th/15th-century examples may survive. Safavid rug weaving is characterized by arabesques developed initially in manuscript illumination and architectural décor. Documentary evidence indicates that these tended to rely on a large central medallion format developed in the last phase of Timurid rug production. Scholars have attributed the centers of Safavid weaving to the major cities or centers of power."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Safavid carpets"@en .

aat:300451766 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Term used to designate the syncretic Afro-Creole religion of Haiti, which melds elements of Roman Catholic ritual with traditional West African magical and religious rites. Adherents commonly embrace spirit possession, animal sacrifice, altar worship, and ceremonial participation to serve the Vodou spirits (or Iwa). While rooted in the Vodún religious practices indigenous to Dahomey, Vodou signifies a specific New World transformation of Vodń as realized by West Africans enslaved on the plantations of Saint-Domingue (modern-day Haiti) during the Atlantic slave trade. Vodou provided a means for enslaved peoples’ liberation and identity-making. The religion played a significant role in facilitating the Haitian Revolution and continues to thrive as a popular folk religion both in Haiti and the diaspora. For the traditional religion of the Fon and Ewe people in Dahomey (modern-day Benin), use “Vodún.” For the African diasporic religion of the Mississippi River Valley, use “voodoo (Mississippi River Valley) .”"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Vodou"@en .

aat:300451768 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Term used to designate a diverse assemblage of spirit and ancestor cults constituting the traditional religion of the Fon and Ewe peoples residing in Dahomey (modern-day Benin). Vodún is a decentralized religious and social system united by common elements including ancestor and spirit veneration, deity ceremonies, and the use of animal sacrifice. It is widely understood as the original ancestor of the African diasporic Vodou and Voodoo religions, which were syncretically developed as enslaved Vodún practitioners encountered and integrated New World practices and cosmologies during the Atlantic slave trade. Vodún continues to thrive as the most practiced traditional African religion in Benin. For the Afro-Creole religion of Haiti, use “Vodou.” For the Afro-Creole religion of the Mississippi River Valley, use “Voodoo.”"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Vodún"@en .

aat:300451769 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus containing eight species of medium-to-large deciduous trees known as baobabs; native to Madagascar, mainland Africa, and Australia, but also widely introduced to in Asia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Adansonia"@en .

aat:300451770 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of tree endemic to the tropical arid savanna of the African continent, but also relatively well distributed in parts of Asia having a tropical or subtropical climate. Long-lived, majestic tree revered in Africa for its medicinal and nutritional value."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Adansonia digitata"@en .

aat:300451771 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Term used to designate the system of folk medical and magical practices originally developed by enslaved people in North America, particularly on the plantations of the old South. Hoodoo melds traditional West and Central African traditions with Native American and colonial European ethno-pharmacopeias and ritual knowledge. Its practitioners embrace natural reverence and engage in practices including herbal healing, ancestor worship, counterclockwise circle dancing (Ring Shout), water immersion, sacred music, and charm use. While united by common elements, Hoodoo is geographically distinct and best described as a body of personalized practices transmitted through apprenticeships. It has been assimilated into, but is distinct from, Voodoo practices in the Mississippi River Valley. For the African diasporic structured religion of the Mississippi River Valley, use “Voodoo.”"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hoodoo"@en .

aat:300451772 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Practitioners of Haitian Vodou, residing in both Haiti and the diaspora, who self identify as servants of the Vodou spirits (or lwa)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "vodouisants"@en .

aat:300451773 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In ancient Greece, young male citizens of around 18-20 years of age, who were taken from their homes and occupied chiefly with garrison duty."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ephebes"@en .

aat:300451774 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Texts used in the context of rites or rituals having to do with the deceased and dying."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "funerary texts"@en .

aat:300451775 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small divisions of land in Ireland and parts of Scotland; the system is of Gaelic origin. A townland typically covers a few hundred acres."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "townlands"@en .

aat:300451776 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Houses that are very narrow in floorplan, often designed to fit in a small footprint between larger buildings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "narrow houses"@en .

aat:300451777 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sites comprising scattered artefacts visible upon the ground surface that indicate past human activity. This can include ceramics, lithics, animal bones, shell middens, metal implements, coins, glass beads, or natural objects used for cultural purposes such ascowries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "artifact scatters"@en .

aat:300451778 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Carved stone stelae distinctive to Armenia and communities in the Armenian diaspora. Khachkars act as focal points for worship, as memorial stones, and as relics facilitating communication between the secular and divine. Most examples include a cross motif, often with additional motifs such as rosettes, leaves, and interlaces."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "khachkars"@en .

aat:300451789 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Persons whose occupation is to make a staple food of flour and water or other liquid or yeast to form a dough which is then cooked, usually by baking."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "breadmakers"@en .

aat:300451795 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Craftspeople traditionally engaged in the making of large kettles or boilers for a variety of purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cauldron-makers"@en .

aat:300451796 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Historically, those whose occupation it is to make wooden ships or vessels watertight by driving oakum or other material into wedge-shaped seams between planks forming the hull."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "caulkers"@en .

aat:300451799 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Those individuals whose occupation is to produce a common food made from the curds of milk pressed into a solid or semi-solid mass, and typically ripened."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cheesemakers"@en .

aat:300451800 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tradespeople who, since the Middle Ages have owned cloth manufactories or wholesale businesses that trade in cloth."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cloth merchants"@en .

aat:300451801 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Trade- or craftspeople who created cloth by weaving threads together. Wool would be gathered from sheep, cleaned, carded, dyed, and then spun into thread. Weavers would then use a loom to weave the threads together to form fabrics. This generally refers to a trade active from the Middle Ages through the Industrial Revolution when the process was taken up by machine looms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cloth weavers"@en .

aat:300451806 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Historically, a specialization of the fisherman occupation, harvesters of coral who brought raw coral to workshops where it would be processed in a variety of ways to sell as jewelry or for other uses in a global coral trade centered in Italy. In the late Medieval and early modern Mediterranean this was a specific occupation, often with its own guild (in places such as Genoa)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "coral fishers"@en .

aat:300451807 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tradespeople who weave cotton cloth, historically speaking, before the advent of the Industrial Revolution, beginning in the Middle Ages."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cotton weavers"@en .

aat:300451811 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Craftspeople who constructed close-fitting body-garments, with or without sleeves, worn by men from the 14th to the 18th centuries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "doublet makers"@en .

aat:300451815 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tradespeople who specialized in the storage of grain for distribution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "grain warehousers"@en .

aat:300451827 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Historically, people who change money from one form to another, as a profession."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "money-changers"@en .

aat:300451846 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Those who remove the fleece from an animal, as a profession."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "shearers"@en .

aat:300451848 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tradespeople that engage in the practice of dyeing raw silk."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "silk dyers"@en .

aat:300451849 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Persons whose occupation is the purchase and sale of silk goods or for profit. Originally used of any trader in goods not manufactured or produced by his or her own hand, from the 16th century, chiefly restricted to wholesale traders, particularly those trading across borders."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "silk merchants"@en .

aat:300451850 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tradespeople that engage in the process for producing single yarn of silk out of discontinuous filament of cocoon."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "silk spinners"@en .

aat:300451858 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Those whose occupation was to comb wool in order to prepare the fibers for spinning into yarns."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wool combers"@en .

aat:300451859 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Those who sell wool in its raw state for processing into products. Merchants historically have acted as purchasers, organizers, and sellers of finished textile material. For its manufacture, which required many processes, including cleaning the raw wool to final fulling and dyeing, merchants relied on an array of subordinate workers."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wool merchants"@en .

aat:300452068 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Those who row a boat using oars."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rowers"@en .

aat:300452069 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Men who row a boat using oars."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "oarsmen"@en .

aat:300452070 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Females who row a boat using oars."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "oarswomen"@en .

aat:300452071 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Prints made from objects, usually inscriptions and low-relief designs found on stones, bronzes and clayworks. A source surface is washed and damp paper is tamped or pasted on and left to dry, then blacked with a specially prepared pad imbued with liquid ink, the projecting areas of the surface becoming dark. The resulting rubbing has white impressions where the paper was pressed into the carving surrounded by a black ink field. Easily disseminated, ink rubbings were the primary means of reproducing historical data, poetry, scholastic texts, calligraphy, and imagery throughout China. Due to deterioration of original stones over time, ink rubbings are often the only evidence of a part of China's cultural heritage. The earliest known rubbings of this kind are Buddhist texts made from wood blocks in 8th century Japan. The technique may have been practiced as early as the 2nd century in China, where it was used to disseminate Confucian texts carved on large stones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ink rubbings"@en .

aat:300452072 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ancient Egyptian clergy who recited spells and hymns during temple rituals and official ceremonies."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lector priests"@en .

aat:300452086 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Identification numbers associated with objects or documents that are assigned by an individual collector, rather than a numerical system applied by a repository. These numbers may appear on the backs of paintings, and occasionally appear in stock books along with other descriptions of an object. These are similar to collector’s marks but without a stamp, and may be numeric or alphanumeric."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "collector assigned numbers"@en .

aat:300452087 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Extended Māori kinship group, in which members share distant common ancestors."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "iwi"@en .

aat:300452088 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Polynesian culture, deities, demons, or other supernatural beings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "atua"@en .

aat:300452089 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Māori tradition, beings living in deep water or dark caves, sometimes taking the form of a giant sea creature."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "taniwha"@en .

aat:300452090 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family containing around 60 genera and more than 1,650 species of flowering plants, some of which produce edible tubers."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Convolvulaceae"@en .

aat:300452091 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus containing over 600 species of flowering plants, such as the morning glory, water convolvulus, and sweet potato."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ipomoea"@en .

aat:300452092 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of annual vine in the morning glory family, cultivated for its edible tuberous roots; native to Mexico."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ipomoea batatas"@en .

aat:300452093 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Variety of sweet potato plant developed in New Zealand, characterized for edible parts that are small, finger-sized, and having yellow skin."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kumara"@en .

aat:300452094 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Units measure of exposure to light given by multiplying lux by hours."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lux hours"@en .

aat:300452095 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Units of measure of exposure to light given by multiplying foot-candles by hours."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "foot-candle hours"@en .

aat:300452096 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Some frames on early European panel paintings that were not only attached, or 'engaged,' but were made from the same piece of wood as the painting’s support where the grain of the upper and lower moldings match, visible if the gilding has been stripped away. These are found on small-scale Netherlandish panel paintings, which in some cases would be carved from a single plank, having either a raised profile only on the front of the panel and a flat surface on the reverse, or in some cases with raised frame projections on both surfaces. Examples of these exist from the fourteenth to the sixteenth century."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "integral frames"@en .

aat:300452097 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A generic relationship between one entity and another, used when no more specific alternative is available."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "associated"@en .

aat:300452099 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Units of illuminance measured as the degree of illumination that falls upon the interior of a sphere with a radius of one foot from a uniform point source of light of one candle and equal to one lumen per square foot. In the metric system, a lumen is measured by a square meter or a lux. Thus a foot-candle is equivalent to approximately 10 lux or 10.57 lux."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "foot-candles"@en .

aat:300452101 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Barrel vaults with a lunette placed transverse to the axis of the main vault."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "barrel vaults with lunette"@en .

aat:300452103 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ceilings having a central field framed by profiles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mirrored ceilings"@en .

aat:300452105 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Risalits achieved by increasing the wall thickness, but which does not change the floor plan inside the building."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "projecting avant-corps"@en .

aat:300452107 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pilasters, which span two (or more) storeys."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "colossal pilasters"@en .

aat:300452109 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A term referring to the closure of a floor plan of a building or part of a building. Often used in reference to sacral architecture, especially church presbyteries or apses."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "closures"@en .

aat:300452111 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two pilasters standing directly next to each other and related to each other by a cornice or wall articulation"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "double pilasters"@en .

aat:300452112 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Churches that fulfill, like filial churches, an auxiliary function to parish churches."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "auxiliary churches"@en .

aat:300452114 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pilasters in composite order."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "composite pilasters"@en .

aat:300452115 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Outer sides of buildings, opposite the main facade. In early modern castles often open to the garden."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rear façades"@en .

aat:300452117 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Roman Catholic term Stadtpfarrkirche (municipal parish church) is roughly equivalent to the Protestant Stadtkirche and refers to churches that represent a particular autonomy of a city in political significance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "municipal parish churches"@en .

aat:300452119 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Windows whose opening is closed by a segmental arch."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "segmental windows"@en .

aat:300452121 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The coat of arms of the donor."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "donors' coats of arms"@en .

aat:300452123 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Inscriptions, that have as a theme the donation of an object."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "donation inscriptions"@en .

aat:300452125 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A hipped gable peak in which the small hip does not engage the roof structure but cantilevers from the gable surface."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "half hips"@en .

aat:300452127 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Cornices below the eaves of a roof"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "eaves cornices"@en .

aat:300452129 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two wings (usually) running at right angles to each other."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "corner pavilions"@en .

aat:300452130 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A theatre that is publicly owned. In Europe the municipal theatres emerged from the court theatres of the later 18th or 19th century and, in some cases, also from private theatres jointly financed by the wealthy citizens of a town or city."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "municipal theatres"@en .

aat:300452132 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Grace churches are Protestant churches that came into being due to the Treaty of Altranstadt, and the Wroclaw Execution Trial (1709). At that time, the Lutherans in Silesia were granted six so-called grace churches, which were allowed to be built outside the city walls, but in stone and with a bell tower. Still in the tradition of peace churches, three wooden grace churches were built (Militsch, Freystadt and Sagan), and three others were erected as solid constructions (Hirschberg, Landeshut and Teschen)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Grace Churches"@en .

aat:300452133 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lesenes at the edges of a façade."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "corner lesenes"@en .

aat:300452135 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Domes formed by a spherical section rather than a hemisphere, so that their vault rises are less than the radius of the hemisphere."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "shallow domes"@en .

aat:300452137 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hall churches whose aisles are filled with galleries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "galleried churches"@en .

aat:300452138 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refuge churches are Protestant churches in Protestant areas in close proximity to recatholicized areas of Silesia. After the Thirty Years' War (1618-1648) in the 17th and 18th centuries, they served not only the local population of the Protestant religion but also the faithful from abroad who did not have religious freedom in their territories."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refuge Churches"@en .

aat:300452139 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Churches whose erection in the Habsburg hereditary duchies of Silesia was approved by the Catholic Emperor Ferdinand III under pressure from the Protestant imperial estates and Sweden. Their building was subject to additional conditions that would make their erection more complicated or if completed, their use short-lived. The churches could only be built outside the city walls within a period of one year. Only wood, clay and straw were allowed as building materials. Likewise, no tower was allowed to be erected."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Churches of Peace"@en .

aat:300452140 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Protestant church buildings erected for persecuted believers as a result of church reductions in the territories of Upper and Lower Lusatia, which had belonged to Saxony since 1635, Protestant Brandenburg, and religiously tolerant Poland. Other border churches were erected in the Piast Principalities of Liegnitz, Wohlau, and Brzeg, and in the Podiebrad Duchy of Oels."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "border churches"@en .

aat:300452141 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Traditional stone hand clubs used by the Mapuche, indigenous inhabitants of south-central Chile and southwestern Argentina; often resembling the form of a claw. They were ceremonial symbols of power; may have had a Polynesian connection."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "clavas"@en .

aat:300455760 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Chopines (platform shoes) of exceptional height, particularly those worn by women in Venice."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "calcagnetti"@en .

aat:300455761 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The technique of visually representing sets of facts or statistics collected for reference and analysis in graphical or schematic form. Formats used in data visualization typically include tables, charts and graphs, but may take a variety of pictographic forms. Data visualization increasingly involves digital rendering or processes, but pre- and co-exists with purely analog means of representation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "data visualization"@en .

aat:300455762 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Optical instruments for projecting kromograms, which are prepared sets of photographic images; the instrument accurately combined and projected three-separation color images. Invented by Fred. E. Ives and first demonstrated in 1896."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kromscops"@en .

aat:300455763 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Strategy board games for two players dating perhaps to the Roman Empire. The game has many variations based on the number of pieces, and corresponding variant names. The playing surface consists of a grid with intersections or points. In the most common form, each player has nine pieces in contrasting colors. Players attempt to form correspondences of pieces aligned to allow a player to remove an opponent's piece from the game. A player wins by reducing an opponent's number of pieces to a minimum, or to allow no possible moves."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "merels"@en .

aat:300455764 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Toys in which beads in various colors are slid over a spiral wire structure. It is used to introduce babies and toddlers to colors and shapes and to improve their hand-eye coordination."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bead mazes"@en .

aat:300455765 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tanzanite is the blue and violet variety of the mineral zoisite (a calcium aluminium hydroxyl sorosilicate), caused by small amounts of vanadium. Tanzanite belongs to the epidote mineral group. Tanzanite is only found in Tanzania, in a very small mining area (approximately 4.3 mi long and 1.2 mi) wide near the Mererani Hills."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tanzanite"@en .

aat:300455766 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Triclinic borosilicate mineral group comprised of Axinite-(Fe), Axinite-(Mg), Axinite-(Mn), and Tinzenite. Axinite without a sub-designation is not recognized as an individual mineral species. The group has its name after the generic name axinite first introduced in 1797 by the French mineralogist Rene Just Haüy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "axinite"@en .

aat:300455767 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sunstone (also: heliolite) is a plagioclase feldspar that, when viewed from certain angles, has a glittering appearance. Sunstone occurs in metomorphic and igneous rocks in southern Norway, Sweden and parts of the United States. It is also found in Canada, India and Russia (Lake Baikal). The most abundant site is at Tvedestrand in Norway, where it can be found in quartz veins running through the gneiss. The name was given by the mineralogist Jean-Claude Delamétherie in 1801, whose orange-like shimmer was reminiscent of the sun. This particular sparkle is created by the hematite and goethite crystals present in the mineral."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sunstone"@en .

aat:300455768 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Alexandrite is a mineral and a variety of chrysoberil. It was discovered in 1832 in the Urals and is named after the Russian Tsar Alexander II. Alexandrite can be green by daylight and red by incandescent light. Other varieties of alexandrite may be yellowish or pink in daylight and a columbine or raspberry red by incandescent light."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "alexandrite"@en .

aat:300455769 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Phenakite or phenacite is a fairly rare nesosilicate mineral consisting of beryllium orthosilicate (Be2SiO4), and occurring as colourless or white scales and prisms, and occasionally used as a gemstone in jewelry. Its name comes from Ancient Greek: phénax, meaning \"deceiver\" due to its close visual similarity to quartz, named by Nils Gustaf Nordenskiöld in 1833."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "phenakite"@en .

aat:300455770 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Professionals who engage in the buying and selling of diamonds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "diamond dealers"@en .

aat:300455771 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who engage in the process of transforming rough diamonds into finished gems."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "diamond workers"@en .

aat:300455772 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Metalworkers who specialize in casting objects in yellow copper or brass."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "brass casters"@en .

aat:300455773 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who, in the process of diamond cutting, split a rough diamond into smaller parts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "diamond cleavers"@en .

aat:300455775 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Animal-drawn carts used historically to transport heavy materials such as manure. These may be considered particular to parts of Belgian and the Netherlands, though the general form of the two-wheeled cart is familiar to many places. These consist of an axle mounted to two wheels supporting a load-bearing platform. The distinguishing feature of the earth cart is that the mounts or beams for pulling the cart are separate hinged pieces attached to the support of the load platform so that the platform may be tilted while still mounted to a pack animal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "earth carts"@en .

aat:300455776 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bicycles or tricycles designed to carry heavy loads, specifically those with large, integrated containers or platforms that can hold cargo or additional passengers, pets, or children."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cargo bikes"@en .

aat:300455777 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two or three-wheeled vehicles, non motorized carts for the transport of liquid manure with fixed, semi-fixed, or separate hoppers."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "liquid manure carts"@en .

aat:300455778 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Support beams with recesses for shoulders and with centrally placed hooks for attachment to a beer barrel. Used for the transport of barrels over a short distance by two people."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "beer trees"@en .

aat:300455779 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two-wheeled freight vehicles pulled by a person or by a pack animal, intended for the transport of beer barrels or beer crates."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "brewers' drays"@en .

aat:300455780 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Four-wheeled vehicle with various loading surfaces, pulled by a pack animal, specifically designed for the transport of beer barrels and crates."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "brewers' wagons"@en .

aat:300455782 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Supports in the form of paired beams used by two people to carry and move loads."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "carrying beams"@en .

aat:300455783 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Carrying tools for moving railroad ties. The jaws are wide enough to engage a tie, the sharpened ends reaching under or into the side of the tie. The arms move away from each other so they automatically clamp when the tool is lifted, and requires two people to handle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tie tongs"@en .

aat:300455784 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two-wheeled vehicles without suspension pulled by a pack animal with a fixed, non-tilting body, used for the transport of goods."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "high carts"@en .

aat:300455785 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "One-wheeled vehicles with an open loading surface and an upright front panel, exclusively for the transport of stones and roof tiles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "brick and tile wheelbarrows"@en .

aat:300455786 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sleds that serve to support a plough during transport to and from the field through mud or earth. These consist of parallel rails connected by beams. The sliding rails are often covered at the bottom with an iron belt or runner. Variations may include dividers covered with planks to form a deck or an open support surface of dividers."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "plough sleds"@en .

aat:300455787 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Handcarts specifically designed for the profession of a traveling blade or scissors sharpener, fitted with a grinding wheel."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "grinding carts"@en .

aat:300455789 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sled-like vehicles for the transport of barrels that may be pushed or pulled by a person or pack animal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "barrel sledges"@en .

aat:300455790 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Two-wheeled suspension vehicles pulled by a pack animal used for the transport of goods on a road, rather than for farm or field work."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dump carts"@en .

aat:300455791 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Korean language expressed in the Hangul writing system."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Korean (Hangul)"@en .

aat:300455792 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Korean language expressed in the Hanja writing system."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Korean (Hanja)"@en .

aat:300455793 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Farmer's two-wheeled carts that can be converted to four-wheeled wagons."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "morfreys"@en .

aat:300455794 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Handcarts propelled mainly by pulling the vehicle by hand. These appear as two- or four-wheeled vehicles often with a cross-handled beam to pull from."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pullcarts"@en .

aat:300455795 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Carts with two or more wheels with low beds and high side barriers, specifically equipped for the transport of cattle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cattle carts"@en .

aat:300455796 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Historicist architectural and furniture style developed in the third and fourth quarters of the 19th century. Reverts to the formal language of Louis XIV architecture from the late 17th and first quarter of the 18th century. Common decorative figures include, among others, acanthus leaves, laurel leaves and lion heads, all inspired by Greco-Roman antiquity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "neo-Louis XIV"@en .

aat:300455797 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Historicist architecture style developed around 1910. Reverts to the formal language of architecture of the mid-18th century, from the Louis XV period (1723-1750). Characterised by specific Rococo ornamentation such as irregular shell shapes, rocailles, and a predilection for asymmetry."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "neo-Louis XV"@en .

aat:300455798 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Historicist architecture style developed from the latter 19th century and onwards. Adopts neoclassical ornamentation from the Louis XVI period (1774-1792), such as oval medallions, laurel and fasces motifs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "neo-Louis XVI"@en .

aat:300455799 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Diamond material whose colour, quality or shape make them unsuitable for gem use, but ideal for industrial use."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "industrial diamond"@en .

aat:300455800 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rare mineral that was first discovered in 1902 in southern Madagascar. The mineral was named after French explorer Alfred Grandidier (1836–1912) who studied the natural history of Madagascar. Grandidierite is bluish-green to greenish-blue, with the blue component increasing with the iron (Fe) content."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "grandidierite"@en .

aat:300455801 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Natural diamond material that has undergone human intervention to temporarily or permanently alter its appearance. Examples of treatments include: optical coatings, filling fractures, pressure heating, irradiation, laser drilling, high pressure and high temperature treatment (HPHT) or any other physical or chemical process. This does not include cutting, grinding or cleaning of the diamond."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "treated diamond"@en .

aat:300455802 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Artificial products that have essentially the same physical and chemical characteristics as a diamond."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "synthetic diamond"@en .

aat:300455803 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Diamond crystals in their natural, unpolished state."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rough diamond"@en .

aat:300455804 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Any natural, treated or man-made material with the appearance but not the composition, structure or physical and optical properties of a diamond. Use \"synthetic diamonds\" when referring to the artificial product with the same physical and chemical properties."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "imitation diamond"@en .

aat:300455805 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Moissanite is naturally occurring silicon carbide and its various crystalline polymorphs. It has the chemical formula SiC and is a rare mineral, discovered by the French chemist Henri Moissan in 1893. Silicon carbide is useful for commercial and industrial applications due to its hardness, optical properties and thermal conductivity. In jewelry it is used as a imitation diamond."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "moissanite"@en .

aat:300455806 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Processes for treating crude or synthetic rubber or similar plastic material to give it useful properties including elasticity, strength, or stability. The term originally referred exclusively to the treatment of natural rubber with sulfur. Currently, the term may be applied to the hardening of other synthetic materials through a variety of means."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "vulcanization"@en .

aat:300455807 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A Japanese metalworking process which produces a mixed-metal laminate with distinctive layered patterns. Mokume-gane translates closely to \"wood grain metal\" or \"wood eye metal\" and describes the way metal takes on the appearance of natural wood grain."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mokume-gane"@en .

aat:300455808 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Serving spoons with wide, round bowls for scooping potatoes, often designed with dual flares on the bowl near the handle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "potato spoons"@en .

aat:300455810 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Fossilized shark's teeth that since the Renaissance were regarded as petrified tongues of dragons or snakes; thus referred to as \"tongue stones\" or \"glossopetrae.\" They were considered talismans against poison or toxins, and were used in the treatment of snake bites. Noblemen and royalty are known to have worn them as pendants or kept them as charms. They were also incorporated into utensils. For the material derived from the animal that may or may not be fossilized, use \"shark teeth.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tongue stones"@en .

aat:300455811 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ornamental cups in the shape of a pineapple, often made of silver, or silver-gilt."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pineapple cups"@en .

aat:300455812 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Traditional headdresses that unmarried women may wear on certain holidays, at festivals and, finally, at their wedding. These are common to central or Northern Europe, and designs vary regionally. In parts of Europe during the Middle Ages, they were decorated with flowers, fir branches, herbs and ripe fruits. From the 17th century until today, more expensive materials may be used such as pearls, mirrors, silver and gold."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bridal crowns"@en .

aat:300455813 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tools designed to pry corks off the necks of champagne bottles and/or to break open the muselet."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "champagne knives"@en .

aat:300455814 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small to medium-sized wheeled vehicles intended to be drawn behind another vehicle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tow carts"@en .

aat:300455815 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who install and maintain pipes and plumbing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "plumbers"@en .

aat:300455817 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Visual works having narrative content in which one scene, episode, or moment in a story is represented, rather than several; the scene includes figures and objects relevant to that one narrative moment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "monoscenic representation"@en .

aat:300455818 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Visual works having subject content in which multiple scenes, episodes, or moments in a story, or multiple stories, are represented."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "multi-scenic representation"@en .

aat:300455819 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Japanese books having a support comprising a long strip of paper with writing on one side, folded in a concertina fashion where each written page faces another written page. A cover may be attached to the front and back of the book; holes may be pierced to allow the book to be laced. Glossy mulberry torinokogami paper is often used. Based on a style of binding known in China during the Tang period."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "orihon"@en .

aat:300455820 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Term invented by artist Robert Delaunay to describe the abstract painting style developed by him and his wife Sonia Delaunay, characterized by interlocking or overlapping areas of contrasting or complementary colors. Derived from the theories of Michel Eugène Chevreul's \"On the law of the simultaneous contrast of colours.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Simultanism"@en .

aat:300455822 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Deities of the Yoruba people of southwestern Nigeria, including numerous minor gods and spirits invoked or worshipped by the Yoruba people and their diaspora and descendants in Cuba, the West Indies, and the Americas."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "orishas"@en .

aat:300455823 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family of saurian reptiles, small lizard-like creatures, distinguished by a prehensile tail, long tongue, eyes moving independently, and covered each with a single circular eyelid, having the characteristic of changing the color of their skin."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chamaeleonidae"@en .

aat:300455824 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Monarchs who are also esteemed as warriors, often confirming the right to rule through prowess."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "warrior kings"@en .

aat:300455829 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An HTTP-based published standard or protocol for adaptive bitrate streaming originally developed by Apple Inc. to distribute live or pre-recorded audio and video with low latency over the Internet. It allows a receiver to adapt the bit rate of the media to the current network conditions in order to maintain uninterrupted playback at the best possible quality. The protocol has been implemented in media players, web browsers, mobile devices, and streaming media servers. While HLS started as an Apple proprietary streaming protocol, it has become an open industry standard."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "HTTP Live Streaming"@en .

aat:300455830 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A family of stateless, application-level, request/response protocols that share a generic interface, extensible semantics, and self-descriptive messages to enable the retrieval of data on networks, especially of HTML documents via the Internet. HTTP is the foundation of data communication for the World Wide Web."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "HTTP"@en .

aat:300455832 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pervasive, oversimplified, culturally contingent, and often inaccurate characterizations of particular social groups, rooted in assumption or perception rather than empirical data. Such assumptions are often deeply embedded within power structures and social institutions. The study of stereotyping within the social sciences is widely traced to Walter Lippmann, who coined the social use of the term in his 1922 book \"Public Opinon.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stereotypes"@en .

aat:300455833 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The French language, comprising multiple Romance dialects, as used in Northern France and elsewhere between the 9th and the early 14th centuries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Old French"@en .

aat:300455834 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Having a three-dimensional rounded shape, resembling a sphere but not necessarily with the geometrical precision of a true sphere."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "globular"@en .

aat:300455836 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Identification of years as Before Common Era or Before Current Era or Before Christian Era. BCE uses the same numbering as BC (Before Christ) and corresponds to the Gregorian Calendar. \"CE\" and \"BCE\" are placed after a year number, and BCE is now most often used instead of BC especially in scientific writing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "BCE"@en .

aat:300455837 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "CE, for \"Common Era,\" and occasionally, \"Christian Era,\" identifies years as part of a historical cultural current for recording dates used today. CE is used as an alternative to \"AD\" used by Christians, but the numbering is the same, with the same meaning as AD, and corresponds to the Gregorian Calendar. CE is now often used instead of AD particularly in scientific writing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "CE"@en .

aat:300455838 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Preserved information constituting evidence about events, usually documents, collected and maintained by the congregation of a synagogue or community house of worship that serves as a place not only for liturgical services but also for the study of Judaism. Modern synagogue records chronicle the same functions associated with ancient synagogues but may reflect added social, recreational, and philanthropic programs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "synagogue records"@en .

aat:300455839 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Documents maintained by or relating to a particular mosque and its activities, including for many public functions; religious, political, social, and educational. Schools and libraries and courts of justice were attached to mosques until the introduction of secular law into many Islamic countries in modern times, so records of these functions may be included."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mosque records"@en .

aat:300455840 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes particular styles or categories of works of art. Genre designates the style or technique of the intellectual content of textual materials or, for graphic materials, aspects such as vantage point, intended purpose, characteristics of the creator, publication status, or method of representation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "genre"@en .

aat:300455841 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Female security guards or soldiers of the guards or household guards."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "guardswomen"@en .

aat:300455843 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Thuribles having a body that is pear-shaped."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "piriform thuribles"@en .

aat:300455844 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Heavy color, or zhongcai describes a style of painting popular in medieval-era China that relies on a dispersion of mineral colors that are opaque and solid. Artworks created with this technique are characterized by a fine, precise definition of volume and the layering of pigmented hues. Having risen to prominence in the Tang Dynasty, the practice of heavy color or zhongcai later waned. Unlike free-flowing ink paintings, the technique displays meticulous brushwork with lifelike effects, while the lightweight texture of the paint differs from the thicker, denser effects produced by oil painting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "heavy color"@en .

aat:300456574 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Overlying horizontal stones of a cromlech or dolmen."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "capstones"@en .

aat:300456575 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Numeric or alphanumeric values that govern the sort order of data in databases, spreadsheets or other datasets."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sort values"@en .

aat:300456576 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sector of Catholicism of the Church of Milan, reflecting a distinctive, ancient rite, including its associated plainsong. Derives its name from St. Ambrose, Bishop of Milan (374-397)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ambrosian"@en .

aat:300456577 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A natural silicate of calcium and aluminum silicate, Ca2Al3(SiO4)3OH, characteristic of regional metamorphism and of hydrothermal alteration of igneous rocks. A member of the epidote group of nesosilicates, it is a lustrous transparent to translucent stone with color ranging grayish-white, peach, pink (thulite), blue (tanzanite) or green. Named for Baron Zois van Edelstein (1747-1819)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "zoisite"@en .

aat:300456578 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Translucent zoisite stone having a pale to deep pink color, in which manganese replaces up to 2 % of the calcium. Thulite was discovered near Telemark, Norway and named for Thule, an old name for Norway."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "thulite"@en .

aat:300456579 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Settlements on water, comprising man-made floating islands, boats, or houses and other structures built on stilts in a body of water or swampy area."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "floating communities"@en .

aat:300456581 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Machines that make prints using special stencils that allow ink to bleed through onto a support. The stencil is mounted onto a drum containing the ink, which is pushed through the stencil onto a support, usually paper."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stencil duplicators"@en .

aat:300456582 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Vessels that are taller than they are wide, and having the basic shape of a cylinder. Examples include Precolumbian, ancient Egyptian, and ancient Chinese vases."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cylinder vases"@en .

aat:300456583 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Spiritual objects made by vodouisants (oungans and mambos) during ceremonies, or for rituals. These are usually in the form of a bottle containing a variety of substances derived from plant and animal ingredients, and that are wrapped in textiles and embellished in a variety of ways. These objects themselves are derived from African spiritual practice, where similar objects called minkisi or nkisi are found, in particular from Congo. Paquets congo sometimes have arms or other anthropomorphic features, and usually involve a technique of careful wrapping, seven or nine times, as a symbol of connecting forces."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "paquets congo"@en .

aat:300456584 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A class of Haitian Vodou priest, who perform a role as ministers to the lwas. They are often the heads of Vodou temples, though these may also be led by their female counterparts, known as manbos or mambos."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "oungans"@en .

aat:300456585 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A class of female clergy in the religion of Haitian Vodou. They act as community leaders, serve spirits, and perform all functions related to Vodou tradition."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "manbos"@en .

aat:300456586 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Term used to designate the syncretic Afro-Creole religion developed along the lower Mississippi River and its tributaries, most notably New Orleans. The evolution of Voodoo was strongly influenced by Haitian immigration to Southern Louisiana in the early 19th century; the diaspora melded its Vodou practices with elements of neo-African counterculture religions, Native American traditions, and traditional folk healing. Voodoo practitioners centered spirits of the dead, embodied by the almighty God figure Li Grand Zombi. While variations of Voodoo are still practiced today, it is widely understood to be a historical religion that died out by the 1940s due to increased persecution of worshipers. Nonetheless, it continues to be associated with the culture and identity of the Southern Louisiana region. In addition to its usage in the specific context of southern Louisiana, the term 'voodoo' is often used as a racialized and pejorative umbrella term for all Black peoples’ religious and spiritual practices. When used outside the context of the Mississippi River Valley, voodoo represents a colonial misunderstanding of Black religion that ignores the distinct histories of Vodún, Vodou, and Voodoo. For the Afro-Creole religion of Haiti, use \"Vodou.\" For the traditional religion of the Fon and Ewe people in Dahomey (modern-day Benin), use “Vodún.”"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "voodoo"@en .

aat:300456587 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Artists whose work synthesizes two or more fields or branches of study to create something new, as a mix of theory and practice, and that may integrate two or more elements of expression, such as sound, image, movement , text, etc.. Interdisciplinary artists may work in many genres, or combine fields such as painting, photography, filmmaking, literature, or philosophy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "interdisciplinary artists"@en .

aat:300456588 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Artists that create works, such as in cinema, opera, or performance art that fuse aesthetic disciplines into a singular artistic experience. Working collaboratively, each individual draws on distinct fields of knowledge to create what is usually a single work, or activity. Bound by these distinct distinct fields, as in, for example in cinema, where photographers, writers, editors, and musicians work together to create a whole, singular piece. The term 'multidisciplinary artist' is often used to mean artists that work in multiple disciplines, but for artists that create works in many disciplines or that individually create works drawing on multiple fields, use 'interdisciplinary artists.'"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "multidisciplinary artists"@en .

aat:300456589 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A generic term that refers to a broad class of anthropomorphic figures or dolls used as focus entities to ritually bind social or supernatural enemies. Extant historical sources specify these figures' roles as mere representations or symbols, rather than facsimilies, of social or supernatural identities. They serve primarily as a tangible medium for ritual performance through which spiritual forces can be contained. Traditional treatment of the figures included twisting, burning, melting, and otherwise manipulating their limbs into bound positions; piercing the objects with pins or nails; and enclosing the dolls within sealed boxes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "binding figurines"@en .

aat:300456592 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bottles or other glass vessels having the neck divided into two or more tubes, causing them to make a gurgling noise when poured. The type has Roman antecedents; it was notably produced by German glassworkers in the later Middle Ages, and among Venetian and façon de Venise glasses of the 16th and 17th centuries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kuttrolfen"@en .

aat:300456594 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The current state of an information object qua information at a particular time; the validity of a piece of data regarding its appropriateness, or rightness to use at the present historical moment. An implicit author is applied to all such judgments."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "information status"@en .

aat:300456595 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state that an information object possessed in the past, but that is no longer valid in a present context."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "historic"@en .

aat:300456596 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The state that an information object possesses in the present; that is valid in a present context."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "current"@en .

aat:300456597 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Evidence, assurance, or guarantee of a fact by supplying a source for the verification of a piece of knowledge."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "warrant"@en .

aat:300456598 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Numeric identifiers which indicate unique iterative states of an object."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "version numbers"@en .

aat:300456599 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Text elements that act as containers or environments created to hold a logical grouping of unique identifiers, as for a scheme or concept."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "namespaces"@en .

aat:300456600 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Electronic devices used for viewing and hearing television broadcasts, or as computer monitors. Includes various models over time, from the 1920s mechanical tuner, display, and loudspeakers unit; to the boxy electronic form using cathode ray tube (CRT) technology; to the 2010s flat-panel televisions incorporating liquid-crystal display (LCD) technology, often LED-backlit LCD technology. The television receiver may be incorporated in the television viewing device or housed in a separate unit."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "televisions"@en .

aat:300456601 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Supporting frameworks consisting of inclined upright pieces with diagonal braces; used in architecture, furniture, and for other purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "trestles"@en .

aat:300456602 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bridges of an ancient form, made of large flat slabs of stone, often granite or schist, supported on stone piers across a water course or depression. Best known examples are in the United Kingdom."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "clapper bridges"@en .

aat:300456603 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Foot bridges comprising a series of stones across a shallow body of water, swamp, or other area."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "step stone bridges"@en .

aat:300456604 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Simple foot bridges comprising a felled tree trunk, or an arrangement of logs, placed across a depression or body of water."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "log bridges"@en .

aat:300456605 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Built works designed for or used for live performances, as distinguished from movie theaters."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "live theaters"@en .

aat:300456606 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genres of performing arts carried out with performers before a live audience. Used to distinguish live theatrical performances from films or online performances."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "live theater"@en .

aat:300456607 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Texts which are precise transcriptions of some original text, such as a passage of a document, written or spoken."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "verbatim texts"@en .

aat:300456608 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Renewing or strongly improving products or services or the processes by which products and services are created. It is the change of a technical or scientific nature made to the good or service offered by a company or organization, in the processes developed within it. This is usually done in order to achieve greater competitiveness. Innovations in technology influence values ​​within a society and often raise ethical questions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "technological innovation"@en .

aat:300456609 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The combination of managing innovation processes and change management. It refers to actively organizing, controlling and executing activities, processes and policies that lead to the creation of substantial new value for a company and its customers. This is done by creatively changing one or more dimensions of the business system."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "innovation management"@en .

aat:300456610 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Circulation, transmission and exchange of knowledge, views and theories between countries, regions and people from different cultures by means of contact and encounter and how this has (mutual) impact on the cultural and artistic production."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cultural exchange"@en .

aat:300456611 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Publications in which one or more artists have a full or large share in the process of conceptualization, production, and often distribution. These can be of various types: books, magazines, catalogs, individual sheets or inserts, audiovisual media and ephemera such as invitations, posters, mailart, flyers, postcards, etc. These may be autonomous works of art, as well as publications in which the artist or an artists’ collective has made an intervention or made a contribution. Examples of the latter are contributions in the form of a lithograph, linocut, fold or a cut-out in a newspaper, magazine or catalog. Certain audiovisual media such as music, film and sound work may also be considered artists’ publications, and are often accompanied by a cover, box or book designed by the artist."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "artists' publications"@en .

aat:300456612 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Business process of planning, developing, launching and managing a product or service. It covers the entire life cycle of a product, from conception to development and marketing. An important goal of product managment is ensuring that a product meets the needs of its target market and contributes to business strategy. The product manager manages a product or products through all phases of the product life cycle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "product management"@en .

aat:300456613 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Series of steps that includes the conceptualization, design, development and marketing of newly created goods or services. This may concern tangible objects, but also a computer program or financial services. The goal from a business perspective is to cultivate, maintain, and increase a company's market share by meeting consumer demand. From the customer's point of view, the idea is to guarantee the quality of the product or service."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "product development"@en .

aat:300456614 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Documents in early modern institutions of higher education for announcing academic dissertations to be defended in public.These decorated broadsheets summarise the student’s conclusion. The aim of the engraved posters was mainly to advertise the disputation and to introduce the theses in question. The practice was mostly common in Catholic European countries: Italy, France, the Southern Netherlands, Germany and Austria."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "thesis prints"@en .

aat:300456615 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Industry that treats in some detail the processes involved in the conversion of fibres to yarn, fabric construction, finishing operations applied to textiles, uses of textile materials, and the relationship between the producer and the consumer."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "textile industry"@en .

aat:300456616 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Conditions under which aspiring artists learned or learn their skills. In Europe, before the establishment of the state art academies in the 17th and 18th centuries, training as craftsmen took place in the workshop of experienced masters, controlled by the guilds. Gradually the teaching programme began to include more theoretically-based disciplines, set up in art schools/academies."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "artist's training"@en .

aat:300456618 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who specialize in making pipes for smoking."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pipe makers"@en .

aat:300456619 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Data types that are a sequence of symbols or linguistic elements in a defined order."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "strings"@en .

aat:300456620 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Data included in fields reserved for spans of time. These may be formatted in various ways."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dates"@en .

aat:300456621 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Data type that represents a number or quantity denoting one or more whole things or units; a whole number or undivided quantity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "integers"@en .

aat:300456622 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Terms that have been manually validated and inserted into a term bank or thesaurus, for the purpose of controlled indexing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "controlled terms"@en .

aat:300456623 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An open-standard format for encoding a variety of geographic data structures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "GeoJSON"@en .

aat:300456624 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Data types representing streams of digitized content or media in binary form."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bitstreams"@en .

aat:300456625 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Forms of data whose expected content is the URI of a referenced entity expected to follow a specific independent data schema."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "reference models"@en .

aat:300456626 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Data types that display content that is the URI of a referenced entity expected to follow a specific dependent data schema."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "collection models"@en .

aat:300456627 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Data types that provide content as an annotation on another piece of data. These may be simple text, multimedia content, or detailed description."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "annotation data"@en .

aat:300456628 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Labels for an information unit in a data system that is meant to be read by the end user."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "UI names"@en .

aat:300456629 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Inverse labels for information units in a data system that are meant to be read by the end user."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "inverse UI names"@en .

aat:300456630 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Unique names assigned to an information unit in a data system to identify it within that context."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "system names"@en .

aat:300456631 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Notes included in a data field that provide descriptive information about a concept or expands upon information recorded in other fields."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "scope notes"@en .

aat:300456633 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "JSON-LD is a lightweight Linked Data format that is easy for humans to read and write. It is based on the already successful JSON format and provides a way to help JSON data interoperate at Web-scale."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "JSON-LD"@en .

aat:300456634 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Resource Description Framework is a standard model for data interchange on the Web. RDF can facilitate data merging when underlying schemas differ, and supports evolution of schemas over time without requiring that all data consumers to be changed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "RDF"@en .

aat:300456635 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "SPARQL is a recursive acronym for SPARQL Protocol and RDF Query Language. It is an RDF semantic query language for databases able to retrieve and manipulate data stored in Resource Description Framework (RDF) format."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "SPARQL"@en .

aat:300456642 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of fermented malt beverage that is fermented rapidly at high temperature; fermentation is checked while a considerable portion of sugar still remains unchanged."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ale"@en .

aat:300456643 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Silver gelatin, or silver halide, black and white prints on a fiber base, or RC-base paper, and having a digital component. Silver gelatin paper is exposed in a photographic laser printer from a digital file, then processed in liquid photo chemistry. These may have archival qualities and a completely neutral image tone."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "digital gelatin silver prints"@en .

aat:300456644 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of fermented malt beverage that is fermented slowly and thoroughly at a low temperature."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lager beer"@en .

aat:300456645 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Earthenware mugs intended as vessels for beer; also, glass vessels having a similar shape; commonly holding about a pint. They may be decorated with references to beer, or to a particular brewery."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "beer steins"@en .

aat:300456646 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tall, narrow, cylindrical drinking vessels, usually with a pedestal foot; may be lidded. From the German, meaning \"pole glass\" in reference to the shape. May be richly ornamented; often used for beer."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stangenglases"@en .

aat:300456647 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Deep-learning models that are intended to generate high-quality text, images, and other content based on the data on which they were trained."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "generative AI"@en .

aat:300456648 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Curtains that hang on the outside of other curtains, to provide added protection from the sun or cold, or as decoration to frame the window opening."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "overcurtains"@en .

aat:300456649 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The process of reusing waste material to create a product of higher quality or value than the original, with the goal of reducing the need for additional raw materials."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "upcycling"@en .

aat:300456650 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Curtains hung over the opening of a doorway, or at another threshold."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "threshold curtains"@en .

aat:300456651 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for screens, sheets, louvers, parasols, or other devices or objects used for protection of a building, person, or other entity from the heat and light of the sun."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sun shades"@en .

aat:300456652 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Large ditches dug as a simple fortification or part of a larger fortification. Usually include a bank built up from the earth removed from the ditch. Generally less extensive than \"moats.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "defensive dikes"@en .

aat:300456653 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to varieties of Spanish spoken in Venezuela."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Venezuelan Spanish"@en .

aat:300456655 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Types of apses designed with special areas for officials or speakers. either a raised platform or a dome, or both. In a Roman basilica, the tribune is semicircular apse with a raised platform, where a presiding magistrates (tribunes) sat in an official chair. In Medieval and later ecclesiastical architecture, the tribune is a designated area circumscribed by a vaulted or semi-domed apse."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tribunes"@en .

aat:300456656 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for raised areas or stage-like platforms designating prominence; usually restricted to interior architecture and suitable for delivering an oration; overlapping in meaning with \"daises\" and ancient \"tribunals.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tribunes"@en .

aat:300456657 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who organize advertising and publicity in the media on behalf of an organization or well-known person."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "press agents"@en .

aat:300456658 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Establishments engaged primarily in providing photographs and photographic equipment; may include services for film processing, developing, and printing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "photographic services"@en .

aat:300456659 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Traditionally refers to the culture, style, and period of the ancient vassal state or regional dynasty of Chu (770-223 BCE), which began as a duke state under the Western Zhou before expansion. Chu eventually fell to the Qin dynasty. Its center was in present-day Anhui province."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chu"@en .

aat:300456662 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family of flowering plants."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ripogonaceae"@en .

aat:300456663 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus of flowering plant comprising six species, native to eastern Australia, New Zealand, and New Guinea."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ripogonum"@en .

aat:300456664 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of rainforest plant, traditionally used by Māori to bind and pull objects. For example, the vine was used to tie firewood together and for towing small canoes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ripogonum scandens"@en .

aat:300456665 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Plant material used for binding and weaving, from Ripogonum scandens plants native to New Zealand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kareao"@en .

aat:300456666 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Cloak pins used to fasten customary Maori cloaks over the shoulder; made from marine mamal ivory, and later boar tusks."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "aureis"@en .

aat:300456667 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Distinctive barge board supports seen in Maori architecture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "amos"@en .

aat:300456668 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Order of seabirds containing one extant family."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Phaethontiformes"@en .

aat:300456669 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family of seabirds who are the sole living representatives of the order Phaethontiformes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Phaethontidae"@en .

aat:300456670 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus containing three living species of tropbirds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Phaethon"@en .

aat:300456671 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of seabird native to tropical parts of the Indian and Pacific Oceans."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Phaethon rubricauda"@en .

aat:300456672 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Fixtures found on religious and ceremonial buildings of the Pacific Islands."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pacific Island religious building fixtures"@en .

aat:300456673 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Raised platforms or stages on a marae."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "atamiras"@en .

aat:300456674 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Forecourts, courtyards, or other open spaces located in front of a marae or other architecture; serving as a public forum."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "marae āteas"@en .

aat:300456675 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Distinctive rafters in traditional Maori buildings; often painted or otherwise decorated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "heke"@en .

aat:300456676 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Māori communities, large central, communal buildings, usually carved and decorated, where assemblies take place."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wharenui"@en .

aat:300456677 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings, often relatively modest buildings in small or colonial communities, where public and often religious meetings took place. For buildings dedicated to religious meetings or services, that no longer have the use of a community center, prefer \"meeting houses (religious buildings).\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "meeting houses"@en .

aat:300456678 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Traditional Polynesian lunar-stellar system based on the movements and phases of the moon and stars."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maramataka Māori"@en .

aat:300456679 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Capes designed to protect the wearer from rain."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rain capes"@en .

aat:300456680 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Protective garments woven that hang from the shoulders, made of grasses and other materials designed to keep the wearer warm and dry."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hieke"@en .

aat:300456681 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family containing the stitchbirds. Although the birds resemble members of the honeyeater family Meliphagidae, genetic analysis has recently proven they are not related."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Notiomystidae"@en .

aat:300456682 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species endemic to New Zealand, but extirpated everywhere except Little Barrier Island; it is being reintroduced elsewhere."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Notiomystis cincta"@en .

aat:300456683 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus containing the stitchbirds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Notiomystis"@en .

aat:300456684 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Large gourd used as a musical instrument, which is played by blowing over the opening at the top."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hue puruhau"@en .

aat:300456687 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Improvised instrument played by blowing over the opening of a bottle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "blown bottles"@en .

aat:300456688 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori spades or digging sticks, used for lighter cultivation work."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kāheru"@en .

aat:300456689 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori feather cloaks, distinctive by a style using feathers all over the cloak, popular in the latter half of the 19th century. Feathers had previously been incorporated into the weaving of cloaks, but usually only in the borders."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kahu huruhuru"@en .

aat:300456690 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori man or woman with leadership responsibilities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kaihautū"@en .

aat:300456691 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family comprising 3 genera of extant parrots, and one or more extinct, endemic to New Zealand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Strigopidae"@en .

aat:300456692 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus of New Zealand parrot."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Strigops"@en .

aat:300456693 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of large, nocturnal, ground-dwelling parrot of New Zealand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Strigops habroptila"@en .

aat:300456694 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus containing two species of large pigeons from New Zealand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hemiphaga"@en .

aat:300456695 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of New Zealand woodpigeon."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hemiphaga novaeseelandiae"@en .

aat:300456696 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori puppet or marionette, tpically a figure carved in human form with arms that move by pulling a string."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "karetao"@en .

aat:300456697 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus containing two extant parrot species from New Zealand and two extinct species."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nestor"@en .

aat:300456698 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of large parrot endemic to the forested and alpine regions of South Island, New Zealand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nestor notabilis"@en .

aat:300456699 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori implement, paddle-like but variable in form; used for agriculture and other purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ketu"@en .

aat:300456700 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori woven bags, such as tote bags, having a decorative pattern."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kete whakairo"@en .

aat:300456701 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Order of birds of New Zealand; members were formerly lumped with Struthioniformes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Apterygiformes"@en .

aat:300456702 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family of birds of New Zealand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Apterygidae"@en .

aat:300456703 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus containing five species of flightless birds of New Zealand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Apteryx"@en .

aat:300456704 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small Maori flute; open at both ends, ductless and notchless, with three to six fingerholes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kōauau"@en .

aat:300456705 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Maori buildings, facing boards on the gable of a house; the lower ends are often ornamented with carving."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "maihi"@en .

aat:300456707 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Māori kites, having wooden or rush frames, and covered with bark, leaves, or other material; thought to have been used for recreation, for divination, for communication, and in celebration of the Maori new year."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "manu tukutuku"@en .

aat:300456708 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Apparatuses used for alluring animals to be captured."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lures"@en .

aat:300456709 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Benin and elsewhere in Africa, distinctive fishing rafts of floating grass and vegetable matter, anchored with stakes near river bank."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "aholos"@en .

aat:300456710 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori garments worn by tying at the waist, including garments similar to kilts, aprons, or loin cloths."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "moro"@en .

aat:300456712 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Period of the Māori New Year and the rising of the Pleiades star cluster (Maramataka)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Matariki"@en .

aat:300456713 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori fish hooks, traditionally made of stone, wood, or shell, later incorporating metal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "matau"@en .

aat:300456714 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Permanent markings on the skin, as traditionally applied in the Māori culture. Moko tattoos served as identifying connections between an individual and their ancestors."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "moko"@en .

aat:300456715 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori tattoos applied on females, usually on the chin."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "moko kauae"@en .

aat:300456716 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Material comprising the fiber of New Zealand hemp, Phormium tenax."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "muka"@en .

aat:300456717 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori fortified settlements, such as a fortress or village defended by a stockade."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pā tūwatawata"@en .

aat:300456718 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Traditional Māori receptacles made of kelp and tōtara bark; used to carry and store food, fresh water, live shellfish, and other substances or items."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pōhā"@en .

aat:300456719 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Maori architecture, central support pole of a meeting house; often carved."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pou tokomanawa"@en .

aat:300456720 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori wooden trumpets; made from a long, hollowed piece of wood, having a bell-shaped end; held together with vine."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pūkaea"@en .

aat:300456721 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori conch shell trumpet with a wooden mouthpiece."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pūtātara"@en .

aat:300456722 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Phylum containing three diverse groups of heterokont protists."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gyrista"@en .

aat:300456723 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Large group of multicellular algae."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Phaeophyceae"@en .

aat:300456724 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Order of brown algae."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fucales"@en .

aat:300456725 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family of brown algae."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Durvillaeaceae"@en .

aat:300456726 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus of large brown algae, in which all members are found in the southern hemisphere, including Australia, New Zealand, South America, and various subantarctic islands."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Durvillaea"@en .

aat:300456727 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Large, robust species of southern bull kelp found on the coasts of Chile, southern New Zealand, and Macquarie Island."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Durvillaea antarctica"@en .

aat:300456728 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori crayfish pots, used for fishing for crayfish."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tāruke"@en .

aat:300456729 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Prows on Maori boats."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tau ihu"@en .

aat:300456730 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sterns on Maori boats."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tau rapa"@en .

aat:300456731 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori entrance gateway to a marae complex."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "waharoa"@en .

aat:300456732 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Double-hulled voyaging canoes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "waka hourua"@en .

aat:300456733 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Polynesian war canoes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "waka taua"@en .

aat:300456734 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Traditional woven mats, particularly those forming the basic floor coverings in Maori structures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "whāriki"@en .

aat:300456735 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Groups of planets, meteors, or other objects that orbit one or more large stars."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "star systems"@en .

aat:300456736 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Star systems in which two or more stars orbit each other, bound by gravitational attraction."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "multiple star systems"@en .

aat:300456737 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Maori culture, skeletal remains."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kōiwi tangata"@en .

aat:300456738 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Māori Christian sect and movement that began in 1918 under the leader Tahupōtiki Wiremu Ratana; it is headquartered in Rātana, New Zealand."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rātana"@en .

aat:300456739 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Human remains comprising preserved heads of Māori indigenous people of New Zealand, valued because the faces had been decorated by tā moko tattooing. The remains were valuable trade items during the Musket Wars of the early 19th century. Many of the remains were repatriated to Te Papa Tongarewa in 2014."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Toi moko"@en .

aat:300456740 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori boxes designed to hold treasured personal adornments, such as amulets, combs, and bird feathers. Generally made from wood, elaborately carved, and suspended from the ceiling of a whare or house, so that the ornately carved underside could be admired."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "waka huia"@en .

aat:300456741 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Traditional idiophones comprising a medium-sized gourd with seeds, used as a musical instrument by shaking."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hue puruwai"@en .

aat:300456742 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Māori group dance, or other choreographed Māori performance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kapa haka"@en .

aat:300456743 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Compasses that track the direction and position in which the stars rise and set on the horizon, providing knowledge needed for wayfinding."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "star compasses"@en .

aat:300456744 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Maori star compasses, used for navigation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kāpehu whetū"@en .

aat:300456745 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of medium-sized heron, pale, slightly bluish-gray in color, with yellow legs and white facial markings; found throughout Australasia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Egretta novaehollandiae"@en .

aat:300456746 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Short, broad-bladed Maori weapons used to jab an opponent in the body or the head; a symbol of chieftainship."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mere"@en .

aat:300456747 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Order containing at least 9 extinct species of flightless birds native to New Zealand. Recent genetic studies reveal that their closest relatives are the flighted South American tinamous. According to Māori tradition, moa were swift runners that defended themselves by kicking when cornered. Early Polynesian peoples hunted moa for food and made spear points, hooks, and ornaments from their bones and water carriers from their eggs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dinornithiformes"@en .

aat:300456749 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Instruments comprising a small gourd with holes, which is attached to a string and twirled to create a sound like that of birds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "poi āwhiowhio"@en .

aat:300456750 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Poi instruments made of corn husks."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "poi kānga"@en .

aat:300456751 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Poi instruments made of fish skin."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "poi ika"@en .

aat:300456752 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Brazilian art movement, that emerged from the Concrete Art movement in Latin America and internationally. Neo-Concrete artists emerged from Grupo Frente. They rejected the purism of Concrete Art and created works that were less scientific, and more phenomenological observations, with greater sensuality, color, and poetic feeling. The group was active from 1959-1961. Their manifesto was written by Ferreira Gullar, and was signed by Amilcar de Castro, Ferreira Gullar, Franz Weissmann, Lygia Clark, Lygia Pape, Reynaldo Jardim and Theon Spanudis. It was published on 22 of March 1959 in Journal do Brazil."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Neo-Concrete"@en .

aat:300456753 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Type of multicolor granite that is fine-grain and easily split into planar sheets, which are so smooth that no cutting, dressing or polishing is required."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "seam-face granite"@en .

aat:300456754 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of seam-face granite quarried in Weymouth, Massachusetts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Weymouth granite"@en .

aat:300456756 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Use for references to identities assigned to people who did not exist; examples are false names assigned to a forged work of art and fictitious literary characters."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fictitious people"@en .

aat:300456757 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who build houses, or who oversee the building of houses. Usually used in historical contexts, where the person was both designer and builder or overseer, and the construction was wood frame."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "housewrights"@en .

aat:300456758 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Digital images of all or part of what is displayed on a computer screen or mobile device, captured at a particular moment in time and saved on a drive as an image file, for documentation or other purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "screenshots"@en .

aat:300456759 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The technique of capturing images directly from television screens with a camera, as a document, or as a source of found images."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "television photography"@en .

aat:300456760 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographs produced using the technique of capturing images directly from television screens with a camera, as a document, or as a source of found images."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "television photographs"@en .

aat:300456761 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Generally refers to small settlements of indigenous people; specifically may refer to settlements and tracts of land in the western United States that were federally recognized and allotted for Native Americans to live, primarily established between 1906 and 1910. These Native populations had been driven to near extinction, made landless and often homeless by non-native migration into the western states, especially after the mid-19th century."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rancherias"@en .

aat:300456762 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Statistical geographic entities representing permanent and/or seasonal residences for Alaska Natives who are members of, or receiving governmental services from, the defining Alaska Native Village located within the region and vicinity of the Native Village's traditional and/or historic location."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Alaska Native village statistical areas"@en .

aat:300456763 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Statistical areas in the state of Oklahoma, identified and delineated by the United States Census Bureau and federally recognized tribes, that had a former American Indian reservation in the state. Boundaries are those of former reservations, except where modified by agreements with neighboring tribes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Oklahoma Tribal Statistical Areas"@en .

aat:300456764 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Library collections that are generally grouped by a common provenance, from a collector or other source, that may or may not be specific to a topic or field of study."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "named collections"@en .

aat:300456766 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Chagong 插栱, which cannot be found in traditional Chinese literature, is a term translated by Liang Sicheng from the Japanese sashihijiki 挿肘木. Sashihijiki refers to the bracket arms of the outer eaves of the South Gate of Todaiji Temple in Nara, Japan, which are directly inserted into the pillar shaft (Tanabe, Liang (trans.) 1932)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chāgǒng"@en .

aat:300456770 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pupaifang is a long, horizontal architectural element placed on top of the pillar top tie beam (lan’e 闌額) atop which the dougong 斗栱 are located. The term pupaifang is found in the chapters on pingzuo 平坐 (lit. “leveling seat”) and xiaomuzuo 小木作 (lit. “small woodwork”) in the Yingzao fashi (1103). In extant buildings, however, pupaifang were not limited to use in pingzuo and xiaomuzuo. Based on information in other textual records, it is presumed that the use of the term pupaifang in the Yingzao fashi may have come from a transcription of the craftsmen's spoken language (Fang 1894, 2.14b; Guo 2003, 12). In the Qing dynasty, the term pingbanfang 平板枋 was used instead of pupaifang. Currently, architectural historians use this term when describing buildings from the Liao and Song through Ming dynasties (907-1644)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pǔpāifāng"@en .

aat:300456775 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Juzhe, literally “raise\" and \"bend, bow” (Kroll 2015, 226, 596), is a method of positioning the purlins in relation to each other in order to manipulate the slope and curvature of the roof. In premodern literature, the term juzhe only appears in the Yingzao fashi. According to this text, a building’s juzhe is determined through a process called ding ceyang 定側樣 (deciding the side appearance) or dian caojia 點草架 (designating the rough structural frame). During this process, the roof frame would be drawn on a wall plane at a scale of 1:10 (Li 2009, 5.9b)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jǔzhé"@en .

aat:300456776 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The term dishui 滴水 (literally “dripping water”) as an architectural term is first found in Ming dynasty literature. The texts explain that dishui are located at the end of the eaves and that the end of the tile “hangs down,” but there is no description of the specific shape. Recent research and existing examples indicate that the term dishui can be understood in both a narrow and a broad sense. When modern scholars define dishui, they are usually referring specifically to the plate tile with a “ruyi”-shape 如意形 descending from the lowest end of the gutter tiles, especially in the context of Qing architecture. Thus, dishui can be used to distinguish Qing style buildings from Song style buildings, the latter of which more commonly employed “multi-lipped plate tiles” (chongchun banwa 重唇瓪瓦),\" as described in the the Song dynasty Yingzao fashi (Liang 1981, 85; Yao 1986, 111; Pan and He 2005, 157). More generally, dishui can be used to refer to any eaves gutter tile with an end that hangs down."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dīshuǐ"@en .

aat:300456778 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The term jiashan 假山, literally “simulated mountain,” refers to a man-made mountain built from rocks and/or earth, that is usually located in a garden. Beginning in the Ming dynasty, jiashan in private gardens increasingly had interior spaces, and large jiashan constructions continued to be popular throughout the Qing period. The term jiashan first appeared in the mid-Tang dynasty and the earliest example of jiashan is found in the title of a poem by the Tang dynasty literatus Quan Deyu 權德輿 (759-818 CE). It was widely used in the Tang and Song dynasty literature to refer to small “mountains” made of stone and placed in gardens or even on desks. In this context, jiashan were thought to abstract the form of real mountains and bore a strong literati style."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jiǎshān"@en .

aat:300456780 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An object transfer via purchase by a named party from another party in a sale that is not publicly advertised and/or available."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "private sales"@en .

aat:300456783 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning, literally, to raise and build up, jujia is the method of positioning the purlins within the roof frame, layer by layer, in order to determine the pitch of the roof. The term mainly appears in the Gongcheng zuofa (1734) and Yingzao suanli edited by Liang Sicheng, so it can be considered part of the official practice of the Qing dynasty. Different from juzhe 舉折 described in the Song dynasty Yingzao fashi (1103), jujia is the process of determining the height of the roof ridge based on the heights of the purlins as calculated from the bottom (eaves) to the top (ridge). Here jugao 舉高 refers to the height that a purlin is lifted relative to the one below it. The jugao of each purlin is determined by the percentage of the distance of bujia 步架, the distance between two adjacent purlins. For instance, “65 ju” ( 六五舉) means that the height of jugao is 65% that of the bujia 步架. The first step is to determine the height between the top of the neighboring pillars, guazhu 瓜柱 post, or tuodun 柁橔 brace. Then add the appropriate height of pingshui 平水, and this will result in the appropriate position for the purlin. As outlined in the Yingzao suanli, the calculation of jugao differs according to the number of purlins in a given building. Few actual buildings correspond exactly to the method of jujia outlined in the Gongcheng zuofa and Yingzao suanli, indicating that the height of the purlins could be increased or decreased according to specific circumstances rather than always rigidly following a single standard that was documented in these texts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jǔjià"@en .

aat:300456784 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The term paishan goudi 排山勾滴 combines the words paishan, referring to the practice of aligning rooftiles (pai 排) along the slope of the gable end (shanmian 山面, lit. “mountain (peak)” “side”), and goudi, which refers to the goutou 勾頭 and dishui 滴水 capping tiles. In placing more emphasis on practice of paishan, it is also called “capping the gable-end tiles” (lingdang paisha 鈴鐺排山; lit. aligning bell clappers along the gable end) (Liu 1932, 193; Wang 1995, 31)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "páishān gōudī"@en .

aat:300456785 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pingbanfang is a tie beam with a rectilinear section that is placed on top of the pillar top tie beam (e’fang 額枋) to support dougong 斗栱 bracket sets (Liang 1981, 77). The term pingbanfang is equivalent to the Song dynasty term pupaifang and is most commonly found in the Gongcheng zuofa (1734) and other books published in the Qing dynasty. Architectural historians use this term to describe buildings from the Ming and Qing dynasties."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "píngbǎnfāng"@en .

aat:300456786 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The term xiaomuzuo (lit. “small woodwork”) initially appeared in literature of the Song dynasty. Of the thirteen specialized categories in the Yingzao fashi, xiaomuzuo is one of two regarding timber. The second is damuzuo 大木作 (lit. “large woodwork” or “carpentry”). The section on xiaomuzuo in the Yingzao fashi comprises six chapters (juan 6-11), which include 42 entries of various practices, such as doors and windows (menchuang 門窗), partitions (geduan 隔斷), ceilings (zaojing 藻井), Buddhist and Daoist cabinets (Fo-Dao zhang 佛道帐), and well-pavilions (jingtingzi 井亭子). The distinctiveness of entries in each category makes it difficult to summarize their common characteristics (Xu 1957, 2995, 3108; Li 2009, juan 6-11). In the Qing dynasty, the term xiaomuzuo was no longer recorded in documents relating to the construction of imperial buildings. In Jiangnan and other places, xiaomuzuo specifically refers to wooden artifacts, furniture, and interior decoration (Jiang 1894, 12.29b; Zhang and Wang 1935, 84a). It can be seen that the meaning of xiaomuzuo developed alongside changes in building practices and construction techniques."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "xiǎomùzuò"@en .

aat:300456788 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The term angxing shuatou refers to any shuatou made into the shape of an \"ang,\" which is usually an oblique or angled element. The term originated from Liang Sicheng's descriptions of buildings and has been widely adopted by scholars as the mainstream name for describing this type of shuatou (Liang 1955, 105-28). The earliest image of an angxing shuatou appears in the wall paintings of the High Tang (ca. 8th century) Cave 172 at Dunhuang (Yu 2017; Xiao 2003, 232). During the Song dynasty, the angxing shuatou was already widespread in northern China, but not in the south. The term does not appear in the 1103 CE Yingzao fashi (Zhang 2015)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ángxíng shuǎtóu"@en .

aat:300456789 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lou is generally understood to mean a multi-story building (two stories or more) or a loft-building (Kroll 2015, 281) elevated on a terrace or city wall. Historically, lou as a type of traditional architecture usually designates a building elevated either by means of multiple stories or man-made or natural platforms. The Eastern Han dynasty (25-220 CE) classic Shuowen jiezi defines lou as a structure with a layered wu 屋 (a roof, or a roofed structure)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lóu"@en .

aat:300456791 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Linggong is a type of bracket arm in dougong (bracket sets) that is parallel to the building’s facade. According to the Yingzao fashi, the linggong is equal in length to the huagong (Li 2009, 4.3b). Based on its location, the linggong can be divided into two types. When used under the eaves, the linggong is located above the uppermost layer of huagong or ang (whichever is on the top) and supports tie beams that in turn support the eaves. When used elsewhere in the building, linggong are located beneath the purlins. Linggong usually support the purlins through an intermediary timber member called a timu 替木 (Pan and He 2005, 99)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lìnggǒng"@en .

aat:300456793 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Citang is a type of building for performing sacrificial rituals to family ancestors, well-known people, religious figures, and nature spirits in traditional China. Citang 祠堂 is a compound word, in which ci means “sacrifice” and can be used as a verb, while tang is a noun indicating a type of building. The term citang as a whole means “a building used for worship” and was first found in the Western Han dynasty (206 BCE-9 CE) history Han shu (Ban 1962, 2948; 3085)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cítáng"@en .

aat:300456795 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bujian puzuo is the Song dynasty (960-1279) term for dougong 斗栱 placed between two pillars and on top of a lan'e 闌額 or a pupaifang 普拍方 (Chen 2010, 331). It is considered to be equivalent to pingshenke 平身科 used in the Qing dynasty Gongcheng zuofa (Gongbu 1734, 15.1a). In premodern sources, the term bujian puzuo appears to be used only in the Yingzao fashi (1103). Modern architectural historians commonly use it to describe the intercolumnar bracket-sets of buildings from the Tang (618-907) through the Ming dynasties (1368-1644)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bǔjiān pùzuò"@en .

aat:300456796 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Chuji 出際 is the name of a roofing structure used in both shaliangtou (hip-gable) and bushaliangtou (overhanging gable) roofs in which the purlins extend beyond the wall plane so that the roof overhangs at the two gable ends. Meaning, literally, \"protruding beyond the limit\" (of a gable wall), chuji can also refer to the part of the roof overhanging beyond the gables, and in that use it is synonymous with the term wufei 屋廢 (lit. “roof end”) (Chen 2010, 126; Pan and He 2005, 61). The term chuji is only found in the Yingzao fashi. In the Qing dynasty, the distance at which the roof protruded from the gable walls was usually determined by the length of the entire purlin rather than only the part extending beyond the gable wall surface. Perhaps as a consequence, the term chuji was no longer commonly used."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chūjì"@en .

aat:300456798 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning, literally, “resisting platform” (Kroll 2015, 82, 440), ditai is a platform structure used for lookout and defense. Based on location and function, ditai can be divided into three categories: ditai in city walls, ditai in the Great Wall, and freestanding ditai. Although the forms and development of these three types of ditai differed, they affected each other to a certain extent. According to the current historical documents, the term ditai first appeared in the Ming dynasty (1368-1644) text Ming Taizong Wen huangdi shilu 明太宗文皇帝實錄 (Zhongyang yanjiuyuan lishi yuyan yanjiusuo 1963, 125.5a)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dítái"@en .

aat:300456799 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning “ornate” (or “blossoming”) “end,” huafei 華廢 refers to the covering of the verges, the part of the roof beyond the vertical ridges of a gable end, with roof tiles, whereby the capping tiles (huatou tongwa 華頭㼧瓦 and chongchun banwa 重唇瓪瓦) are aligned to be perpendicular to the vertical ridges and parallel to the roof ridge. The term is found in texts as late as the Song dynasty (960-1279) (Li 2009, 13.2a; 13.4b) and is written huafei 化廢 (lit. “transforming the end”) in some literature (Guo 1368, 1.8b). Modern scholars are generally of the opinion that the tiles aligned perpendicular to the vertical ridges outside the two gables should be called huafei 華廢 (Liang 1983, 256; Chen 2010, 298)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "huāfèi"@en .

aat:300456800 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The earliest known use of the term huagong 華栱, literally, “blossoming bracket-arms” (Kroll 2015, 167 (huā), 135) or “flower arms” (Fu 2017, 357), is in the Yingzao fashi (YZFS 4.2b-4.3a). As an architectural term, huagong refers to any bracket arm in the bracketing structure that projects away from the wall plane."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "huāgǒng"@en .

aat:300456801 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning, literally, “trimming (the) edge” (Kroll 2016), in traditional literature the only reference to jianbian 剪邊 as an architectural term is in the Yingzao fashi (1103). Jianbian refers to the part of the tiled roof extending beyond the wall plane in which the plate tiles are arranged parallel to the edges along the slopes. According to the Yingzao fashi, jianbian are usually used in ordinary buildings (Li 2009, 13.4b). Jianbian have not been found in extant buildings contemporary with the Yingzao fashi, but it can be seen in paintings of architecture from around the same time, such as in “Pictures of Gentlemen” (Gao Shi Tu 高士圖) by Wei Xian 衛賢 (fl. 10th cen.) and “Along the River During the Qingming Festival” (Qingming Shanghe Tu 清明上河圖) by Zhang Zeduan 張擇端 (fl. early 12th cen)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "jiǎnbiān"@en .

aat:300456802 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pingshui (lit. “level water”) can be defined as a method of compensating for the height difference between the purlins on the front/back (zhengmian 正面) and sides (shanmian 山面) of buildings with hip (wudian 廡殿) or hip-gable (xieshan 歇山) roofs, often by adding a board (dianban 墊板) above the pillar to lift the purlins."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "píngshuǐ"@en .

aat:300456803 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Dipeng (lit. “foundation shed”) (Kroll 2015, 84, 338) was described in the small-scale carpentry (xiaomuzuo 小木作) section of the Yingzao fashi as a raised wood floor system designed for granaries and storehouses during the Song dynasty (Xu 1978, 123; Zhu 1848, shengbu 2.23b-24a; Duan 1815, 6.49b; Pan and He 2005, 134). The primary purpose was to create a wooden framework to elevate the floor above the ground to protect stored goods from humidity and groundwater (Xu 1957, 2727). Mention of dipeng used in common residential architecture can be found in textual sources after the Song dynasty, suggesting a more widespread use of this system (Huang 1629-1644, 15.14b; Lü zu zhi 1522-1620, 3.8b)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dìpéng"@en .

aat:300456805 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Duntai, meaning “mound” or “platform” (Kroll 2015, 96, 440), constitutes a high platform used to look out for the enemy and convey military signals. The front-most post in the beacon transmission system, duntai were mostly used along inland borders such as the Great Wall, as well as in seaside and riverside border defense. They were also constructed in military training grounds for military practice. Duntai were usually built on high ground and in open places and were spaced about two li (li=approximately ⅓ mile) apart. The duntai was also the place where “mound troops” (dunjun 墩軍) lived."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dūntái"@en .

aat:300456806 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lankan gouchuang 闌檻鉤窗 (“balustrade bench and railing window”) is a kind of window supported by a balustrade-like series of posts crowned with a built-in bench for leaning or sitting (kanmianban 檻面版, “railing-top plate” or “railing cap”). Outside the window is an ornate railing supported by goose-neck (鵝項 e’xiang (arched)) balusters which are topped with small cloud-shaped brackets (yungong 雲栱) that hold the support rail (xunzhang 尋杖). This is called a “cloud-bracket goose-neck link-railing” (yungong e'xiang goulan 雲栱鵝項鉤闌). Based on current knowledge, the only extant pre-modern use of this term is in the Yingzao fashi (1103; Li 2009, 7.3b-4b) where it is categorized under xiaomuzuo 小木作 (“small woodwork” or “fine carpentry”). The construction method of the lankan gouchang is recorded in detail in this text, including an accompanying drawing in later chapters (Li 2009, 32.7b)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lánkǎn gōuchuāng"@en .

aat:300456808 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning, literally, a \"plaque\" (-holding) \"commemorative monument,\" paifang is a structure that functions to commemorate virtuous people and actions, markoff an area, and/or delineate space. Paifang adopt a wide variety of forms and are made of wood, stone, or brick. They are usually composed of two or more pillars connected by tie beams, which are covered by roofs called lou 樓. Paifang are often erected on streets and bridges, as well as near buildings such as government offices, schools, ancestral halls, temples, and tombs. The name of each paifang and the reason for its establishment were often inscribed on a plaque affixed to the tie beam and were sometimes inscribed directly onto the tie beam itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "páifāng"@en .

aat:300456809 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Meaning, literally, \"plaque\" (-holding) \"tower\" (Kroll), the pailou functions to mark specific locations, divide space, and commemorate virtuous individuals and actions. The ‘lou’ in pailou can refer to a roof or eaves (Guo 2002, 61), however, in Ming dynasty (1368-1644) literature, the term pailou was widely used and was often used interchangeably with the term paifang. For example, in the Jiajing Dezhou zhi 嘉靖德州志 (ca. 1545 CE), pailou are recorded under the chapter on paifang, indicating that pailou and paifang refer to the same objects (Zheng and He 1522-1566, 1.13a). The term paifang was often used to emphasize why the structure was erected, while the term pailou was used to emphasize the location. These structures were so symbolic in their appearance that they became the markers of specific places. Junctions composed of multiple paifang structures at road intersections were often found in cities, such as the “Four Pailou” and “Three Pailou” in Nanjing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "páilóu"@en .

aat:300456810 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The term pingzuo 平坐 (also written pingzuo 平座; lit. “level” “seat” Kroll 2015 346, 636) has a variety of meanings in classical Chinese literature beyond the scope of architecture, including a sitting posture (Li 1995, 36), a certain kind of sitting apparatus (Xu and An 2004, 2444; Zhou 2000, 12515), and a base (Gu 1984, 12.186). In the Ming and Qing dynasties (14th-20th centuries), pingzuo was also used to refer to a seat or throne (Zhang 1981, 58). All these meanings emphasize that pingzuo is a structure designed to provide a level base for whatever is on top of it. Current architectural historians focus on the use of pingzuo as described in the Song court architectural manual Yingzao fashi (1103 CE) in the context of timber-framed structures. Here the basic characteristic of pingzuo is a platform supported by bracket sets on top of pillars, providing a level foundation for a building constructed above it. The pillars supporting the pingzuo can be erected on top of a lower story, directly into the ground, in the water, or on top of city walls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "píngzuò"@en .

aat:300456812 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The term chidao, literally “speed” and “way” (Kroll 2015, 51, 79), was first used in the Qin dynasty (221-206 BCE) to refer to national expressways for the emperor's tours and military affairs. The roadways were first established in the Warring States Period (475-221 BCE); in the Qin dynasty, the First Emperor (Qin Shihuang) expanded them from Xianyang to all parts of the country. New roads were built during the Han, Sui, and Ming Dynasties. Chidao were very wide and were divided into three lanes, with the middle lane being exclusively used by the emperor. After the Eastern Han dynasty (25-220 CE), chidao had two meanings. (1) Chidao located near the imperial palace were strongly associated with imperial authority and the term was gradually used interchangeably with “middle road” (zhongdao 中道), “imperial road” (yudao 禦道), and “defensive road” (yulu 禦路). (2) Between cities and counties, the military and transportation functions of chidao were emphasized. During the Ming and Qing dynasties and into the twentieth century, the term chidao was used generally to refer to common roadways (Ban 1962, 2242, 2888; Wei 1974, 68; Zhu 1984, 705; Wang 2012)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chídào"@en .

aat:300456814 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The term dingtougong 丁頭栱, literally meaning T-shaped (half) bracket arm, is only found in the Yingzao fashi (1103). In this text, dingtougong is categorized as a type of huagong 華栱 (lit. blossoming bracket arm) measuring half the length of a standard huagong, which is inserted into a pillar or a bracket cluster. If it is inserted into a corner of the interior of a building, it can be called xiaxugong 蝦須栱 (lit. “shrimp antenna bracket arm” YZFS 4.3a). As a type of huagong, dingtougong functions as a corbel, projecting outward to support the components above it (Xu and Guo 1984, 114)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dīngtóugǒng"@en .

aat:300456817 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Shuatou, literally, “mocking” “head” or “top” (Kroll 2015, 424, 458), is a component part of the dougong (bracket-set) located above the uppermost layer of huagong or ang. The shuatou protrudes from the center of the dougong, intersecting with the linggong, which is perpendicular to it (Liang 1981, 22). In the Qing dynasty Gongcheng zuofa the timber element equivalent to the shuatou is called “grasshopper head” (mazhatou 螞蚱頭) (清工部 2018, 196). This element might also be called “initial-cut head” (qieji tou 切幾頭) when the end is cut into a basic rectangular shape (Pan and He 2005, 84)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "shuǎtóu"@en .

aat:300456818 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Digital inkjet prints that use pigment-based ink rather than dye-based ink to create an image. These are often additionally created with high-quality fine art papers or other media as substrates for longevity and are considered archival."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pigment prints"@en .

aat:300456819 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In nations with a federal system, buildings that house local offices of various federal government departments and agencies."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "federal buildings"@en .

aat:300456820 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographs made by dissolving light-sensitive bitumen in oil of lavender and applying a thin coating over a polished metal plate, or other substrate. The process was invented ca.1822 by Joseph Nicéphore Niépce, and is considered the first process to produce a permanent photographic image from a camera projection."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "heliographs"@en .

aat:300456821 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes text that appears on Web pages that provides an equivalent of what would be presented by an image. This appears in HTML as the 'alt text' label. Alternative text can be provided at the same time as images when building Websites. Screen readers may read 'alt text' aloud, and present text when images fail to load."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "alternative text"@en .

aat:300456822 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Public buildings that house federal-level judicial and administrative facilities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "federal courthouses"@en .

aat:300456823 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Projects involving planning and execution of significant engineering and construction activities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "engineering projects"@en .

aat:300456824 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Built works or other structures that have been moved from one location to another."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "relocated architecture"@en .

aat:300456825 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Visual works, including architecture, that have been begun by the makers but was not completed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "unfinished works"@en .

aat:300456826 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Incense pot, usually clay, widely found in Gambian homes and used to burn incense in order to provide protection from spirits."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "andichurai"@en .

aat:300456827 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Negatives made from a line copy and suitable for use in making a line plate, these do not render gradient tones or a grayscale."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "line negatives"@en .

aat:300456829 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bead-like pieces of pure gold, typically incuse with designs or markings, used as currency and said to have facilitated specific transactions such as in rituals, religious or high value settings. The coins were used in the Mataram kingdom in Java, Indonesia and found subsequently in neighbouring polities such as Bali and Philippines. These coins have also been found in Southern Thailand. Piloncitos is a term given to these coins retrospectively."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "piloncitos"@en .

aat:300456830 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Shoes, sandals or slippers which body or a significant part of the footwear is elaborately decorated with beads. The beads, which are not always but typically sewn on, usually decorate the footwear with patterns or motifs such as geometric patterns, floral and animal motifs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "beaded shoes"@en .

aat:300456831 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gifts presented by the groom’s family to the bride’s family during a betrothal ceremony, as part of pre-wedding customs. In Asian cultures, the gifts serve as a token of appreciation to the bride’s family for raising their daughter, and a show of the groom’s sincerity in marrying her. Betrothal gifts typically have symbolic and auspicious meanings associated to them, and may include food items, tea, jewellery and bride price."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "betrothal gifts"@en .

aat:300456832 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Peranakan Chinese covered jars which are oval with a symmetrical tapering profile. The lids (covers) commonly but not always, have a funnel-shaped knob at the top. Chupu are usually designed with Peranakan Chinese motifs which have auspicious symbolisms such as peonies, phoenixes, the eight auspicious Buddhist emblems, butterflies, and other flowers. The jars can be used for double-boiling foods such as soups. They are also used as ceremonial vessels for Peranakan Chinese weddings. Smaller ones were used to store beauty products as well."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chupu"@en .

aat:300456833 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gifts prepared by the bride’s family for the bride to bring with her to the groom’s family at the time of marriage. The gifts serve as a form of financial security for the bride, and their amount and quality typically reflect the social status of her family. Dowry items are usually used to help the newlyweds establish their new household, and may include money, property or goods such as furniture, clothing and everyday objects. Some cultures may also prepare chests, baskets or other containers to accommodate the dowry items."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dowry items"@en .

aat:300456834 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A Peranakan Chinese bulbous, tub-like container with a dome-shaped lid that usually can be found in Peranakan households. The bodies of the kamcheng may have small handles, and the lids, may be topped by an auspicious animal design. Kamcheng can be found in a range of sizes. Kamcheng are usually designed with Peranakan Chinese motifs which have auspicious symbolisms such as peonies, phoenixes, the eight auspicious Buddhist emblems, butterflies, and other flowers. The term “kamcheng” in Hokkien (a Chinese dialect) means “covered jar”. It is an important ware in Peranakan Chinese wedding ceremonies. On a day-to-day basis, a kamcheng is used for serving and storing different types of food such as pickles and rice. Smaller kamcheng have also been used to store cosmetic products."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kamcheng"@en .

aat:300456835 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pairs of shoes or slippers with handmade beaded faces, typically worn by Peranakan communities in various Southeast Asian countries. The intricate designs of the beaded faces are handsewn using specific coloured glass or metal seed beads onto fabric stretched over a wooden frame. Common decorative motifs for kasut manek include florals, birds, and geometric patterns. “Kasut manek” are typically worn with the sarong-kebaya and on special occasions such as weddings, as part of the Peranakan wedding outfit. Kasut manek may be closed or open-toed and could also be designed with heels."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kasut manek"@en .

aat:300456836 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Close-fitting, oval-shaped caps with a flat crown and made of felt, cotton or velvet. Usually worn by Muslim males in many Southeast Asian countries, especially during religious, customary or formal events."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "songkok"@en .

aat:300456837 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tiered cylindrical containers consisting of usually two to four separate containers of the same shape and size, which are stacked one on top of the other within a frame that has a handle. Tiffin carriers are used for carrying cooked food, with different dishes placed in the separate containers. Traditionally, these stacked containers are held together by the carrier handle which frame is fitted through the small side handles of each container and fastened with a catchment at the top. Presently, there are various designs of tiffin carriers. Tiffin carriers are usually made from stainless steel or enamel. Tiffin carriers, as they are called in Singapore and Malaysia, are used in other parts of Asia and have their own local names."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tiffin carriers"@en .

aat:300456839 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to text-based description of images. These texts offer the equivalent information that a sighted reader would get when looking at a picture, but intended as an accessibility aid for those who are blind or visually impaired. Image descriptions may be in an image caption, in a text post, or shared in a text link for extended descriptions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "image description"@en .

aat:300456840 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A printmaking process that uses two or more blocks, one often as a key or line block in black and others in a single colors in differing tones. Tone blocks were printed in lighter and darker versions of the same color, a characteristic of the 'camaieu' print as resembling engraved cameo stones. Early scholars distinguished two-block from multi-block processes by calling them \"camaïeu,\" that is, the ‘monochrome method,’ distinguishing them from \"chiaroscuro woodcuts,\" Current usage does not make this distinction, and the terms have been used interchangeably in various sources."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chiaroscuro woodcut"@en .

aat:300456841 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Anything that is created, produced, or generated using artificial intelligence, including texts, codes, images, and etc."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "AI-generated content"@en .

aat:300456842 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The practice or method of using AI to generate, create, or produce something such as images, texts, codes or the like."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "AI generation"@en .

aat:300456843 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Structures forming the heart of a shrine complex, connected to the rest of the shrine although raised on a higher level; protected from public access, sacred spaces where the deities reside."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hondens"@en .

aat:300456844 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Edible starchy tuberous roots of various plants of the genus Dioscorea."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "yams"@en .

aat:300456846 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Order of flowering plants comprising three families, 22 genera, and about 850 species."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dioscoreales"@en .

aat:300456847 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Family of flowering plants comprising about 715 known species in nine genera."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dioscoreaceae"@en .

aat:300456848 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus of flowering plants comprising over 600 species."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dioscorea"@en .

aat:300456849 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Political divisions of nations or other adminstrative entities, where the division is totally or primarily comprised of an island."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "island divisions"@en .

aat:300456850 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In design, the positioning of two elements set back-to-back, such as two identical figures facing in opposite directions. Examples are in heraldry and architectural ornament. For two figures facing each other, use \"affronted.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "addorsed"@en .

aat:300456851 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In design, the positioning of two elements set facing each other, such as two mirror-image figures facing toward each other. Examples are in heraldry and architectural ornament. For two figures set back-to-back, use \"addorsed.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "affronted"@en .

aat:300456852 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Lacquerware formed on clay, plaster and wooden bodies, on which grass or silk cloth and raw lacquer are mounted layer by layer for drying, before the original form is removed, leaving the shaped lacquered cloth. This is then polished, and decorated with more lacquer, paint, or otherwise."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bodiless lacquerware"@en .

aat:300456853 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of large historical agricultural wagon with sides consisting of horizontal ladders attached to the loading bed for hauling hay, grain, or other materials. The sides could be removed to suit other purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ladder wagons"@en .

aat:300456854 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Casts that have been created from molds taken directly from an existing work. After-casts are therefore made using the indirect lost-wax process, or in some instances by sand casting. The term is normally applied to bronzes, but may be applied to other types of casts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "after-casts"@en .

aat:300456856 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the surface of a cast object immediately after removal from a mold, before fettling and chasing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "as-cast surfaces"@en .

aat:300456857 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Repairs consisting of a localized cast of molten copper alloy to fill cavities or other casting defects. Cast-on repairs may fill a void in a sculpture or secure a separately formed patch or element to the cast."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cast-on repairs"@en .

aat:300456858 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Unintended imperfections on a bronze that occurs during casting and appears as a more or less subtle discontinuity in the desired form and is associated with either a lack or an excess of metal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "casting defects"@en .

aat:300456859 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Methodological tools that have been developed by researchers to reverse engineer the casting sequence of a bronze sculpture and visually represent the separately cast parts. It is based on the evidence presented in the object and attempts to map the decisions made by a foundry regarding the number and position of separately cast pieces."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "casting plans"@en .

aat:300456860 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of metal insertion placed in the gap between the core and the outer mold as a spacer to hold the core in place during a casting operation. A number of these are placed strategically throughout the mold. They are most often made of an alloy similar to that of the surrounding metal, as they will become embedded in the cast. In modern foundries, chaplets are mainly used in sandcasting, but they have been encountered in historic lost-wax casting as well."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chaplets"@en .

aat:300456861 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "French term for replicas of the artist’s model (often in bronze) that is used to make molds for the production of large editions in sandcasting. In rare cases, a chef-modèle has been used in lost-wax casting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chef-modèles"@en .

aat:300456862 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Act of using a chisel and hammer to remove metal. With each strike of the hammer the tool jumps, often leaving a visible “step.” Chiseling may be part of either fettling or chasing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chiseling"@en .

aat:300456863 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In casting, interfaces where two streams of metal come together in a mold but do not fuse properly, often due to premature cooling of the metal in the mold. A cold shut may also describe a hole or void in a cast caused by premature cooling. The cooled metal edges will be rounded in profile."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cold shuts"@en .

aat:300456864 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Those portions of refractory molds that define the internal spaces in hollow bronze sculptures. These may be formed in a variety of ways and are usually (but not always) made of similar material as that used for the outer portion of the mold. The term is also used as shorthand to refer to the material it is made of. A core is generally solid but may be hollow."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cores"@en .

aat:300456865 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Metal rods, nails, or wire that is embedded in both the core and the outer mold of a cast that serves to secure the core in place during a pour. Core pins have traditionally been made of copper alloys, iron, or steel, and today are generally made of stainless steel."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "core pins"@en .

aat:300456866 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes a variety of metal or other features that reinforce or support the core during the casting process. This term is used in different ways depending on the context. In industry, it is applied to metal inserts or spacers that hold the core in place during the pour; therefore, core pins, chaplets, and mold extensions are examples of core supports. In cultural contexts, the term is applied to internal wires or rods that help strengthen the core during assembly of the casting model and/or during the pour. Generally reserved for smaller wires in projecting limbs, or wires and rods used to strengthen joints between separately molded wax sections in the indirect lost-wax process. For clarity, when the second definition is intended, the term “internal core support” should be used."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "core supports"@en .

aat:300456867 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ability of a liquid metal to fill and pick up every detail of a mold."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "castability"@en .

aat:300456868 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The areas or compilations of changes made to a built work in order to restore its original appearance."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "restorations"@en .

aat:300456869 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The areas or compilations of changes, up to and including the complete replacement of a built work."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rebuilding"@en .

aat:300456870 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The areas or compilations of changes to fix, correct, and repair a work of art or architecture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "repairs"@en .

aat:300456871 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Unsprung four-wheeled vehicles pulled by a pack animal with an open platform for the transport of flax bundles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "flax wagons"@en .

aat:300456872 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People who, after cleaving or sawing, cut off the rough edges of a diamond and further shapes the stone."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bruters"@en .

aat:300456873 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sleds used specifically for freight transport. These are pulled by a person or a pack animal with slides for the transport of loads."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cargo sleds"@en .

aat:300456874 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Monstrances with cylinder-shaped, glass thecas where the consacrated host is kept."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cylinder monstrances"@en .

aat:300456875 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small pump devices used to administer enemas."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "clysopumps"@en .

aat:300456876 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tools consisting of a steel pin encased in a wooden handle. These may be used by a metalworker at a lathe, for example, to press a silver or copper plate against a rotating wooden mould. Spinning mandrels have various head shapes, with which different forms can be pressed into metal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "spinning mandrels"@en .

aat:300456877 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Glass objects that have a function in everyday life and are not explicitly made as an artistic expression."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "utility glassware"@en .

aat:300456878 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Round or oval sand-filled leather cushions used as a base when engraving silverware or stamps. An engraving block or an engraving board is placed on this, making it lie firmly but still easy to turn."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "leather sandbags"@en .

aat:300456879 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Typical Frisian money chests given by a husband to his fiancée during an engagement ritual. These are usually small and rectangular and are often made of silver. The surface of the chest is usually decorated or engraved and sometimes contains a text."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wedding caskets"@en .

aat:300456880 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Glass or plastic supports on which images are created using a screen process. With this process, additive color images are created using a screen of red, green, or blue filters to record the relative intensity of each hue on a panchromatic, black-and-white emulsion which is processed to form a transparency. When viewed through a similar filter, each filter contributes its hue with its intensity modulated by the silver image."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "additive color screen plates"@en .

aat:300456881 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small cologne boxes hinged in two halves and that contain a sponge soaked in perfume water. The lodderein box differs from other silver boxes in that its shape is always higher than wide, this to prevent the perfume from evaporating too quickly. The name lodderein is a corruption of \"eau de la reine (de Hongrie),\" a special type of perfume, related to eau de cologne."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lodderein boxes"@en .

aat:300456882 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Half metal balls, which are filled with sand and covered with a layer of pitch. These form the basis for making chasing tools: the pitch fixes the object and forms a springy surface. By heating the pitch ball, the object is released. There are various recipes for making chasers pitch, but black or brown pitch is always the main ingredient."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pitch bowls"@en .

aat:300456883 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographic films on which additive color images are created using a screen process. This process uses a screen of red, green, or blue filters to record the relative intensity of each hue on a panchromatic, black-and-white emulsion which is processed to form a transparency on film. When viewed through a similar filter, each filter contributes its hue with its intensity modulated by the silver image."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "additive color screen films"@en .

aat:300456884 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Additive color screen plates introduced by Agfa Company on the German market in 1916. There are four generations of this additive color screen process on plate: Agfa Farbenplatte (1916-1923), Neue Agfa Farbenplatte (1923-1932), Agfacolor-Platte (1932-1938) and Agfacolor Ultra (1936-1938)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Agfacolor plates"@en .

aat:300456885 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Additive color screen films introduced by Agfa Company on the German market in 1916. There are two generations of this additive color screen process on film: Agfacolor-Film (1932-1934) and Agfacolor Ultra (1934-1938)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Agfacolor films"@en .

aat:300456886 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Additive color screen plates invented in England in 1913 by the Paget Prize Plate Company."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Paget plates"@en .

aat:300456887 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Additive color screen plates invented in France in 1910 by Louis D. Dufay."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dufaycolor plates"@en .

aat:300456888 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Carbon prints in which the transfer paper is sensitized when placed in contact with a silver bromide print. The term is generally used for colored carbro prints, but monochrome carbro prints also exist."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "carbro prints"@en .

aat:300456889 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Carbro prints that are permanent color pigment prints. Three separate carbro transfer sheets are made, each corresponding to one of the subtractive primaries (cyan, magenta, and yellow); the sheets are transferred in register to a single support to form the image."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "three-color carbro prints"@en .

aat:300456890 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Color prints produced through three black-and-white separation negatives. In this process, yellow, magenta and cyan gelatine films sensitized with silver bromide are transferred to a common support after exposure through the three negatives. The Duxochrome system is an improvement of the Pinatype. The materials were on the market from 1929 to 1963."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Duxochrome prints"@en .

aat:300456891 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "An improved version of the ozotype. Using different chemicals and enlarging paper, it is possible to print from smaller negatives. Ozobrome is the precursor of the carbon print. Contrary to ozotype, with the ozobrome and carbon print the final image is on the transfer paper, and not on the original carrier."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ozobromes"@en .

aat:300456892 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographic prints on quadrichrome carbon paper with a small tonal range. Colored grains are visible in the image. This process is used from 1951."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fresson prints"@en .

aat:300456893 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gum prints combined with another process, typically platinum. The highlights and mid-tones are formed using a platinum print. A gum dichromate print is placed on top to enhance the shadow areas. Combination gum dichromate prints are rare."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "combination gum dichromate prints"@en .

aat:300456894 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Style and culture of the dependency of Saint Martin, the French depency on the island of Saint Martin."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Saint Martinoise"@en .

aat:300456895 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Style and culture of the dependency of Sint Maarten, the Dutch depency on the island of Saint Martin."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sint Maarten"@en .

aat:300456896 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gum bichromate prints in which two or more superimposed differently pigmented gum layers produce a color image."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "color gum dichromate prints"@en .

aat:300456897 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographic prints made by transferring oil prints that are still wet to a secondary support."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "oil transfer prints"@en .

aat:300456898 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographic prints made using a light-sensitive emulsion of a ferric salt and silver nitrate. When developed, a metallic silver image is formed; various developers can produce black, brown, purple, or maroon tones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kallitypes"@en .

aat:300456899 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographic prints made using an iron process in which a mixture of platinum and palladium is reduced from salts to form an image."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "platinum-palladium prints"@en .

aat:300456900 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Photographic or photomechanical prints in which a grid acts as a barrier to alternately reveal and obscure parts of the image, thereby creating the illusion of movement. Photographic prints of two or three phases of a depicted motion are assembled into one print. An animated picture is produced through a striped transparent overlay across the interlaced image. The order can also be reversed, with lined paper as the bottom layer, over which a transparent film with the photo is moved. It is the predecessor of the lenticular print. Examples include the Magic Moving Pictures card by G. Felsenthal & Co or the Ombro-Cinéma toys."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "barrier-grid prints"@en .

aat:300456901 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Simplified versions of carbon prints. These use silver bromide paper to make a contact print. These prints were then placed against pigment paper and through a chemical developer and warm water, and a pigmented print was created on the bleached original carrier. A double transfer, used for carbon printing, was no longer necessary."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ozotypes"@en .

aat:300456902 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Women entertainers for the Korean royal court, as well as in provincial centers. Traditionally, gisaeng were educated women trained in music, dance, calligraphy, and poetry, while at the same time they were from a low social class, and are often referred to as sex workers or courtesans, especially during the Japanese colonial era. It is generally agreed that the appearance of gisaeng began in the Goryeo dynasty (918-1392), where they acted primarily as government servants. By the time of Korean liberation in 1945, the practice formally disappeared."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gisaeng"@en .

aat:300456903 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Building projects intended to restore or renovate built works."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "restoration projects"@en .

aat:300457618 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Joins between two metal parts that are undertaken using molten metal. Examples of metallurgical joints include those made using welding, brazing, soldering, and interlock casting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "metallurgical joints"@en .

aat:300457619 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Models or replicas of an artist’s model around which a mold is formed for bronze casting. It is made by the foundry in order to preserve the artist’s model. A foundry model may also be used as a reference for the finishing of a bronze for the purpose of quality control."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "foundry models"@en .

aat:300457620 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Replica in wax obtained from the reusable mold of an original model. Inter-models are used in indirect lost-wax casting. Inter-models are often slush molded. One inter-model may vary from another through additions or changes made in the wax before the investment is applied."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "inter-models"@en .

aat:300457621 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ridges of excess metal that can occur when molten metal enters cracks in the refractory mold (both outer and core). Flashing most often rises perpendicularly to the inner or outer wall of bronze, although a gap in consecutive layers of the mold material may result in thin flanges of excess bronze that spread parallel to the metal wall. This type of feature can appear on the cast’s internal or external surfaces (respectively called “core flashing” and “mold flashing”), and in the latter case is often removed during fettling. Flashing may also occur along seam lines of an ill-fitted piece mold."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "flashing"@en .

aat:300457622 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the refractory molds used in the lost-wax casting process or to the material used to make that mold. It also denotes the process of coating or embedding the wax model in this material and is applicable to clay-based, plaster-based, and ceramic shell molds. In all of these, the first layers have a special, fine consistency that is designed to pick up the detail and avoid problems during casting; the later layers are coarser. Clay-based investment may also be referred to as “loam.” The investment is destroyed in the process of casting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "investments"@en .

aat:300457623 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers specifically to the reproduction of a once-living form (either plant or animal) that results in a cast characterized by its high realism and fine detail. Life-casts are made by encasing the form in a refractory mold and burning out the form, and are therefore generally solid, though there are some exceptions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "life-casting"@en .

aat:300457625 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Architectural style of Modernist or International Style origins, tailored to the indoor-outdoor lifestyle and aesthetics of desert communities, particularly Palm Springs, California."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Desert Modernism"@en .

aat:300457626 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Descriptive text meant to identify collections of works or visual materials, often by an individual, such as photographs or prints, issued or housed together in a portfolio."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "portfolio titles"@en .

aat:300457628 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes where an individual has carried out significant actions or primary locations of activity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "active place"@en .

aat:300457629 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes the geographic location where an individual was born."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "birthplace"@en .

aat:300457630 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes the geographic location where a group or association was formed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "formed place"@en .

aat:300457631 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes the geographic location where a group or corporate body was dissolved."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dissolved place"@en .

aat:300457632 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes the physical location where a collection was amassed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "collection place"@en .

aat:300457633 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes the physical location where an individual died."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "death place"@en .

aat:300457634 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes a location depicted in a visual work."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "place depicted"@en .

aat:300457635 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes the physical location where a specific activity or work was realized."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "place executed"@en .

aat:300457636 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes the physical location where an object or work was produced."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "production location"@en .

aat:300457637 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Describes the physical location where a work was prepared, printed, or otherwise made available for distribution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "publication place"@en .

aat:300457641 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tianhua 天花 refers to a type of ceiling in traditional Chinese architecture, and its use was most common in the Ming and Qing dynasties. Literally meaning “heavenly flowers,” the semantic range for the compound is quite rich."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tiānhuā"@en .

aat:300457644 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Píngqí 平棊, meaning literally “level game board,” is a kind of ceiling in traditional Chinese architecture. The term is used to describe the ceiling of a palatial type hall (dian 殿) in Yingzao fashi 營造法式 (1103). Modern scholars usually use it to refer to the ceilings of buildings constructed in the Tang, Liao and Song dynasties. In the Ming and Qing dynasties the term pingqi panding 平棋盘顶 was used instead of píngqí. The Chinese character 平 (ping) means flat or level, while the character 棊 (qi) means the game weiqi 圍棋 (“Chinese chess” or “Go”) or, by extension, the orthogonal-grid pattern of a game board. The implication is that pingqi is a kind of ceiling with a chessboard pattern. The term pingqi panding 平棋盘顶 has the same meaning."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "píngqí"@en .

aat:300457645 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Literally, wo 窩 refers to a recessed shelter or safe hiding place and pu 鋪 can refer to a bed, and perhaps by extension, an inn or station, such as a postal station. In the Tang dynasty (618-907) pu 鋪 was associated with military security, meaning “a place for guards”. As a compound architectural term, wopu usually refers to a simple military facility where soldiers could reside and store equipment. Wopu were mostly built on city walls, but also on riverbanks, campsites, and other areas where temporary quarters were needed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wōpù"@en .

aat:300457648 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Liujin douke is one of the bracket set (dougong 斗栱 or douke 斗科) types used in Ming and Qing dynasty official architecture. According to the Gongcheng zuofa (1734) the term liujin douke refers to a bracket set that connects the eaves purlin (yanheng 檐桁) to the interior purlin (jinheng 金桁) via one or more slanted timbers (chenggan 秤桿). Literally meaning “flowing gold bracket set,” it can be used in any of the three eaves bracket sets types, i.e. the inter-columnar bracket set (pingshenke 平身科), the corner bracket set (jiaoke 角科), and the pillar-top bracket set (zhutouke 柱头科). Here the word jin 金 refers to the interior purlin (xiajinheng 下金桁), while liu 溜 can be understood as a verb meaning “to slide”. It might also be understood as “a row\" (pai 排). The slanted timber bar echoes the form of the Song dynasty ang 昂, but it functions less for roof load transfer and more as a stabilizing component locking the eaves to the interior structure. Modern scholars have used the term liujin dougong 溜金斗栱 to refer both to the liujin douke and tiaojin douke 挑金斗科."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "liūjīn dǒukē"@en .

aat:300457649 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Bridge or spacer made of refractory material between a core and the outer mold in casting. It serves a double function as core support while also providing better air flow into the core to aid in its drying before the bronze is poured."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mold extensions"@en .

aat:300457650 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Decorative element resting on the surface of a sculpture via any of a variety of attachment methods, including solder, adhesives, cements, and/or rivets. Overlay materials may include a range of materials, among them metals, glass, stone, or bone."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "overlays"@en .

aat:300457651 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of repair in a cast metal object that fills a flaw that extends through the full thickness of the metal wall, specifically those that occur with the removal of core pins and armature rods. Circular plugs are often threaded in order to mechanically lock them in place."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "plugs"@en .

aat:300457652 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A common type of casting flaw that includes a group or area of cavities caused by shrinkage or trapped gases. Porosity may vary considerably in dimension and may or may not break through thesurface of the cast object."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "porosity"@en .

aat:300457653 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Temporary, heat-resistant, cohesive, porous molds that capture the fine impression of a model to be reproduced and forms the void into which a molten material will be cast. Investment, green sand, and ceramic shell are examples of refractory molds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "refractory molds"@en .

aat:300457654 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Joints between two separately cast metal elements in which one element slots into the hollow “sleeve” of the other. The joint is generally further secured using pins, rivets, or by soldering."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Roman joints"@en .

aat:300457655 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Faintly raised lines that form at the joints between mold sections when a molten material or slurry is poured into a mold. Seam lines are found on plaster or wax casts as well as on bronzes cast in piece molds. In ancient Chinese bronzes, some seam lines were exaggerated and integrated into the design of the casts. The term also refers to the line along which the pieces of a refractorypiece mold join, which is the locus of the line that forms on a bronze. Depending on how well the piece mold pieces fit together, the seam line may be more or less raised. More extreme flashing occurs with ill-fitting pieces."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "seam lines"@en .

aat:300457656 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Channels that feed molten material to a mold, in contrast to a vent,which lets air escape. Both sprues and vents make up the “sprue system,” which circulates molten material from a pouring cup through a refractory mold and allows air and casting vapors such as steam to be released. In lost-wax casting, “sprue” is the term used for the solid wax rods (rarely reeds or terra-cotta pipes) used to create the channels in the mold. Sprues are also the solid metal that has filled the channels upon cooling, which is generally removed during fettling."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sprues"@en .

aat:300457658 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Construction of dwellings that follows a standard for energy efficiency, particularly construction that is certified to meet criteria for heating, cooling, and other energy demands in addition to airtightness. These standards often include reliance on solar heating and insulation to create low-energy buildings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "passive house construction"@en .

aat:300457659 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In the context of property loss from fire, defensible space is a buffer between a structure and a surrounding landscape. Adequate defensible space acts as a barrier to slow or halt the progress of fire that would otherwise engulf property by clearing plants or objects from the perimeter of a structure. It also helps ensure the safety of firefighters."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "defensible space"@en .

aat:300457660 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Vegetation that is considered less likely to combust from flame or other ignition sources. These may be damaged or killed by fire, but may not contribute to fueling the fire itself."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fire resistant plants"@en .

aat:300457661 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Wall assemblies or segments of walls for buildings that resist high temperatures and limit the spread of flames. These follow code to resist flames for specific amounts of time, and are constructed using materials that have high fire resistance made to withstand extremely high temperatures without combusting, and feature construction details that limit air exchange and other factors."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fire-rated walls"@en .

aat:300457662 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Plate glass produced typically by subjecting annealed to glass to high temperatures and rapid cooling, causing the outer and inner layers to cool at different rates, the inner layer staying in a state of tension, and the outer in a state of compression, increasing its impact and thermal strength many times. When surface failure occurs, tempered glass shatters into very small pieces, causing less potential harm from sharp edges. It is used for a variety of applications where safety standards are in place, including windows for cars or homes, shower doors, or tables. It is prized in architectural contexts for its thermal properties and durability."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tempered glass"@en .

aat:300457663 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The concept of a supernatural force, typically a deity, causing a person to experience creative desire. The inspiration may be to the extent that the deity describes the specifications for the work to be created. The deity may be said to have expressed their wishes in a dream or vision, or in another fashion."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "divine inspiration"@en .

aat:300457664 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Equipment that provides a means for passengers and aircraft personnel to traverse between aircraft doors and the ground or another surface outside the vehicle. These may be towed, self-propelled, or in some cases are integral to the aircraft."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "boarding stairs"@en .

aat:300457665 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Type of traditional gardens and large houses, where the house surrounds a courtyard garden; the garden is typically symmetrical in plan with a central fountain. Generally in Morroco or based on the Morrocan style."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "riads"@en .

aat:300457667 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Built works cosntructed primarily of cast iron, including commercial buildings warehouses, bridges, and others. Fourished during the Industrial Revolution, from the late 18th century through 19th century."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cast iron architecture"@en .

aat:300457668 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The nationality, culture, or style of the Hakka people, an ethnic group of China, considered to be a branch of the Han. Originally from North China, but migrated to South China (especially Guangdong, Fujian, Jiangxi, and Guangxi provinces) during the fall of the Nan (Southern) Song dynasty in the 1270s."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hakka"@en .

aat:300457669 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Member of certain hereditary nobility, including duke, marquess, earl, viscount, or baron; or a person elevated to the peerage for life, without hereditary rights. Primarily in Britain or Ireland."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "peers"@en .

aat:300457670 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gardens that are located within walls, hedges, fencing, or the arcades of a cloister; usually associated with an architectural structure. The traditional design of an enclosed garden includes a site split into four quarters separated by paths, and a fountain, well, or pool at the center; the earliest-known such gardens of this design were found in Persia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "enclosed gardens"@en .

aat:300457671 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Distinctive furnished dollhouses created for and by women; flourished in 17th-century Netherlands; may be seen as women's miniature couterparts for the curiosity cabinets collected by men."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pronk poppenhuisen"@en .

aat:300457672 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Art works, such as paintings, sculptures, masks, or other works, that are created by shamans engaged in healing or spiritual development through symbolic and practical actions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "shamanic art"@en .

aat:300457673 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Cemeteries placed within a large rural landscape, planted and laid out with winding paths."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rural cemeteries"@en .

aat:300457675 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Residences of the chief executive officer of a governmental entity; often used for the home of the governor of a U.S. state."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "executive mansions"@en .

aat:300457676 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In a society, people who have a high level of competence in reading and writing, especially when they represent a relatively small percentage of the population; usually refers to historical rather than modern societies, and professions such as priests, officials, clerks, merchants, poets, and artisans."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "literate elites"@en .

aat:300457677 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In a society, people who are successful in military endeavors and hold high status due to this, especially when they represent a relatively small percentage of the population; usually refers to historical rather than modern societies."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "warrior elites"@en .

aat:300457678 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "People in a society who hold a special rank and position in which they read, write, and interpret sacred or special texts. Examples are the class of lector scribes in Ancient Egypt."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lector scribes"@en .

aat:300457679 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small, decorative daggers typically worn hanging from or through a sash or belt, often as part of a ceremonial costume or military uniform, and as a symbol of rank or custom; may feature an ornate handle and blade design."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sash daggers"@en .

aat:300457680 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Artisans who apply a patina to surfaces or objects, usually metal, by using chemicals and heat to create a colored surface layer; sometimes to create the appearance of age or weathering."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "patinators"@en .

aat:300457681 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Strategy-based board game played on a checkered board of 16x6 squares that can be played by two or four players. The number of game pieces and rules of play vary based on the number of players. The game was invented ca.1880 by George Howard Monks."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "halma"@en .

aat:300457682 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings that house students, including apartments, dormitory rooms, or single family dwellings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "student residences"@en .

aat:300457683 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Governmental agencies or professional associations that exercise jurisdiction over activities in a licensing and regulating capacity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "regulatory bodies"@en .

aat:300457684 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Microphones designed for underwater use. These detect acoustic signals under the water, as conventional microphones pick up signals through the air. Most hydrophones use ceramics that produce electrical current when subjected to changes in underwater pressure, and are normally omnidirectional."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hydrophones"@en .

aat:300457687 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Fretted electric stringed instruments based on the same principle as the electric guitar, invented by Emmett Chapman in the early 1970s. It is most commonly strung with 10 to 12 strings and is played by tapping and fretting the strings instead of plucking. There are many variations of the instrument, including bass models."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chapman sticks"@en .

aat:300457689 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Experimental musical instruments in the friction idiophone family invented by Hans Reichel ca. 1980s. These are made of wood, and consist of a 'tongue' that is fixed in a block that contains one or more contact microphones. The tongue is bowed with a conventional stringed instrument bow while simultaneously pressing down on the the tongue with another piece of shaped wood called a 'dax,' thus achieving various tonalities or notes. The tongue can also be excited by plucking. The shape of the tongue, and the type of wood also influence the sound."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "daxophones"@en .

aat:300457691 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Territorial subdivisions of a state. Chiefly in Latin American nations, these administrative units are composed of cultural, natural, or economic regions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "comarcas"@en .

aat:300457692 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Functional requirements, criteria, rules, or codes established by an authority that are intended to establish common usage, meaning or semantics of data by its owners and users."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "metadata standards"@en .

aat:300457693 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Standard for encoding archival finding aids using the Standard Generalized Markup Language (SGML). The standard is maintained in the Network Development and MARC Standards Office of the Library of Congress (LC) in partnership with the Society of American Archivists."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "EAD"@en .

aat:300457694 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The style and culture of the colonial Viceroyalty of New Spain, which existed from 1519 to 1821 and encompassed the territory that is now Mexico."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "novohispano"@en .

aat:300457695 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The style and culture of the colonial Kingdom of New Granada, which existed from the 16th century to the early 19th century and encompassed the territory that is now Colombia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Neogranadian"@en .

aat:300457696 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The style and culture of the colonial Viceroyalty of Rio de la Plata, which existed from the 17th century to the early 19th century and encompassed the territories that are now Argentina, Paraguay, Bolivia, and Uruguay."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rioplatense"@en .

aat:300457697 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Tsnungwe, a federally-recognized tribe indigenous to parts of what is today Trinity and Humboldt counties, California; specifically, the Tsnungwe are indigenous to areas along the South Fork Trinity River, Willow Creek, Salyer, Burnt Ranch, and New River. The name of the tribe in their local language is tse:ningxwe. \"tse:ning\" refers to Ironside Mountain."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tsnungwe"@en .

aat:300457698 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the culture of the Nisenan, a group indigenous to Northern California, specifically areas along the foothills of what are today Nevada, Sierra, Yuba, Sutter, and Placer counties. While historically the Nisenan have been classified as part of the Maidu people, the tribe corrects this mis-identification in their literature."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nisenan"@en .

aat:300457699 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Acjachemen (also known as Juaneño), an indigenous people originally inhabiting what is now Orange county, California, as well as parts of San Diego, Los Angeles, and Riverside counties."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Acjachemen"@en .

aat:300457700 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the federally recognized Pit River Tribe. The Pit River Tribe is comprised of eleven separate bands: Ajumawi, Atsugewi, Atwamsini, Ilmawi, Astarawi, Hammawi, Hewisedawi, Itsatawi, Aporige, Kosalektawi, and Madesi. The indigenous homeland of the Pit River Tribe is spread across the California counties of Shasta, Siskiyou, Modoc, and Lassen."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pit River"@en .

aat:300457701 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Chimariko, an indigenous people whose ancestral lands are located along the Trinity River in northern California."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chimariko"@en .

aat:300457702 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Wailaki people, an indigenous people whose ancestral lands were along the Eel River, near Round Valley, California."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wailaki"@en .

aat:300457704 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Anishininew, an indigenous people of Canada whose lands span from the provinces of Ontario to Manitoba. In the past, the Anishininew were named \"Oji-Cree,\" referring to people whose culture and language result from a blend of Anishinaabe and Cree. However, the Anishininew consider themselves uniquely separate from both."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Anishininew"@en .

aat:300457705 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Japanese folklore, tools or household objects that have acquired a kami or spirit."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tsukumogami"@en .

aat:300457706 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Peoria, an Algonquian-speaking people whose ancestral land was located east of the Mississippi River and north of the Ohio River. They were assigned land in \"Indian Territory\" after the Omnibus Treaty in 1867; today they are a federally-recognized tribe with headquarters in Miami, Oklahoma."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peoria"@en .

aat:300457707 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus of flowering tree having over 200 species, located in the tropical Americas, Africa, and Asia; known for edible fruit and hard, heavy wood."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pouteria"@en .

aat:300457708 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of evergreen tree native to tropical South America, known for yielding hard heavy timber and edible ovoid or round fruit."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pouteria caimito"@en .

aat:300457709 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Skokomish, an indigeous people of the northwest United States. The Skokomish were originally members of the largest of a group of Twana communities, however, those descendents are now part of the Skokomish community."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Skokomish"@en .

aat:300457710 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Small replicas of objects signifying an honor bestowed upon an individual; usually worn on the person. These correspond to an original honor decoration and are used on certain occasions and uniforms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "miniatures"@en .

aat:300457711 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Cords of silk or other fine material used to hang honor decorations and orders around the neck. The colors and characteristics of the cords may indicate positions and specialties."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "suspension cords"@en .

aat:300457712 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Ribbons of various widths, colors, and compositions from which medals and decorations hang. These complement the decorations and orders and may indicate being awarded the same distinction more than once."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ribbons"@en .

aat:300457713 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Straight bars or clips which are generally made of the same metal as a medal; they have engraved data on their surfaces that are allusive to the decoration and complement it. These may be located on the decoration itself or the ribbons from which the decorations hang."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bars"@en .

aat:300457714 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Devices that attach one or more decorations (miniature or full-size) to a uniform or clothing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "medal bars"@en .

aat:300457716 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Designated spaces within wineries, breweries, or distilleries where visitors can sample the products made there."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tasting rooms"@en .

aat:300457717 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tall structures designed to support antennas or other equipment for transmitting radio and television signals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "broadcasting towers"@en .

aat:300457718 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Notebooks in which photographers note important details of the creation of a photograph whether in the field or the darkroom."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "photography log books"@en .

aat:300457719 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Collections of electronic mail, stored as records, or otherwise held separately from active mailbox locations. These may be held in cloud storage or on electronic storage devices due to the nature of the medium."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "email archives"@en .

aat:300457721 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Spaces in Buddhist practice where people gather to chant scriptures, recite mantras, and conduct other rituals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chanting halls"@en .

aat:300457722 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Spaces in Buddhist practice where people gather to perform zazen, or seated meditataton."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "meditation halls"@en .

aat:300457724 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the M'Chigeeng First Nation, a part of the Ojibwe First Nation band, centrally located on Manitoulin Island in Ontario, Canada."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "M'Chigeeng First Nation"@en .

aat:300457725 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Books having leaves that are relatively rigid, and that may be in non-traditional forms. Often used to refer to board books or books whose leaves are glued together as part of the binding process, though the concept of board books is distinct."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stiff-leaf book structures"@en .

aat:300457727 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Single sheets of paper which are cut and folded to form books."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "single-sheet books"@en .

aat:300457729 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Letterpress prints characterized by soft edges and a range of tonalities accomplished in a single run. The print is made by placing a flexible, layered matrix behind the printing sheet which is then fed into the grippers to pull an impression from a type-high, inked surface. The matrix performs as elaborate make-ready that applies more pressure in thicker areas and less in thinner. Often, pressure prints introduce visual noise around the central image."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pressure prints"@en .

aat:300457731 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Letterpress printing technique that utilizes a flexible, layered matrix that is placed behind the printing sheet to pull an impression from a type-high, inked plate. The plate is most often a solid, uncut surface.�"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pressure printing"@en .

aat:300457733 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Plates or surfaces that hold marks or information necessary to produce an image or text on a substrate, producing a print or reproduceable multiple, traditionally by applying ink to the matrix before running through a press, but digital files may be considered a type of matrix as well when used in the 3D printing process, for example."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "matrices"@en .

aat:300457735 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Term typically applied in the context of letterpress printing, referring to localized or micro-adjustments made to a matrix or to cylinder, platen, or tympan packing to remedy issues with irregular impressions. The adjustments typically consist of torn or cut paper or other thin material being placed under or over a matrix (ie. on the press bed or on the cylinder, platen, or tympan) to increase pressure in an area with lighter or no impression. The term may also refer to the activity of preparing for printing including proofing and other adjustments to pressure, ink, etc."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "make-ready"@en .

aat:300457737 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Black-and-white or monochrome photographs that have color pigments manually applied to the surface. Referred to as hand-coloring or hand-tinting or hand-painting, colorants, usually dye, ink, or watercolor are applied lightly to the surface, such that the photograph beneath is still visible. Color is typically applied to selected areas of the photograph but may also be applied overall."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hand-colored photographs"@en .

aat:300457739 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sets of containers and implements fitted in a box or tray, to store and prepare ingredients for the practice of betel chewing. A typical set might include containers for storing betel leaves, sliced areca nuts, lime paste and gambier, a nut cutter, a spatula for scooping the paste, a mortar and a spittoon."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "betel chewing sets"@en .

aat:300457741 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sets which consist of a kettle and a pot usually with a perforated flat lid used by people from cultures that dine using their right hands, to wash their hands before and after meals. The kettle sits on the perforated lidded pot and is placed at the dining place. The kettle is filled with water and diners pour the water over their hands into the perforated lidded pot to wash their fingers. Hand washing kettles come in different designs and shapes, ranging from simple to elaborate and are made from materials such as stainless steel, brass or plastic. These are common to Southeast Asia and other parts of the world."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hand washing kettles"@en .

aat:300457742 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Textiles produced by the Malay people in Southeast Asian countries, in which gold or silver coloured threads are woven into a base fabric, typically silk or cotton, creating raised patterns on the fabric. The textiles are usually decorated with geometric, floral or symbolic motifs which bear cultural meanings. Kain songket was historically worn only by royalty but has evolved to be worn as formal attire during festive events, traditional and religious occasions by Malays in Brunei, Indonesia, Malaysia and Singapore."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kain songket"@en .

aat:300457743 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Periods of restricted movement, physical interaction and access into common and/or public spaces enforced by an authority to contain emergencies for public safety and health."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lockdowns"@en .

aat:300457747 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Tsartlip First Nation, located on the Saanich peninsula on Vancouver Island, British Columbia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tsartlip First Nation"@en .

aat:300457748 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Process where the owner or inhabitant has directly organised the design and construction of their own home or other building."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "self-build housebuilding"@en .

aat:300457749 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Process where the owner or inhabitant may make various choices in the final design of the home, based on a general model or set of possibilities that are provided. Variations of a custom build include homes on a serviced plot, shells, and turnkey."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "custom housebuilding"@en .

aat:300457751 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Andean refers to the cultures, styles, and expressions rooted in the Indigenous cultural heritage of the Andes, the continental mountain range of South America that transcends political boundaries. It encompasses both the native peoples of the pre-Hispanic era and the contemporary inhabitants of the region."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Andean"@en .

aat:300457752 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Houses or other buildings that slot into a tight space between two older buildings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gap houses"@en .

aat:300457753 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Semiahmoo, an indigenous people and culture of Canada and the Pacific Northwest of the United States, and considered a Coast Salish subgroup."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Semiahmoo"@en .

aat:300457754 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the indigenous people of the Aleutian island chain and the Pribilof Islands. This group is distinguished from the Alutiiq or Sugpiaq culture, also known as 'Aleut,' from Kodiak Island, Prince William Sound, outer Kenai Peninsula, and parts of the Alaskan Peninsula. The people prefer the term 'Unangax̂' to refer to themselves."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unangax̂"@en .

aat:300457755 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Built works that manifest landscape and ecology, utilizing properties usually associated with the natural environment, such as continuity with surrounding topography, green roofs, artificial mountains, and geological forms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "landform buildings"@en .

aat:300457756 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Camps offering supervised programs focused on social, recreational, and educational activities for children. They may include day camps, overnight camps, or longer-term camps."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "children's camps"@en .

aat:300457758 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Arches that are curved in the shape of a parabola, following a plane curve that is mirror-symmetrical and is approximately U-shaped."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "parabolic arches"@en .

aat:300457759 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Living organisms that are unique to geographic locations and cannot be found anywhere else on earth."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "indigenous species"@en .

aat:300457760 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Living organisms that are not unique to a geographic location, having been introduced intentionally or unintentionally to habitats other than their own, often with harmful or invasive impact to an environment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "non-native species"@en .

aat:300457761 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of a group of First Nations indigenous peoples, largely residing in the interior of British Columbia, Canada, with some territories extending into the northern part of Washington state, in the United States. Generally, the Interior Salish peoples are: the St'át'imc (also Lillooet), the Secwepemc (Shuswap), the Nlaka'pamux (Thompson), Sinixt, and Okanagan First Nations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Interior Salish"@en .

aat:300457762 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The consideration of complex ecological environments as systems, rather than as assemblages of individual species. The well-being of an ecosystem depends on the functioning of its parts, and on higher-order phenomena such as population dynamics, community structure, and landscape processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ecosystem health"@en .

aat:300457763 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Penelakut, an indigenous, Coast Salish, First Nations people primarily residing on Penelakut island."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Penelakut"@en .

aat:300457765 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Process of dismantling buildings or other large constructions in a way that allows the reuse of materials and minimum negative impact on the surrounding structures or environment. It prioritizes salvaging materials for reuse or recycling. It is in contrast to the traditional demolition processes that use heavy machinery to raze structures and dump the debris in landfills."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "deconstruction"@en .

aat:300457766 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Works comprising outdoor spaces that are designed and often have a relationship to buildings or other constructions. Designs may incorporate plants, water features, and land forms. Landscape design works may be outdoor areas around a built work, large or small parks, gardens, urban developments, and projects for natural restoration by altering or removing built works."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "landscape design"@en .

aat:300457767 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Complexes planned or used for multiple purposes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mixed-use complexes"@en .

aat:300457768 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Building projects for urban or other built-community environments. For projects to renovate existing urban environments, use \"urban renewal projects.\" For the movable work drawing or other rendering of the project, use \"plans (orthographic projections)\" or \"plans (maps).\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "urban planning projects"@en .

aat:300457769 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Mobile phones that have the ability to access the internet, send text messages, make calls, or store data such as music, but that lack the advanced functionality of smartphones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "feature phones"@en .

aat:300457770 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Senior-level architects who lead a team and ensure project coherence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lead architects"@en .

aat:300457771 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Licensed architects or architectural firms that are responsible for the design and construction documents for a building project."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "architects of record"@en .

aat:300457772 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Lekwungen (lək̓ʷəŋən) people, a Coast Salish subgroup who primarily reside on southeastern Vancouver Island. In the past, the term \"Songhees\" was used to generally refer to the Lekwungen people, however Lekwungen and lək̓ʷəŋən are preferred, as the Lekwungen are represented by both the Songhees and Esquimalt (Xwsepsum) First Nations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lekwungen"@en .

aat:300457773 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Salishian (or, Salish) language with several dialects spoken on southern Vancouver Island."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Northern Straits Salish"@en .

aat:300457774 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Dialect of Northern Straits Salish; scholarship denotes that the term Lekwungen (lək̓ʷəŋən) was once preferred for the culture and Songish was preferred for the dialect. However, more recently, the Songhees and Esquimalt First Nations are preferring Lekwungen (lək̓ʷəŋən) and referring to Songish as a historic term for the language."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lekwungen"@en .

aat:300457775 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the members of the Esquimalt Nation, a First Nation band of Lekwungen people (a Coast Salish subgroup)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Esquimalt"@en .

aat:300457776 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Architectural style that developed particularly in Italy in the 1920s and 1930s, that emphasized a scientific and rational approach to design, prioritizing functional structures, geometric forms, and a rejection of ornamentation. It is based on theories that emerged during the Renaissance with Vitruvius, in the Enlightenment, and in Neoclassicism."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Rationalist"@en .

aat:300457777 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the members of the Songhees Nation, a First Nation band of Lekwungen people (a Coast Salish subgroup). At one point, the term 'Songhees' was used for all Lekwungen people, but now refers to those who are members of the Songhees Nation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Songhees Nation"@en .

aat:300457778 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Large houses or estates in Spain, Portugal, and Latin America in the country or on the outskirts of a town; may be associated with vineyards or cattle ranches."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "quintas"@en .

aat:300457779 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Building plan having a central square or rectangular space with four semi-circular apses or niches projecting from each side. Notable examples are in baptisteries and some churches."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "quadriconco plan"@en .

aat:300457780 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Stoney Nakoda, an indigenous people of Canada and the United States, particularly Montana. They are culturally connected to the Assiniboine and usage overlaps. The Stoney Nakoda Nations are: Bearspaw First Nation, Chiniki First Nation, and Goodstoney First Nation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stoney Nakoda"@en .

aat:300457783 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "French art movement founded in Paris in 1978. It quickly attracted international artists. It emerged as a reaction to the official discourse, which no longer promoted figurative art and instead favored abstract art. The movement rejected this trend, asserting that art should not be limited to a specific message or medium. Art did not need to carry an explanatory idea, but instead should express a critical stance toward dominant values. The movement organizes an annual salon."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Critical Figuration"@en .

aat:300457784 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Setbacks or tiers in architecture, particularly in tall buildings, where each tier is set back from the one below in a symetrical or regular design, resembling the layers of a wedding cake."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wedding-cake setbacks"@en .

aat:300457785 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Burial places having elaborate tombs, funerary monuments, mausoleums, and often other architectural features."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "monumental cemeteries"@en .

aat:300457786 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Stz'uminus First Nation and its members, a Coast Salish people whose traditional territory is located on east Vancouver Island."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stz'uminus First Nation"@en .

aat:300457787 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Montauk, an indigenous people whose ancestral land includes the eastern end of what is now Long Island, New York."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Montauk"@en .

aat:300457788 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Art objects acquired unjustly or without sanction. This may include objects stolen, confiscated, sold by force, exchanged or renounced during armed conflicts, revolutions or in a colonial context."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "looted art"@en .

aat:300457789 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Placing goods in a transportation vessel such as a ship, airplane, or automobile. The concept may be extended to fixed locations such as warehouses. In general this is done in a manner that takes up the least space for economic reasons and that limits the risk of damage."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stowing"@en .

aat:300457790 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "European avant-garde doctrine or school of art that existed between 1932 and ca. 1960. The musicalists attempted, influenced by the works of František Kupka and Auguste Herbin, to ‘compose’ abstract paintings, and incorporated musical concepts such as ‘harmony’ and ‘resonance’ in their compositions. Characteristic for their works are vibrant colours and rhythmic lines. The driving force and main theorist behind the movement was French artist Henry Valensi, other associates were Charles Blanc-Gatti, Louise Janin, Marcel Lempereur-Haut and Gustave Bourgogne. The musicalists often held expositions in the Galerie Renaissance in Paris."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Musicalist"@en .

aat:300457791 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Communication form which takes place through images, objects, gestures and/or colors. Examples of visual communication forms may include video or photographs, graphs, diagrams, infographics or other forms of data visualization, typography, maps, illustrations and graphic design, etc."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "visual communication"@en .

aat:300457792 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Discipline that focuses on the presentation of information using visual and written elements. Communication design is a broad concept covering various disciplines, such as advertising, branding, web design, user interface design, information design, and social media. Communication designers focus on the strategy, content, and context of their messages, and how they can influence the behavior, attitude, and perception of their audience. Communication design uses research, analysis, storytelling, and user testing to create effective and engaging visual solutions that meet the needs and goals of clients and users."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "communication design"@en .

aat:300457793 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Type of European red-firing slipware, the creation of which can be situated in the second half of the sixteenth and first half of the seventeenth century. The pottery exhibits a red or pinkish-red color with a standing surface and decorations applied by means of a sludge-scratching technique. Werra slipware seems to have been a reaction of German potters to North Holland mud pottery. Examples have been found at sites in Germany and the Netherlands. The name derives from the river on which it was transported and where examples are most often found."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Werra ware"@en .

aat:300457794 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Relation between respective parts or between parts and the whole of the human body, whether considered purely visually or numerically. The rendering of body proportion plays an important role in achieving realistic forms of depiction, which has made it a focal point in artists' training, and also for practical purposes such as the design of apparel."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "human proportion"@en .

aat:300457795 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Dances that have a ceremonial or ritual function. Characteristic are movements that are continuously repeated that may reflect a form or inducement of worship, prayer, visionary insights, or healing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dance rituals"@en .

aat:300457796 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Actions required to arrive at a design. A design method is a structured approach or method for designing and building objects or structures. The techniques of a design process may include: defining problems, conducting research, brainstorming and conceptualizing, prototyping, selecting and finalizing, product analysis and improvement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "design methods"@en .

aat:300457797 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The aggregate of movement including people and means of transport, specifically in the public space and context of a city."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "urban traffic"@en .

aat:300457798 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Conducting moral reflections with 'the good' as a criterion. In the European cultural climate from the 16th century onwards, bourgeois morality increasingly influenced Christian morality. Moralizing paintings emerged in which messages advocating for forms of morality can be read."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "moralizing"@en .

aat:300457799 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Technology or craft of modelling clay which is then hardened into pottery and stoneware by heating in a kiln or the sun. Ceramics is used as a collective term for all materials made by heating clay. These include pottery, stoneware and porcelain, depending on the type of clay and the temperature at which they were baked. The results of pottery can be utilitarian objects, or purely decorative objects."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "pottery making"@en .

aat:300457800 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In the context of heraldry, short texts that reflect significance to the bearer, often placed above or below coats of arms, usually on a scroll. Mottos may appear in the blazons of noble families, in institutes, knightly orders, abbeys and chapters, monastic orders and congregations, clerics, universities, cities and states. These are traditionally rendered in Latin, but other languages are also common."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mottos"@en .

aat:300457801 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings in which the floor levels of adjacent living areas differ by less than a full story."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "split-level buildings"@en .

aat:300457802 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A lithographic technique where portions of the stone or plate that are to remain without color are covered with gum arabic, which protects from the effects of grease, and that is lightly tinted with ink so it may be seen on the stone while drawing. This layer is subsequently removed and etched so that the stone when wetted will repel the rolled-on ink in these areas. This technique can create a light image on a dark ground."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "staging-out"@en .

aat:300457803 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The process or activity of preparing beer from barley or other grains. The brewing process can be divided into four steps: mashing, separating, boiling and fermentation. Different approaches to the brewing process lead to variation in beers and beer types."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "brewing"@en .

aat:300457804 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Activity that consists of producing (often) decorative works with various materials. This is often encouraged from an early age to spark creativity in children."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "crafting"@en .

aat:300457805 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The reverence, worship, or allegiance accorded individuals identified as saints, often expressed through ceremonies, prayers, or other religious rituals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hagiolatry"@en .

aat:300457806 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Drawing technique used to render three dimensions in a two-dimensional medium. Depth is represented by rendering all lines in the drawing so that they disappear to one or more points. This term is often used when representing architectural and spatial elements, such as buildings, cityscapes, streets, and other structures. It may also be applied to the rendering of objects in the context of industrial design."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "perspective drawing"@en .

aat:300457808 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Secondary prints pulled from a plate without reinking, made for any purpose. This technique produces a print with radically reduced tonality, as it is pulling only residual ink from the plate."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rétirage prints"@en .

aat:300457809 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Secondary prints pulled from a previously inked and pulled plate, produced intentionally for their lighter tonal quality, especially in monotype, collagraphy, or intaglio, they may also be called ghost prints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cognate prints"@en .

aat:300457810 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Very strong lightweight synthetic fiber made especially by carbonizing acrylic fiber at high temperatures. It contains at least 92% carbon and generally has excellent tensile properties, low densities, high thermal and chemical stabilities in the absence of oxidizing agents, good thermal and electrical conductivities, and excellent creep resistance. Carbon fiber has been extensively used in composite materials."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "carbon fiber"@en .

aat:300457811 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A spontaneous phenomenon where a thin film on a surface ruptures into an ensemble of separated objects, like droplets, stripes, or pillars. This occurs when a liquid film retracts from a solid to form a bead-shaped drop."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dewetting"@en .

aat:300457812 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The preparation of archival materials for use."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "archival processing"@en .

aat:300457813 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Genus of aquatic herbs producing leaves up to 10 feet in width."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Victoria"@en .

aat:300457814 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of giant aquatic herb endemic to Bolivia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Victoria boliviana"@en .

aat:300457815 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Species of giant aquatic herb endemic to shallow waters of the Amazon River basin"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Victoria amazonica"@en .

aat:300457816 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of ancient mudbrick building, often a temple, in the Sudan; possibly the term derives from a Nubian term for a fortified mudbrick building."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "deffufas"@en .

aat:300457817 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Skilled workers who produce wrought iron or steel from pig iron by melting and agitation in a bath, followed by working and forging the metal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "iron puddlers"@en .

aat:300457819 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Design professionals who work with architects and engineers in the creation and development of architectural projects and translating client needs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "architectural designers"@en .

aat:300457821 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Single-family, detached homes with green space. They may be characterized as being located on smaller plots of land as contrasted to the larger plots of traditional single-family homes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "garden homes"@en .

aat:300457822 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Temples, sanctuaries, or shrines dedicated to the Greek goddess, Hera."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "heraions"@en .

aat:300457823 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Self-sufficient, rural built complexes centered on iron production; prevalent in the late 18th and early 19th centuries. They were often located near iron ore deposits and near forests for fuel. The structures served all aspects of ironmaking for the community, from mining to smelting and manufacturing. They typically included dwellings for the community members."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "iron plantations"@en .

aat:300457824 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Managers, overseers, or owners of an ironworking operation, such as an iron foundry, ironworks, or iron plantation, particularly where iron was manufactured on a relatively large scale."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ironmasters"@en .

aat:300457825 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Prints made from a direct-process duplication technique using gelatin. The gelatin process uses a master or transfer paper upon which the image to be duplicated is rendered with a special ink or ribbon. This master is then pressed against a moist gelatinous plate, to which the image is transferred in reverse. Sheets of paper pressed against this ink-imbibed gelatin receive the image. This may be achieved either manually on the plate or flatbed or a rotary machine. Multicolored prints may thus be produced in one operation. Prints pulled in this way usually number under 200."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hectograph copies"@en .

aat:300457826 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In India, structures that were historically used along roads as distance markers. May be associated with communication hubs for relaying messages and shelters for travelers. Typically, kos minars are plain structures with an octagonal base and a tapering cylindrical shaft. They were first introduced by Sher Shah Suri, and later used by the Mughal Empire to mark distances along roads, particularly the Grand Trunk Road during the Mughal era."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kos minars"@en .

aat:300457827 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Commercial business spaces that are located in residences, such as a shop, bakery, or other enterprise. Usually refers to relatively small or medium-sized buildings, where the primary resident is the owner. This is as contrasted to larger structures where there are spaces for businesses at ground level and apartments for renters at an upper level."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "home-based businesses"@en .

aat:300457828 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Spirit duplicator copies, are often referred to as \"ditto\" copies, which was a brand name. Spirit duplication was considered an evolution of the hectographic print process. Invented by Wilhelm Ritzerfeld in 1923, spirit duplication became a common and economical printing method for limited quantities of copies in the context of offices, classrooms, clubs, or churches. Many early fanzines were produced this way. Instead of an ink-transmitting gelatin pad as used in the hectographic process, spirit duplication incorporates a waxy, paste-like ink that is partially dissolved with the application of a hydrocarbon spirit solvent, materials which later were understood to be unsafe."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "spirit duplicator copies"@en .

aat:300457829 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sikh places of worship and community gathering that may include a dining hall, medical facilities, a library, nursery, classroom, meeting rooms, and recreation areas. Gurudwaras are open to people of all faiths and religions.the name; the term literally translates to \"door to the guru\" or \"gateway to the guru,\" in Punjabi. It is the site of a copy of the Adi Granth (“First Volume”), of the sacred scripture of Sikhism."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gurudwaras"@en .

aat:300457831 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Plan with a quadratic naos that is divided by four columns or piers into nine bays, the inner five forming a cross shape, supporting a central dome; common in Byzantine and Eastern Orthodox architectural traditions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cross-in-square plan"@en .

aat:300457832 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Type of flat, thin, red burnt-clay brick; was a popular building material in Mughal architecture. Distinguished by the bricks' distinctive size and shape, which differs from the larger, standard bricks used in later construction."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "lakhori brick"@en .

aat:300457833 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Tile that is coated with a layer of enamel or liquid glass before firing, creating a smooth, protective layer."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "glazed tile"@en .

aat:300457834 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Type of sandstone having a light warm-beige to a rich golden brown hue. Often used for paving, wall cladding, and decorative elements. Prime examples are found in Indian architecture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "buff sandstone"@en .

aat:300457836 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In or near a temple complex, wells or reservoirs, typically below ground level, often a Hindu temple in India. The tanks serve for ritual bathing and purification, and also function as water sources for the community. They may take any of several forms and are known by various specific terms, depending upon their form and the language."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "temple tanks"@en .

aat:300457838 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Wet'suwet'en, formerly known as the Broman Lake Indian Band, whose traditional territories are in present-day central British Columbia. The Wet'suwet'en First Nation is a member of the Carrier Sekani Tribal Council."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wet'suwet'en"@en .

aat:300457839 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Pavlions or other light structures, tradtionally having twelve doors and designed to allow for ample airflow. Typical uses include as a location for small concerts or performances, or as a refuge in hot weather. Prominent examples are found in South Asia, particularly in India and Pakistan."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "baradaris"@en .

aat:300457840 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In India, a townhouse, mansion, or manor houses, typically relatively large with courtyards and intricate decorations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "havelis"@en .

aat:300458676 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Dynasty of an Afghan family that ruled in northern India from 1540 to 1556."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sūr"@en .

aat:300458677 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In India, the Muslim style of construction that was based on arches, vaults, and domes, on columns, and with pyramidal towers or slender spires."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Indo-Islamic"@en .

aat:300458679 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A device consisting of a wooden beam worn as shackles placed around the neck to restrain enslaved people."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "wooden collars"@en .

aat:300458680 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of black face paint composed of carbon-black. Literally, \"face covered in lamp-black.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "ixtlilmaca"@en .

aat:300458685 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Complexes of built works surrounding a cathedral."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cathedral complexes"@en .

aat:300458686 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Visual works that are incorporated into another work. The original work may be incorporated in whole, or in part. Examples are older buildings that have been incorporated into a larger, newer building."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "incorporated works"@en .

aat:300458687 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Visual works that are repurposed for use other than the original use. Examples are warehouses that are converted into dwellings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "repurposed works"@en .

aat:300458691 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In India and other locations, mansions or palaces, or other buildings or sets of buildings used by certain organizations devoted to a particular activity, group, or concern."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "bhavans"@en .

aat:300458692 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "General term for a sacred tanks or pools of water linked to spiritual practices and cleansing rituals for devotees, in Hinduism and other religions in south Asia."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sacred tanks"@en .

aat:300458693 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Sikh temples or shrines."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "gurdwaras"@en .

aat:300458694 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Sikhism, sacred tanks or pools, located at or near a gurdwara (temple)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sarovars"@en .

aat:300458695 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Musicians and composers who work in service to the monarch or other court."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "court musicians"@en .

aat:300458700 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Sinixt, an indigenous First Nations people in eastern Washington state and British Columbia. The Sinixt primarily live on the Colville Indian Reservation and are part of the Confederated Tribes of the Colville Reservation. However, the Sinixt state that they were driven to the Colville Reservation from their indigenous lands in British Columbia. In 2017, the government of Canada officially recognized the tribe."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sinixt"@en .

aat:300458701 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Plans that depict defensive installations of a town or fortress, while the interior of the town is often left as generic built space. They were used as building plans as well as for the management, inspection, maintenance, and defense of towns. The topography around the fortress or fortified town may also be represented."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "fortification plans"@en .

aat:300458702 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to a certain type of king or prince, particularly in India, but also a Malay or Javanese ruler or chief. Used for the rulers of the principal native states in India; later extended to a petty chief or dignitary, or a member of the Hindu nobility in India. Designates a rank below that of a \"maharaja,\" which means \"great king.\""@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "rajas"@en .

aat:300458703 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Mughal and other architecture, cupola-like structures typically found crowning buildings, such as gateways, pavilions, or towers; characterized by dome-shaped roofs supported by pillars."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chhatris"@en .

aat:300458704 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings, halls, or chambers in which judicial assemblies or courts of law are conducted."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "courts"@en .

aat:300458705 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In India, from Tamil, a reservoir, tank, lake or other body formed by damming or otherwise confining water."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "tals"@en .

aat:300458706 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Method of construction where stones are laid or stacked without any mortar to bind them together; stones are typically secured by having carefully selecting interlocking stones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dry stone technique"@en .

aat:300458707 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Style and culture of the Chalon, or members of the Chalon Indian Nation, an indigenous people whose ancestral lands are located in San Benito County, California, including Pinnacles National Park."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chalon"@en .

aat:300458710 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Style and culture of the Stó:lō, an indigenous Coast Salish people whose ancestral lands are located in what is now the Fraser Valley of British Columbia, Canada."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stó:lō"@en .

aat:300458711 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Inscriptions threatening harm to anyone who would steal or damage a book or manuscript."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "book curses"@en .

aat:300458714 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Culture and style of a widely distributed South Asian social and ethnic group living mainly in the area now comprising north-western India and Pakistan."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jat"@en .

aat:300458715 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In Muslim cultures, tombs that are revered as a shrine, or other buildings of religious pilgrimage; may be associated with Sufi saints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "mazars"@en .

aat:300458716 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A type of coarse limestone found in many parts of India; found in large tabular strata or interspersed throughout the surface soil. It is used in construction, burned to lime, and as a binding to form a compact, hard, and even surface."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "kankar"@en .

aat:300458717 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gateways or doors; in the context of Mughal architecture, refers to grand entrances to significant buildings or complexes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "darwazas"@en .

aat:300458719 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Paintings or other art works that serve as a reminder of the inevitability of death, usually due to the depicted subject."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "memento mori"@en .

aat:300458720 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The adoption of the practices or aethetics of one social group or society by another, more dominant social group or society, in ways that are exploitative, offensive, and/or disrespectful. These customs or aethetics may include cultural traditions, clothing, and general behavior. The term is most commonly used to refer to the adoption of non-Western aesthetics by people in Western cultures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cultural appropriation"@en .

aat:300458721 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The return of cultural property, such as looted artwork or historic artifacts, to its country or culture of origin. From the late 20th century into the 21st, the issue of and debates surrounding repatriation has grown as countries assess their laws related to cultural property, and their histories related to colonialism."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "repatriation"@en .

aat:300458722 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Hybrid objects that use photographs as the primary element in a sculptural object."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "photo-sculptures"@en .

aat:300458723 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rooms, suites of rooms, other enclosed, domestic interior spaces having the primary purpose of sleeping and relaxing."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "sleeping chambers"@en .

aat:300458724 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Rooms, suites of rooms, or other interior spaces, usually distinguished by being set aside for a particular purpose and occupied by a particular person."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "chambers"@en .

aat:300458725 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Coins or medals without any inscriptions (legends)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "anepigraphics"@en .

aat:300458726 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Currency that does not have the name of the issuing authority in its inscriptions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "anonymous coins"@en .

aat:300458727 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Coins made of metals with little value, such as copper, iron, lead, or tin, covered with a thin layer of precious metal, that imitate pieces of gold or silver."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "clad coins"@en .

aat:300458728 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Coins that have undergone the intentional alteration of some of their formal elements, for example, in their inscriptions or motifs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "defaced coins"@en .

aat:300458729 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Continuous or broken lines made of dots, zigzags, or other similar motifs that run parallel to a coin's edge. These lines delimits the coin's field and frames both the obverse and reverse designs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "dentils"@en .

aat:300458730 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Value assigned to currency by the issuing authority, which all users accept, is engraved on one side of a coin or on paper money. Sometimes called nominal value."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "face value"@en .

aat:300458731 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Coins or medals in which each of their sides corresponds to different issues. It can be attributed to minting misstrikes or forgeries."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "hybrids"@en .

aat:300458732 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The metal interior of a coin. Especially in clad coins, the core is usually made of a lower-value metal (copper or bronze) covered by a higher-value metal (gold or silver)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "cores"@en .

aat:300458733 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Coins that consist exclusively of inscriptions (legends)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "legend coins"@en .

aat:300458734 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Authority that creates and puts into circulation, according to its instructions and in an official manner, the currency and paper money of legal tender in some territory. Depending on the political context, the issuer may be an individual (such as a king, an ecclesiastical leader, or a military commander) or a geopolitical entity (such as a republic, a state, or a city-state)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "money issuers"@en .

aat:300458735 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the island territory of Guam. For a time the term was synonymous with the indigenous Chamorro people of Guam, however, in recent decades, the term's meaning has expanded to be more ethnically ambiguous, and refers to any resident of Guam, with \"Chamorro\" being distinct."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Guamanian"@en .

aat:300458736 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Refers to the style and culture of the Chamorro, an indigenous people of Guam and the Northern Mariana Islands."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chamorro"@en .

aat:300458737 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A post-World War I conservative cultural movement in France and elsewhere in Europe, defined by a resurgence of classicism, nationalism, and a rejection of the avant-garde. Considered to be a reaction to the shock of WWI. The term is thought to derive from the book of essays by the artist and poet Jean Cocteau, 'Le rappel a l’ordre,' published in 1926."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "return to order"@en .

aat:300458738 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Banknotes with additional official stamps placed after issuance, by which the authority legitimized them, authorized their circulation, extended their maturity, etc."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "overstamped banknotes"@en .

aat:300458739 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Coins with one or more holes. This characteristic is a post-minting alteration that does not respond to an official typology of a central hole. These perforations may respond to a process of demonetization (withdrawal from circulation) by the State or may be of a ritual, transforming the piece into an object of adornment or amulet."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "perforated coins"@en .

aat:300458740 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Coins that are impossible to identify and hardly recognizable due to wear or poor preservation, except by their shape."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "poorly preserved coins"@en .

aat:300458741 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In the age of forgeries, banknotes were submitted to banks for authentication using stamps (either dry or ink), which indicated their legitimacy. This method was commonly used during the 19th century."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "stamped banknotes"@en .

aat:300458742 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Banknotes that were withdrawn from circulation using various methods: circular drilling, cutting with scissors, and stamping."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "withdrawn banknotes"@en .

aat:300458744 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Public toilets built alongside Roman baths; often consisting of a single room with stone or wooden benches pierced with seats."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "foricae"@en .

aat:300458745 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Gardens used for growing food, located in a heavily populated place like a town or a city. May be a communal gardens."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "urban gardens"@en .

aat:300458746 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "Buildings housing exhibits, offices, laboratories, or other facilities having to do with science."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:aat ;
    skos:prefLabel "science centers"@en .

mimo:2205 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Parts dels instruments musicals"@ca,
        "Bestandteile von Musikinstrumenten"@de,
        "Elements of musical instruments"@en,
        "Partes de los instrumentos musicales"@es,
        "Eléments d'instruments de musique"@fr,
        "Componenti di strumenti musicali"@it,
        "Delen van muziekinstrumenten"@nl,
        "Części instrumentów muzycznych"@pl,
        "Delar av musikinstrument"@sv,
        "乐器组成部分"@zh .

mimo:2206 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In music, a bow /boʊ/ is a tensioned stick which has hair (usually horse-tail hair) coated in rosin (to facilitate friction) affixed to it. It is moved across some part (generally some type of strings) of a musical instrument to cause vibration, which the instrument emits as sound. The vast majority of bows are used with string instruments, such as the violin, viola, cello, and bass, although some bows are used with musical saws and other bowed idiophones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arquets"@ca,
        "Bögen"@de,
        "Bows"@en,
        "Arcos"@es,
        "Archets"@fr,
        "Archetti"@it,
        "Boog"@nl,
        "Smyczki"@pl,
        "Stråkar"@sv,
        "弓"@zh .

mimo:2207 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "In music, a bow /boʊ/ is a tensioned stick which has hair (usually horse-tail hair) coated in rosin (to facilitate friction) affixed to it. It is moved across some part (generally some type of strings) of a musical instrument to cause vibration, which the instrument emits as sound. The vast majority of bows are used with string instruments, such as the violin, viola, cello, and bass, although some bows are used with musical saws and other bowed idiophones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arquet"@ca,
        "Bogen"@de,
        "Bow"@en,
        "Arco"@es,
        "Archet"@fr,
        "Archetto"@it,
        "Boog"@nl,
        "Smyczek"@pl,
        "Stråke"@sv,
        "弓"@zh .

mimo:2208 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "musical instrument that produces its sounds using electronics"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Instruments electrònics"@ca,
        "Elektronische Musikinstrumente"@de,
        "Electronic instruments"@en,
        "Instrumentos electrónicos"@es,
        "Instruments électroniques"@fr,
        "Strumenti elettronici"@it,
        "Elektronische instrumenten"@nl,
        "Elektrofony"@pl,
        "Elektroniska instrument"@sv,
        "电子乐器"@zh .

mimo:2209 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Electrònics"@ca,
        "Elektronische Musikinstrumente"@de,
        "Electronic"@en,
        "Electrónicos"@es,
        "Electroniques"@fr,
        "Strumenti elettronici"@it,
        "Elektronische instrumenten"@nl,
        "Instrumenty elektroniczne"@pl,
        "Elektronisk"@sv,
        "电子乐器"@zh .

mimo:2210 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Caixa de ritmes"@ca,
        "Beatbox"@de,
        "Beatbox"@en,
        "Caja de ritmos"@es,
        "Boite à rythmes"@fr,
        "Beatbox"@it,
        "Beatbox"@nl,
        "Beatbox"@pl,
        "Beatbox"@sv,
        "电子节奏鼓"@zh .

mimo:2211 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The clavioline is an electronic keyboard instrument, a forerunner to the analog synthesizer. It was invented by French engineer Constant Martin in 1947 in Versailles. The instrument consists of a keyboard and a separate amplifier and speaker unit. The keyboard usually covered three octaves, and had a number of switches to alter the tone of the sound produced, add vibrato (a defining feature of the instrument), and provide other effects. The Clavioline used a vacuum tube oscillator to produce a buzzy waveform, almost a square wave, which could then be altered using high-pass and low-pass filtering, as well as the vibrato. The amplifier also aided in creating the instrument's signature tones, by deliberately providing a large amount of distortion."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Clavioline"@ca,
        "Clavioline"@de,
        "Clavioline"@en,
        "Clavioline"@es,
        "Clavioline"@fr,
        "Clavioline"@it,
        "Clavioline"@nl,
        "Clavioline"@pl,
        "Clavioline"@sv,
        "键盘式电子小提琴"@zh .

mimo:2212 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gmebaphone"@ca,
        "Gmebaphone"@de,
        "Gmebaphone"@en,
        "Gmebaphone"@es,
        "Gmebaphone"@fr,
        "Gmebaphone"@it,
        "Gmebaphone"@nl,
        "Gmebaphone"@pl,
        "Gmebaphone"@sv,
        "Gmebaphone"@zh .

mimo:2213 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gmebogosse"@ca,
        "Gmebogosse"@de,
        "Gmebogosse"@en,
        "Gmebogosse"@es,
        "Gmebogosse"@fr,
        "Gmebogosse"@it,
        "Gmebogosse"@nl,
        "Gmebogosse"@pl,
        "Gmebogosse"@sv,
        "Gmebogosse"@zh .

mimo:2214 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Heliophon"@ca,
        "Heliophon"@de,
        "Heliophon"@en,
        "Heliophon"@es,
        "Heliophon"@fr,
        "Heliophon"@it,
        "Heliophon"@nl,
        "Heliophon"@pl,
        "Heliophon"@sv,
        "Heliophon"@zh .

mimo:2215 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Mellotron is an electro-mechanical musical instrument developed in Birmingham, England, in 1963. It is played by pressing its keys, each of which pushes a length of magnetic tape against a capstan, which pulls it across a playback head. As the key is released, the tape is retracted by a spring to its initial position. Different portions of the tape can be played to access different sounds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mellotron"@ca,
        "Mellotron"@de,
        "Mellotron"@en,
        "Mellotron"@es,
        "Mellotron"@fr,
        "Mellotron"@it,
        "Mellotron"@nl,
        "Melotron"@pl,
        "Mellotron"@sv,
        "美乐特朗琴"@zh .

mimo:2216 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "MIDI (/ˈmɪdi/; Musical Instrument Digital Interface) is a technical standard that describes a communications protocol, digital interface, and electrical connectors that connect a wide variety of electronic musical instruments, computers, and related audio devices for playing, editing, and recording music. The specification originates in the paper Universal Synthesizer Interface published by Dave Smith and Chet Wood of Sequential Circuits at the 1981 Audio Engineering Society conference in New York City."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Instrument MIDI"@ca,
        "MIDI-Instrument"@de,
        "MIDI instrument"@en,
        "Instrumento MIDI"@es,
        "Instrument MIDI"@fr,
        "Strumento MIDI"@it,
        "MIDI-instrument"@nl,
        "Instrument z systemem MIDI"@pl,
        "Midiinstrument"@sv,
        "MIDI乐器"@zh .

mimo:2217 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The ondes Martenot (/ˈoʊnd mɑːrtəˈnoʊ/ OHND mar-tə-NOH; French: [ɔ̃d maʁtəno], \"Martenot waves\") or ondes musicales (\"musical waves\") is an early electronic musical instrument. It is played with a keyboard or by moving a ring along a wire, creating \"wavering\" sounds similar to a theremin. A player of the ondes Martenot is called an ondist. The ondes Martenot was invented in 1928 by the French inventor Maurice Martenot. Martenot was inspired by the accidental overlaps of tones between military radio oscillators, and wanted to create an instrument with the expressiveness of the cello."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ones Martenot"@ca,
        "Ondes Martenot"@de,
        "Ondes Martenot"@en,
        "Ondas Martenot"@es,
        "Ondes Martenot"@fr,
        "Onde Martenot"@it,
        "Ondes Martenot"@nl,
        "Fale Martenota"@pl,
        "Ondes Martenot"@sv,
        "马特诺琴"@zh .

mimo:2219 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The Ondioline is an electronic keyboard musical instrument, developed and built by Frenchman Georges Jenny. Sometimes referred to as the \"Jenny Ondioline,\" the instrument is considered a forerunner of the synthesizer. First conceived by Jenny in 1939, he continued refining and reconfiguring the device, producing dozens of variant models up until his death in 1975."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ondioline"@ca,
        "Ondioline"@de,
        "Ondioline"@en,
        "Ondioline"@es,
        "Ondioline"@fr,
        "Ondioline"@it,
