mimo:2281 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "E-Piano"@de,
        "Elektropiano"@de ;
    skos:definition "An electric piano is a musical instrument which produces sounds when a performer presses the keys of a piano-style musical keyboard. Pressing keys causes mechanical hammers to strike metal strings, metal reeds or wire tines, leading to vibrations which are converted into electrical signals by magnetic pickups, which are then connected to an instrument amplifier and loudspeaker to make a sound loud enough for the performer and audience to hear. Unlike a synthesizer, the electric piano is not an electronic instrument. Instead, it is an electro-mechanical instrument. Some early electric pianos used lengths of wire to produce the tone, like a traditional piano. Smaller electric pianos used short slivers of steel to produce the tone (a lamellophone with a keyboard & pickups). The earliest elect"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Piano elèctric"@ca,
        "Elektrisches Klavier"@de,
        "Electric piano"@en,
        "Piano eléctrico"@es,
        "Piano électrique"@fr,
        "Pianoforte elettrico"@it,
        "Elektrische piano"@nl,
        "Fortepian elektryczny"@pl,
        "Elpiano"@sv,
        "电子钢琴"@zh .

mimo:2282 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A euphonicon is a variety of upright piano. The distinguishing feature of the euphonicon is that the iron harp frame projects from the body on the left, such that the bass strings are open to view. It also has unusual stringing and tuning arrangements, and an early example of drop-action. Rather than a single soundboard, it has three soundbases which imitate the appearance of the cello (behind the bass strings), viola (behind the tenor strings) and violin (behind the treble strings)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Euphonicon"@ca,
        "Euphonikon"@de,
        "Euphonicon"@en,
        "Euphonicon"@es,
        "Euphonicon"@fr,
        "Euphonicon"@it,
        "Euphonicon"@nl,
        "Euphonicon"@pl,
        "Euphonicon"@sv,
        "键盘式竖琴（一种harp-piano）"@zh .

