        "二胡"@zh .

mimo:3139 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The erxian (二弦; pinyin: èrxián; literally \"two string\") is a Chinese bowed string instrument in the huqin family of instruments. It has two strings and is used primarily in Cantonese music, most often in \"hard string\" chamber ensembles. In the 1920s, following the development of the gaohu, the erxian experienced a decline and since the late 20th century has been little used outside the tradition of Cantonese opera. The erxian of earlier times came in two forms: one for playing bongjee/bangzi (梆子), and a slightly larger one for playing yiwong/erhuang (二黃)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Erxian"@ca,
        "Erxian"@de,
        "Erxian"@en,
        "Erxian"@es,
        "Erxian"@fr,
        "Erxian"@it,
        "Erxian"@nl,
        "Erxian"@pl,
        "Erxian"@sv,
        "二弦"@zh .

mimo:3140 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Dra che"@zh,
        "Esrar"@zh ;
    skos:definition "The esraj (from the Punjabi: ਇਸਰਾਜ) is an Indian stringed instrument found in two forms throughout the Indian subcontinent. It is a relatively recent instrument, being only about 300 years old. It is found in North India, primarily Punjab, where it is used in Sikh music and Hindustani classical compositions and in West Bengal. The esraj is a modern variant of the dilruba, differing slightly in structure."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Esraj"@ca,
        "Esraj"@de,
        "Esraj"@en,
        "Esraj"@es,
        "Esraj"@fr,
        "Esraj"@it,
        "Esraj"@nl,
        "Esraj"@pl,
        "Esraj"@sv,
        "埃史拉吉"@zh .

mimo:3142 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A fiddle is a bowed string musical instrument, most often a violin. It is a colloquial term for the violin, used by players in all genres, including classical music. Although in many cases violins and fiddles are essentially synonymous, the style of the music played may determine specific construction differences between fiddles and classical violins. For example, fiddles may optionally be set up with a bridge with a flatter arch to reduce the range of bow-arm motion needed for techniques such as the double shuffle, a form of bariolage involving rapid alternation between pairs of adjacent strings. To produce a \"brighter\" tone than the deep tones of gut or synthetic core strings, fiddlers often use steel strings. The fiddle is part of many traditional (folk) styles, which are typically aura"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fídula"@ca,
        "Fidel"@de,
        "Fiddle"@en,
        "Fídula"@es,
        "Vièle"@fr,
        "Fidula"@it,
        "Vedel"@nl,
        "Chordofon szyjkowy, smyczkowy"@pl,
        "Fiddla"@sv,
        "Fiddle小提琴"@zh .

mimo:3143 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Endingedi"@zh,
        "Goudoulka"@zh ;
    skos:definition "The gadulka (Bulgarian: гъдулка) is a traditional Bulgarian bowed string instrument. Alternate spellings are \"gǎdulka\", \"gudulka\" and \"g'dulka\". Its name comes from a root meaning \"to make noise, hum or buzz\". The gadulka is an integral part of Bulgarian traditional instrumental ensembles, commonly played in the context of dance music. There is a smaller variant of the instrument in the Dobrudja region with no sympathetic strings at all."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gadulka"@ca,
        "Gadulka"@de,
        "Gadulka"@en,
        "Gadulka"@es,
        "Gadulka"@fr,
        "Gadulka"@it,
        "Gadulka"@nl,
        "Gydułka"@pl,
        "Gadulka"@sv,
        "加杜卡[保加利亚]"@zh .

