mimo:3151 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Gusla"@zh,
        "Nanhu"@zh ;
    skos:definition "The gusle (Serbian Cyrillic: гусле) or lahuta (Albanian: lahutë) is a single-stringed musical instrument (and musical style) traditionally used in the Dinarides region of Southeastern Europe (in the Balkans). The instrument is always accompanied by singing; musical folklore, specifically epic poetry. The gusle player holds the instrument vertically between his knees, with the left hand fingers on the strings. The strings are never pressed to the neck, giving a harmonic and unique sound."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gusla"@ca,
        "Gusle"@de,
        "Gusle"@en,
        "Gusla"@es,
        "Gusle"@fr,
        "Gusla"@it,
        "Gusle"@nl,
        "Gusle"@pl,
        "Gusle"@sv,
        "Gusle"@zh .

mimo:3154 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Hardingfele"@zh,
        "Zhonghu"@zh ;
    skos:definition "A Hardanger fiddle (Norwegian: hardingfele) is a traditional stringed instrument considered to be a national instrument of Norway. In modern designs, this type of fiddle is very similar to the violin, though with eight or nine strings (rather than four as on a standard violin) and thinner wood. Four of the strings are strung and played like a violin, while the rest, named understrings or sympathetic strings, resonate under the influence of the other four."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fídula de Harding"@ca,
        "Hardanger Fidel"@de,
        "Hardanger fiddle"@en,
        "Fídula de Harding"@es,
        "Violon de Hardanger"@fr,
        "Violino dell'Hardanger"@it,
        "Hardangervedel"@nl,
        "Hardanger fiddle"@pl,
        "Hardangerfela"@sv,
        "奚琴"@zh .

mimo:3156 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Esrar"@zh,
        "Gidzhak"@zh,
        "Gijak"@zh,
        "Goudoulka"@zh,
        "Gougue"@zh,
        "Hu kin"@zh,
        "Hu-Tjin"@zh,
        "Huchin"@zh,
        "Ti-kin"@zh,
        "Tiqin"@zh ;
    skos:definition "Huqin (Chinese: 胡琴; pinyin: húqin) is a family of bowed string instruments, more specifically, a spike fiddle popularly used in Chinese music. The instruments consist of a round, hexagonal, or octagonal sound box at the bottom with a neck attached that protrudes upwards. They also usually have two strings, and their soundboxes are typically covered with either snakeskin (most often python) or thin wood. Huqin instruments usually have two tuning pegs, one peg for each string. The pegs are attached horizontally through holes drilled in the instrument's neck. Most huqin have the bow hair pass in between the strings. Exceptions to having two strings and pegs include variations of huqin with three, four, and sometimes even more than five. These include the zhuihu, a three stringed huqin, the si"@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Huqin"@ca,
        "Huqin"@de,
        "Huqin"@en,
        "Huqin"@es,
        "Huqin"@fr,
        "Huqin"@it,
        "Huqin"@nl,
        "Huqin"@pl,
        "Huqin"@sv,
        "胡琴"@zh .

mimo:3158 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Amza"@zh,
        "Amzad"@zh,
        "Anzad"@zh,
        "Gusla"@zh,
        "Guslitza"@zh,
        "Hardingfele"@zh,
        "Hu kin"@zh,
        "Inzad"@zh ;
    skos:definition "The imzad (alternately amzad) is a single-string bowed instrument used by the Tuareg people in Africa. Its body is made out of a calabash or wood which is covered by animal skin. The strings are made from horse hair and are connected near the neck, and runs over a two-part bridge. The round bow is also equipped with horse hair. The imzad is only played by the women for example to accompany songs, often during an evening ceremony called . However, there are modern attempts to promote the instrument as inherent to Tuareg culture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Imzad"@ca,
        "Imzad"@de,
        "Imzad"@en,
        "Imzad"@es,
        "Imzad"@fr,
        "Imzad"@it,
        "Imzad"@nl,
        "Imzad"@pl,
        "Imzad"@sv,
        "单弦提琴"@zh .

