mimo:3332 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The charango is a small Andean stringed instrument of the lute family, from the Quechua and Aymara populations in the territory of the Altiplano in post-Colonial times, after European stringed instruments were introduced by the Spanish during colonialization. The instrument is widespread throughout the Andean regions of Bolivia, Peru, Ecuador, northern Chile and northwestern Argentina, where it is a popular musical instrument that exists in many variant forms. The charango was primarily played in traditional Andean music. A charango player is called a charanguista."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Charango"@ca,
        "Charango"@de,
        "Charango"@en,
        "Charango"@es,
        "Charango"@fr,
        "Charango"@it,
        "Charango"@nl,
        "Charango"@pl,
        "Charango"@sv,
        "恰兰戈"@zh .

