mimo:3365 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The gittern was a relatively small gut-strung, round-backed instrument that first appears in literature and pictorial representation during the 13th century in Western Europe (Iberian Peninsula, Italy, France, England). It is usually depicted played with a quill plectrum, as can be seen clearly beginning in manuscript illuminations from the thirteenth century. It was also called the guiterna in Spain, guiterne or guiterre in France, the chitarra in Italy and Quintern in Germany. A popular instrument with court musicians, minstrels, and amateurs, the gittern is considered an ancestor of the modern guitar and other instruments like the mandore, bandurria and gallichon."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gittern"@ca,
        "Gittern"@de,
        "Gittern"@en,
        "Gittern"@es,
        "Guiterne"@fr,
