mimo:3828 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The alto clarinet is a woodwind instrument of the clarinet family. It is a transposing instrument pitched in the key of E♭, though instruments in F have been made. In size it lies between the soprano clarinet and the bass clarinet. It bears a greater resemblance to the bass clarinet in that it typically has a straight body (made of grenadilla or other wood, hard rubber, or plastic), but a curved neck and bell made of metal. All-metal alto clarinets also exist. In appearance it strongly resembles the basset horn, but usually differs in three respects: it is pitched a whole step lower, it lacks an extended lower range, and it has a wider bore than many basset horns."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Clarinet contralt"@ca,
        "Altklarinette"@de,
        "Alto clarinet"@en,
        "Clarinete contralto"@es,
        "Clarinette alto"@fr,
        "Clarinetto contralto"@it,
        "Altklarinet"@nl,
        "Klarnet altowy"@pl,
        "Altklarinett"@sv,
        "中音单簧管"@zh .

mimo:3829 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Arghoul"@zh,
        "Arghul el-kebyr"@zh,
        "Arghul el-soghayr"@zh,
        "Argul"@zh,
        "Contrabassoon"@zh,
        "Curtal"@zh,
        "Kortholt"@zh,
        "Sordone"@zh ;
    skos:definition "The arghul (Arabic: أرغول or يرغول), also spelled argul, arghoul, arghool, argol, or yarghul, is a musical instrument in the reed family. It has been used since ancient Egyptian times and is still used as a traditional instrument in Egypt, Syria, Jordan, and Palestine."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arghul"@ca,
        "Arghul"@de,
        "Arghul"@en,
        "Arghul"@es,
        "Arghul"@fr,
        "Arghul"@it,
        "Arghul"@nl,
        "Arghul"@pl,
        "Arghul"@sv,
        "Arghul"@zh .

mimo:3832 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:altLabel "Baßklarinette"@de ;
    skos:definition "The bass clarinet is a musical instrument of the clarinet family. Like the more common soprano B♭ clarinet, it is usually pitched in B♭ (meaning it is a transposing instrument on which a written C sounds as B♭), but it plays notes an octave below the soprano B♭ clarinet. Bass clarinets in other keys, notably C and A, also exist, but are very rare (in contrast to the regular A clarinet, which is quite common in classical music). Bass clarinets regularly perform in orchestras, wind ensembles and concert bands, and occasionally in marching bands, and play an occasional solo role in contemporary music and jazz in particular."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Clarinet baix"@ca,
        "Bassklarinette"@de,
        "Bass clarinet"@en,
        "Clarinete bajo"@es,
        "Clarinette basse"@fr,
        "Clarinetto basso"@it,
        "Basklarinet"@nl,
        "Klarnet basowy"@pl,
        "Basklarinett"@sv,
        "低音单簧管"@zh .

mimo:3833 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The basset clarinet is member of the clarinet family similar to the usual soprano clarinet but longer and with additional keys to enable playing several additional lower notes. Typically a basset clarinet has keywork going to a low (written) C or B, as opposed to the standard clarinet's E or E♭. The basset clarinet is most commonly a transposing instrument in A, although basset clarinets in C and B♭ and very seldom in G also exist. The similarly named basset horn is also a clarinet with extended lower range, but is in a lower pitch (typically F); the basset horn predates, and undoubtedly inspired, the basset clarinet."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bassettklarinette"@de,
        "Basset clarinet"@en,
        "Clarinette de basset"@fr,
        "Clarinetto di bassetto"@it,
        "Bassetklarinet"@nl,
        "Klarnet basetowy"@pl,
        "Bastklarinet"@sv,
        "巴塞单簧管"@zh .

mimo:3834 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The basset horn (sometimes hyphenated as basset-horn) is a member of the clarinet family of musical instruments."@en ;
