mimo:4184 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The chalumeau (English: /ˈʃæləmoʊ/; French: [ʃa.ly.mo]; plural chalumeaux) is a single-reed woodwind instrument of the late baroque and early classical eras. The chalumeau is a folk instrument that is the predecessor to the modern-day clarinet. It has a cylindrical bore with eight tone holes (seven in front and one in back for the thumb) and a broad mouthpiece with a single heteroglot reed (i.e. separate, not a continuous part of the instrument's body) made of cane. Similar to the clarinet, the chalumeau overblows a twelfth."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chalumeau"@ca,
        "Chalumeau"@de,
        "Chalumeau"@en,
