mimo:4937 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "The igil (Tuvan: игил) is a two-stringed Tuvan musical instrument, played by bowing the strings. (It is called \"ikili\" in Western Mongolia.) The neck and lute-shaped sound box are usually made of a solid piece of pine or larch. The top of the sound box may be covered with skin or a thin wooden plate. The strings, and those of the bow, are traditionally made of hair from a horse's tail (strung parallel), but may also be made of nylon. Like the morin khuur of Mongolia, the igil typically features a carved horse's head at the top of the neck above the tuning pegs, and both instruments are known as the horsehead fiddle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme lev:mimo ;
    skos:prefLabel "Igil"@ca,
        "Igil"@de,
        "Igil"@en,
        "Igil"@es,
        "Igil"@fr,
        "Igil"@it,
        "Igil"@nl,
        "Igil"@pl,
        "Igil"@sv,
